window.MAPLE_SEARCH=[{"p":"Licence","h":"Licence","u":"license.html","x":"Licence Copyright (c) 2026 Kinn Coelho Juliao <kinncj@gmail.com>. All rights reserved. MAPLE HARNESS — BINARY LICENCE (FREE TO USE, CLOSED SOURCE) 1. Use of the binaries. Permission is granted, free of charge, to any person or organisation to download, install and run the compiled Maple Harness programs (maple-harness,","m":"both"},{"p":"Agents that call agents (A2A)","h":"Agents that call agents (A2A)","u":"a2a.html","x":"Other AI agents can hand Maple Harness a coding task and read its answer, and the proxy can put several agents behind one address with access rules, their own credentials and a record of every call, all over the Agent2Agent protocol (A2A) , versions 1.0 and 0.3 . Off until you start it Both parts are off by default . N","m":"single"},{"p":"Agents that call agents (A2A)","h":"In plain words","u":"a2a.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. A2A is a common language agents use to hand each other tasks. With it, an orchestrator or another agent can ask Maple Harness to do a coding task in one of your projects, and read the answer. The harness as an agent. `maple-harness a2a serve` runs a session in a project folder and takes tasks from other age","m":"single"},{"p":"Agents that call agents (A2A)","h":"The harness as an agent","u":"a2a.html#the-harness-as-an-agent","x":"","m":"single"},{"p":"Agents that call agents (A2A)","h":"Start it","u":"a2a.html#start-it","x":"Make a token for the caller. `maple-proxy token add orchestrator --scope a2a` prints it once; give it to the agent that will send tasks. Existing tokens never gain this scope. Start the agent in the project folder. `cd` into the project and run `maple-harness a2a serve`. Point the caller at the agent card , `http://127","m":"single"},{"p":"Agents that call agents (A2A)","h":"What a task does","u":"a2a.html#what-a-task-does","x":"Tasks run one at a time, in order , as turns of the same session, so a later task sees the conversation of the earlier ones. Up to 16 tasks wait behind the running one. With `--continue` or `--session`, the conversation's earlier history is shared with callers too, and the agent says so when it starts. A task's answer ","m":"single"},{"p":"Agents that call agents (A2A)","h":"Who may approve what","u":"a2a.html#who-may-approve-what","x":"In plain words. The calling agent can always say no. Saying yes is yours, except for what you have already allowed in your rules, or what you trust a caller with by granting its token. The question An ordinary caller A caller whose token you granted A call your allow rules already cover (it asks only because something ","m":"single"},{"p":"Agents that call agents (A2A)","h":"The proxy as a gateway","u":"a2a.html#the-proxy-as-a-gateway","x":"List the agents under `a2a_gateway.agents` (below), each with its address, a description and the header that carries its own credential, and set `enabled: true`. Say who may call each one with `people` and `teams`; both left out means anyone with an a2a token. Give each caller an a2a token from the proxy : `maple-proxy","m":"single"},{"p":"Agents that call agents (A2A)","h":"Find an agent","u":"a2a.html#find-an-agent","x":"Shell The answer lists the agents the caller may reach, best match first, with each one's address, card address, tags and skills (from its card). With the built-in embedding model installed, matching is by meaning (`\"method\": \"meaning\"`); without it, by shared words (`\"method\": \"words\"`). Without `query` it lists them ","m":"single"},{"p":"Agents that call agents (A2A)","h":"On the setup page","u":"a2a.html#on-the-setup-page","x":"The Hubs & chaining tab has an A2A gateway card once `a2a_gateway` is set up. It lists each agent with its description, who may call it (people and teams, or anyone with an a2a token), its address (scheme and host only), where callers reach it on this proxy, and when its card was last read. Check reads an agent's card ","m":"single"},{"p":"Agents that call agents (A2A)","h":"Technical details","u":"a2a.html#technical-details","x":"Versions. The JSON-RPC binding of A2A 1.0 (`SendMessage`, `SendStreamingMessage`, `GetTask`, `ListTasks`, `CancelTask`, `SubscribeToTask`) and 0.3 (`message/send`, `message/stream`, `tasks/get`, `tasks/cancel`, `tasks/resubscribe`). The `A2A-Version` header (or query parameter) picks the version; without it the method'","m":"single"},{"p":"Agents that call agents (A2A)","h":"Troubleshooting","u":"a2a.html#troubleshooting","x":"You see Why, and what to do `a2a serve` says no access token has the a2a scope Make one: `maple-proxy token add <label> --scope a2a` 401 from the agent or the gateway The token is not an a2a token, or was revoked. A proxy or remote-control token does not work here The task stays in input-required It waits for an answer","m":"single"},{"p":"Agents that call agents (A2A)","h":"What has been checked","u":"a2a.html#what-has-been-checked","x":"The agent and the gateway were run against the A2A project's own conformance suites and samples (2026-11-21): its Technology Compatibility Kit for 0.3 and for 1.0 (JSON-RPC), its Python SDK's clients for both versions, and two of its sample agents behind the gateway. What the suites check passes, except: Checks that ne","m":"single"},{"p":"Agents that call agents (A2A)","h":"See also","u":"a2a.html#see-also","x":"Questions the agent asks you How a question reaches a calling agent, and how it answers. Access control Address lists and access hours, which the gateway applies too. MCP hub Find the gateway's agents from an MCP client. Background agents What happens to agents the session starts while it serves a task.","m":"single"},{"p":"Access control: organizations, roles, keys and end users","h":"Access control: organizations, roles, keys and end users","u":"access-control.html","x":"A shared proxy can do more than count who used what. This page covers the controls added in 3.0: organizations above teams, roles for the people who manage the proxy, rules per access token, budgets per end user of your own application, budgets over several windows at once, temporary increases, model access groups, hid","m":"teams"},{"p":"Access control: organizations, roles, keys and end users","h":"In plain words","u":"access-control.html#in-plain-words","x":"Organizations group teams. An organization can cap what all its teams spend together and narrow the models they may use. A team belongs to at most one organization. Roles say what a person's own token may do through the management API: `admin`, `viewer`, `org_admin`, `team_admin` and `member`. An `org_admin` looks afte","m":"teams"},{"p":"Access control: organizations, roles, keys and end users","h":"Who this is for","u":"access-control.html#who-this-is-for","x":"Read on if one proxy serves several teams or departments, if you hand tokens to programs that should have their own cap, if you build an application whose customers each need a spending limit, or if you are moving from LiteLLM and want its management calls to keep working. For one person, or a small team that only need","m":"teams"},{"p":"Access control: organizations, roles, keys and end users","h":"Organizations","u":"access-control.html#organizations","x":"YAML Requests from people in `web` and `data` count against `acme`'s budget as well as their own and their team's. A model must be allowed by the organization's list too. An organization's budget can be in AI units and in estimated dollars, like a team's. When it is spent, the refusal names the organization without sho","m":"teams"},{"p":"Access control: organizations, roles, keys and end users","h":"Roles","u":"access-control.html#roles","x":"YAML With `management_api: on`, a person's own token (the `proxy` scope) reaches the management API as far as their role does: Role Organizations, model aliases, spend People (`/user`), teams (`/team`) and keys (`/key`) `admin` changes organizations and model aliases; reads everything everything, as a manage token `vie","m":"teams"},{"p":"Access control: organizations, roles, keys and end users","h":"Key rules","u":"access-control.html#key-rules","x":"YAML A key's budget can be in AI units (`daily_ai_units`, `monthly_ai_units`), in estimated dollars (daily, monthly, windows) or both, with a temporary increase of either. Its spend is kept in the proxy's spend store (the spend file on one machine, the shared store with several replicas), so a restart keeps it; it is n","m":"teams"},{"p":"Access control: organizations, roles, keys and end users","h":"A caller on this machine without a token","u":"access-control.html#a-caller-on-this-machine-without-a-token","x":"On an address that only listens on this machine, a program may leave its token out. Once `keys:` has rules, that would leave the key's rules behind, so such a request is refused (`401`, saying what to do) unless you say otherwise: YAML With an entry for token `local`, such a request is served and held to that entry (bu","m":"teams"},{"p":"Access control: organizations, roles, keys and end users","h":"A gateway that names people","u":"access-control.html#a-gateway-that-names-people","x":"In plain words. A gateway in front of the proxy, such as a LiteLLM gateway, sends every request with its one token, so the proxy would count all of them as the gateway's. If the gateway's own callers are your people, you can let its token say, for each request, which person it is for, out of a list you give: that perso","m":"teams"},{"p":"Access control: organizations, roles, keys and end users","h":"End-user budgets","u":"access-control.html#end-user-budgets","x":"YAML The end user is read from the header when one is set and present (only that header, when one is set), else from LiteLLM's `x-litellm-end-user-id` or `x-litellm-customer-id` header, otherwise from OpenAI's `user` field or Anthropic's `metadata.user_id`. An id of letters, digits and `. _ @ : -` up to 64 characters i","m":"teams"},{"p":"Access control: organizations, roles, keys and end users","h":"Budget windows and temporary increases","u":"access-control.html#budget-windows-and-temporary-increases","x":"Any budget (a person's, team's, organization's, key's or end user's) can add rolling dollar windows and a temporary increase: YAML A window counts the last `days` UTC days, today included, and moves on at 00:00 UTC; windows of 1 to 62 days are accepted. A temporary increase adds its amounts to the limits of the same ki","m":"teams"},{"p":"Access control: organizations, roles, keys and end users","h":"Lifetime budgets","u":"access-control.html#lifetime-budgets","x":"In plain words. A budget that never resets: once a person, team, organization, token or end user has spent it, they stop until you raise it. LiteLLM's budgets without a `budget_duration` come across as one. YAML The technical view. It counts everything spent since the spend store first counted it: the days the store st","m":"teams"},{"p":"Access control: organizations, roles, keys and end users","h":"Model access groups and hidden models","u":"access-control.html#model-access-groups-and-hidden-models","x":"YAML A group's models are added to the model list of whoever is granted it (`access_groups` on a person, team, organization or key). Someone granted only groups may use only their models. A hidden group's models are left out of `/v1/models` and `/model/info` for everyone; anyone allowed them can still ask for them by n","m":"teams"},{"p":"Access control: organizations, roles, keys and end users","h":"Access hours","u":"access-control.html#access-hours","x":"YAML Outside every window the team's people are refused with `403 outside_access_hours`, before anything is counted. The time zone is an IANA name the machine knows; without one, UTC. Calls through the A2A gateway are held to the same hours.","m":"teams"},{"p":"Access control: organizations, roles, keys and end users","h":"Address allow lists","u":"access-control.html#address-allow-lists","x":"`allowed_ips` on a key, a team or an organization lists addresses and ranges (`10.0.0.0/8`, `2001:db8::/32`, `192.0.2.7`). A request from anywhere else is refused with `403` and `ip_not_allowed`. Behind a load balancer, set `server.trusted_proxies` so the proxy sees the client's own address. On a request a proxy below ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Access control: organizations, roles, keys and end users","h":"The management API","u":"access-control.html#the-management-api","x":"The proxy's OpenAI-compatible address answers LiteLLM-shaped management calls (see LiteLLM's management API for `/key`, `/user`, `/team`, `/model/info` and `/health`). 3.0 adds organizations, model aliases and spend: Call What it does here Who `POST /organization/new`, `/organization/update`, `/organization/delete` org","m":"teams"},{"p":"Access control: organizations, roles, keys and end users","h":"The settings","u":"access-control.html#the-settings","x":"Key What it is `organizations[]` `id`, `budget`, `models.allow`, `access_groups`, `allowed_ips` `teams[].organization`, `teams[].access_groups`, `teams[].allowed_ips`, `teams[].access_hours[]` a team's organization, groups, addresses and hours (`days`, `from`, `until`, `timezone`) `people[].role`, `people[].access_grou","m":"teams"},{"p":"Access control: organizations, roles, keys and end users","h":"What is not covered, and what is not verified","u":"access-control.html#what-is-not-covered-and-what-is-not-verified","x":"In a chain, a hub hands down its people, teams and organizations, with their access groups turned into the models in them, and the `keys:` rules of the tokens it hands down; a proxy below never widens them. A local organization of the same id may only narrow one (budget, models, addresses); anything else is reported as","m":"teams"},{"p":"Access control: organizations, roles, keys and end users","h":"Related","u":"access-control.html#related","x":"Teams People, teams, access tokens, budgets and rate limits on one proxy. Share Maple with a team Hubs, people and fallbacks, from one shared proxy to a hub of hubs. Monthly dollar budgets A monthly allowance for an Anthropic API account, and what happens when it is spent. Guardrails Block, mask or log secrets, persona","m":"teams"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Alerts and forecasts","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html","x":"The proxy can warn you before a budget runs out, and estimate when it will at the rate you have been using it. Both read the usage log on your own disk. Nothing here changes a request: an alert only warns, and a forecast is an estimate. If you have not set a budget, there is nothing to do and nothing changes: no alerts","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"In plain words","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#in-plain-words","x":"An alert is a warning: \"this budget is 80% used\", \"a quarter of the last hour's requests failed\". A forecast is the proxy's estimate of when a budget runs out at the rate you have been using it. Neither stops anything; a budget is what stops spending. On the setup page they live in two places: Costs & budgets (in the W","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"What fires by default","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#what-fires-by-default","x":"For each budget you set (`budgets:` in `config.yaml`, or on the setup page), the proxy warns twice a day at most: once when that provider has used 80% of the day's budget, and once at 100%. Days are UTC days, the same days the budget counts. A monthly dollar budget warns the same way at 80% and 100% of its month, with ","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Set the rules on the setup page","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#set-the-rules-on-the-setup-page","x":"Monitoring , step 7 Alerts , then Alert rules . Each field is one key; an empty field turns it off: Field Key Budget warnings (%) `budget_fractions`, as percentages: `80, 100`. Empty turns budget alerts off Quota warnings (%) `quota_fractions` Daily AI units `daily_spend_ai_units` Daily cost (USD, est.) `daily_spend_co","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Other rules","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#other-rules","x":"All off unless you set them, on the page or in `config.yaml`: YAML A provider's quota is what the provider says about your account (for example a monthly allowance of premium requests). Each level fires once per reset period. With `quota_fractions` set, the proxy asks the provider about the account once a minute, the s","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"When a batch's usage cannot be written","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#when-a-batchs-usage-cannot-be-written","x":"This one is always on and has no setting. It only concerns proxies that share a usage store (several replicas, `storage.mode: central`). In plain words: if the shared usage store keeps refusing a batch's usage for 10 minutes, you are told. Nothing is lost meanwhile: the proxy keeps the usage and tries again every 30 se","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Where you see them","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#where-you-see-them","x":"In Maple Harness: one warning line in the conversation, the first time an alert comes back from the proxy. It is shown once, never added to what the model is sent, and only appears when an alert has fired. On your phone or desktop: if you have notifications set up, an alert is sent once, with the project's name. Leave ","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Who gets which alert","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#who-gets-which-alert","x":"On your own machine, without a token, you get every alert, as before. With people set up, a person using their own token gets only the alerts about their own budget (at the same levels, `budget_fractions`, for their daily and monthly limits), with their own figures and nobody else's; never a provider's budget, quota, s","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Send alerts to Slack, Teams or a webhook","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#send-alerts-to-slack-teams-or-a-webhook","x":"Off unless you add one. A webhook is told of each alert as it fires (not when it ends), once. It is sent the alert's text, level, rule, id and time, and nothing else: never your requests, prompts or usage rows. Who gets what follows the same rules as the header: a webhook for the admin (the default) gets every alert; a","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Slack","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#slack","x":"Create an incoming webhook for the channel (an address starting `https://hooks.slack.com/services/`) and add it with `--format slack`. The message is one line, for example: Maple warning: openai has used 80% of today's budget (80 of 100 requests); it resets at 00:00 UTC (budget.80)","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Microsoft Teams","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#microsoft-teams","x":"In the channel, add a Workflows webhook (\"Post to a channel when a webhook request is received\") and add the address it gives you with `--format teams`. Maple sends an Adaptive Card with the same line, and the alert's id and time beneath it.","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Any other endpoint (JSON)","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#any-other-endpoint-json","x":"With `--format json` (the default) the body is: JSON A test message has `\"rule\": \"test\"` and `\"test\": true`.","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"In config.yaml","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#in-configyaml","x":"The page and the CLI write this for you; the address and the secret stay in the secret store. `url` is always a reference: an address written here is refused. YAML","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Check the signature","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#check-the-signature","x":"With a signing secret, each request carries two headers: `X-Maple-Timestamp` (Unix seconds) and `X-Maple-Signature`, which is `v1=` and the hex HMAC-SHA256, keyed with your secret, of the timestamp, a dot, and the raw body. Check it on the raw body before parsing, and refuse an old timestamp so a request cannot be repl","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Where it may be sent","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#where-it-may-be-sent","x":"https only. Plain http only to this machine, and only with `allow_http_loopback: true` (with `allow_private: true`), for a local relay. Not to your own network (this machine, private ranges) unless the webhook says `allow_private: true`. Link-local and cloud-metadata addresses are refused whatever it says. The check is","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Forecasts","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#forecasts","x":"A forecast takes the average of what you spent on each of the last seven complete UTC days, with the 25th and 75th percentile days as the range, and sets it against what is left. Today does not count until it is over. With fewer than three days of usage there is no forecast at all. Shell It answers in words such as: At","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"When you stop using it","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#when-you-stop-using-it","x":"Going back to a release before alerts: the `alerts:` block is ignored, and the alert lines already in the events log stay readable.","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Related","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#related","x":"Proxy settings: Alerts Every alert key, its default and where it is sent. Monthly dollar budgets A monthly allowance for an Anthropic API account, and what happens when it is spent. See and filter your usage The usage log on your own disk: filters, views and Grafana. Your usage data The usage log's columns, exports and","m":"both"},{"p":"Approvals when nobody answers","h":"Approvals when nobody answers","u":"approvals.html","x":"The agent asks you before it runs a tool call your rules do not settle. Sometimes nobody is there to answer: you stepped away, the run is a script, or it serves another agent. In 3.0 a question like that never holds a run forever and never approves anything by itself. That one call is denied, the refusal is recorded wi","m":"single"},{"p":"Approvals when nobody answers","h":"In plain words","u":"approvals.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. A limit on how long an approval waits, and a clear answer for runs where nobody can be asked. Unanswered means no. A question nobody answers in 10 minutes (`harness.approvals.timeout`) is denied. A run where nobody can be asked at all denies it at once. Nothing is allowed or remembered either way. A piped \"","m":"single"},{"p":"Approvals when nobody answers","h":"Who can answer, run by run","u":"approvals.html#who-can-answer-run-by-run","x":"Where it runs Who can answer When nobody answers or can The full-screen terminal You, in the terminal; a phone or browser while the session is shared Denied after `harness.approvals.timeout` `--prompt` or `--repl` at a terminal You, at the `allow …?` line Denied after the timeout `--prompt` with no terminal on standard","m":"single"},{"p":"Approvals when nobody answers","h":"How long a question waits","u":"approvals.html#how-long-a-question-waits","x":"`harness.approvals.timeout` is `10m` by default. It takes `off`, `1m`, `2m`, `5m`, `10m`, `15m` or `30m`. When it runs out, that one call is denied and recorded as `answered_by: timeout`. The model is told and the run goes on, so it can try another way or say what it could not do. `off` waits as long as it takes, as be","m":"single"},{"p":"Approvals when nobody answers","h":"Runs with no terminal","u":"approvals.html#runs-with-no-terminal","x":"When standard input is a pipe, a file or closed (a CI job, `echo … | maple-harness --prompt …`), nothing on it is read as an answer. Each question is denied at once as `answered_by: no_person`, and the reason names what would allow it, for example `--allow-tool 'bash(git push origin main)'` or an allow rule in `harness","m":"single"},{"p":"Approvals when nobody answers","h":"Rules for one run","u":"approvals.html#rules-for-one-run","x":"Four flags work with `--prompt`, `--repl`, `--rpc` and the full-screen interface (not with `serve` or `a2a serve`): Flag What it does `--allow-tool RULE` Allow calls matching the rule without asking, for this run. Repeatable `--deny-tool RULE` Deny calls matching the rule, for this run. Repeatable `--policy FILE` Read ","m":"single"},{"p":"Approvals when nobody answers","h":"How a rule is written","u":"approvals.html#how-a-rule-is-written","x":"Maple's own form, as in `harness.permission.rules` , works as it is: `bash(git status*)`, `read_file`, `mcp__github__create_issue`. The forms Claude Code and Copilot CLI use are read too: You write It means `Bash(…)`, `shell(…)` `bash` `Read`, `Edit`, `MultiEdit`, `Write`, `Grep` `read_file`, `edit_file`, `edit_file`, ","m":"single"},{"p":"Approvals when nobody answers","h":"Which wins","u":"approvals.html#which-wins","x":"A deny rule for this run always wins. A deny rule in your config always wins, wherever it sits in your list: no run allow gets past it. An ask rule for this run makes the call ask. An allow rule for this run lets through what your config asks about or does not mention.","m":"single"},{"p":"Approvals when nobody answers","h":"A policy file","u":"approvals.html#a-policy-file","x":"YAML Shell JSON with the same three lists works too. An unknown key is an error, so a typo does not go unnoticed. Rules from `--allow-tool` and `--deny-tool` are added to the file's.","m":"single"},{"p":"Approvals when nobody answers","h":"Seeing what was refused","u":"approvals.html#seeing-what-was-refused","x":"Every call denied because nobody answered (or nobody could) shows up in five places: A `--prompt` run with no terminal to ask: refused at once, said on the error stream, and the model told. A demo setup with a scripted model. The error stream , in `--prompt` and `--repl`, as it happens: `--json-summary` , in a `refusal","m":"single"},{"p":"Approvals when nobody answers","h":"Exit status 4","u":"approvals.html#exit-status-4","x":"A `--prompt` run that finished but refused a call because nobody answered exits with 0 , as before, so scripts that read the status keep working. To tell such a run apart, set `harness.approvals.refusal_exit_code: on`, or pass `--refusal-exit-code` for one run: it then exits with 4 . Status Means 0 The run finished 1 A","m":"single"},{"p":"Approvals when nobody answers","h":"Answer from your phone","u":"approvals.html#answer-from-your-phone","x":"A `--prompt` run with no terminal on standard input, and `maple-harness a2a serve`, show up on your phone when both hold: this machine's gateway is already running (`maple-harness gateway start`, or `/remote` in a session): such a run never starts one; a device that may drive sessions is paired . The run then appears i","m":"single"},{"p":"Approvals when nobody answers","h":"Sessions with nobody at the terminal","u":"approvals.html#sessions-with-nobody-at-the-terminal","x":"A session started with `maple-harness serve` (through the gateway or on its own port), or a remote host whose terminal is gone, asks only while someone can answer: a phone or browser connected, or a paired device that may drive sessions (pushed a notification when the gateway's notifications are on). With neither, a qu","m":"single"},{"p":"Approvals when nobody answers","h":"Work another agent sent","u":"approvals.html#work-another-agent-sent","x":"A turn started by another agent's message, and a session that `agent_start` opened in a pane, ask you about their tool calls: the agent that sent the work never answers them, and neither does the session's control socket. Past the timeout they are denied, and the session's reply tells the sending agent that you did not","m":"single"},{"p":"Approvals when nobody answers","h":"Waiting for a heavy-run slot","u":"approvals.html#waiting-for-a-heavy-run-slot","x":"When `limits.heavy_runs` holds back a test suite or a build because another one runs, the wait is bounded too. Past it the command is not run and the model is told why. Setting Default Where `harness.limits.heavy_wait` `30m` A session you use `harness.limits.heavy_wait_headless` `10m` Runs nobody watches: `--prompt`, `","m":"single"},{"p":"Approvals when nobody answers","h":"Settings","u":"approvals.html#settings","x":"Setting Default What it does `harness.approvals.timeout` `10m` How long a question about a tool call waits before that call is denied. `off` waits as long as it takes. Not settable from a project's config `harness.approvals.refusal_exit_code` `off` `on`: a `--prompt` run that refused a call nobody answered exits with 4","m":"single"},{"p":"Approvals when nobody answers","h":"Technical details","u":"approvals.html#technical-details","x":"Never an allow. A timeout or a missing person always ends in a deny for that one call. \"Always\" is never recorded from it, and the next call asks again. The pause. The countdown stops while a phone or browser that may answer has the session open (a device that drives sessions, or a share that drives its own), and resum","m":"single"},{"p":"Approvals when nobody answers","h":"Troubleshooting","u":"approvals.html#troubleshooting","x":"You see Why What to do `■ refused … [answered_by: no_person]` in a CI job Nobody can be asked in a run with no terminal Add the rule it names with `--allow-tool` or `--policy`, or an allow rule in your config `■ refused … [answered_by: timeout]` Nobody answered within `harness.approvals.timeout` Answer sooner, raise th","m":"single"},{"p":"Approvals when nobody answers","h":"What has been checked","u":"approvals.html#what-has-been-checked","x":"Checked on Linux with automated tests: the plain prompt with and without a terminal (a piped answer never approves), the timeout in the full-screen terminal and in a shared session, denial at once with nobody to reach, `--json-summary` and exit status 4, the rules for one run, an approvals-only session, and A2A end to ","m":"single"},{"p":"Approvals when nobody answers","h":"See also","u":"approvals.html#see-also","x":"Questions the agent asks you The model's own multiple-choice questions, and their timeout. Agents that call agents (A2A) What a calling agent may approve, and how to grant it more. Control it from your phone Pair a phone that answers approvals. Harness settings Every setting, permission rules included.","m":"single"},{"p":"Background agents: out on the ice while you talk","h":"Background agents: out on the ice while you talk","u":"background-agents.html","x":"Since 3.0 an agent the model starts in the background keeps working after the turn ends. You can go on talking to the main agent, or leave it idle, and each background agent's report comes back later on its own: it lands in the conversation and wakes the main agent, which reads it and carries on. A finished agent can b","m":"single"},{"p":"Background agents: out on the ice while you talk","h":"In plain words","u":"background-agents.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Helpers that keep working while you and the main agent move on. When one is done, its report arrives by itself and the main agent reads it. Why you want it. A long search, a test run or a second opinion no longer holds the conversation up. You keep the main agent free for the next thing. What to do. Nothing","m":"single"},{"p":"Background agents: out on the ice while you talk","h":"What you see","u":"background-agents.html#what-you-see","x":"When a turn ends with agents still working, one line says so, and the line under the input box keeps saying it while the session is idle: When an agent finishes and nothing else is running, its report appears as one line and the main agent starts a turn to read it: While a turn is already running, the report reaches th","m":"single"},{"p":"Background agents: out on the ice while you talk","h":"Talking to an agent","u":"background-agents.html#talking-to-an-agent","x":"Open an agent from the tray (Enter on its row) to see its transcript. Press m to write to it: an agent that is still running reads your message at its next step; a finished agent is resumed : it starts again in the background with its whole conversation, and your message at the end. Its new report comes back like the f","m":"single"},{"p":"Background agents: out on the ice while you talk","h":"Stopping agents","u":"background-agents.html#stopping-agents","x":"Where What it does `x` on an agent in the tray, then `y` Stops that agent `X` in the tray, then `y` Stops every agent still running The palette (`ctrl+p`): Stop all agents The same Esc Stops the main agent's turn, and an agent it waits for in the foreground; background agents keep working (with `outlive_turn: off`, Esc","m":"single"},{"p":"Background agents: out on the ice while you talk","h":"When you quit with agents out","u":"background-agents.html#when-you-quit-with-agents-out","x":"In plain words: What it is. You want to close the terminal, but an agent is halfway through a long job. You can let it finish instead of stopping it. Why you want it. An hour of work does not have to be thrown away because it is time to go home. What to do. The steps below. Press Ctrl-C once. The line under the input b","m":"single"},{"p":"Background agents: out on the ice while you talk","h":"After a restart","u":"background-agents.html#after-a-restart","x":"The finished agents' conversations (the newest 20, each up to 2 MB of text) are saved beside the session, in a file readable by you only, whenever they change. Start the session again with `--session <name>` and they are back under their old ids, marked as from before the restart. Ask the main agent to follow up (\"ask ","m":"single"},{"p":"Background agents: out on the ice while you talk","h":"Settings","u":"background-agents.html#settings","x":"Setting Default Meaning `harness.subagents.outlive_turn` `on` `on`: background agents keep working after the turn, and their reports wake the session. `off`: the turn waits for every background agent before it ends, as before 3.0 `harness.subagents.keep_after_exit` `offer` `offer`: quitting with agents still working of","m":"single"},{"p":"Background agents: out on the ice while you talk","h":"Technical details","u":"background-agents.html#technical-details","x":"","m":"single"},{"p":"Background agents: out on the ice while you talk","h":"The tools the model uses","u":"background-agents.html#the-tools-the-model-uses","x":"The same four tools that reach other agents on your machine reach this session's own agents, by their id (`a3`), by `name#id` (`tester#a3`), or by a name only one agent has: Tool On one of this session's agents `agent_read` Its state, its transcript so far, and its report once finished. On a background command or monit","m":"single"},{"p":"Background agents: out on the ice while you talk","h":"How a report reaches the model","u":"background-agents.html#how-a-report-reaches-the-model","x":"A report is added at the end of the conversation (so the provider's prompt cache keeps working), opened by a note saying it is the report of work the agent started, not your words. Several reports that arrive while one turn runs are read at its next step.","m":"single"},{"p":"Background agents: out on the ice while you talk","h":"Safety","u":"background-agents.html#safety","x":"A background agent has the same permissions as before: every tool call that would ask you still asks, in the terminal or on your phone, labelled with the agent's name, even after the turn that started it has ended. The tools that act outside the session (typing into a pane or another agent, starting one, a monitor) ask","m":"single"},{"p":"Background agents: out on the ice while you talk","h":"See also","u":"background-agents.html#see-also","x":"Subagents The helpers themselves, the lineup they share, and how to write your own. Background commands and monitors Long commands and log watches that tell the agent when they end. Memory caps and heavy runs What keeps a room full of agents from eating your RAM. Questions the agent asks you How an agent asks when it n","m":"single"},{"p":"Background commands and monitors","h":"Background commands and monitors","u":"background-commands.html","x":"The agent can start a long command (a test suite, a build, a server) in the background and keep working while it runs. When that command ends, the agent is told: its exit code, how long it ran and the last lines it printed, so it never has to keep checking. It can also watch a command's output, such as a log or a CI ru","m":"single"},{"p":"Background commands and monitors","h":"In plain words","u":"background-commands.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Commands that run while the agent does something else. When one ends, a line appears in the conversation and the agent reads the result. If the agent was idle, it wakes up and carries on. Why you want it. A ten-minute test run no longer holds up the work, and nothing waits on the agent polling for a result:","m":"single"},{"p":"Background commands and monitors","h":"When a background command ends","u":"background-commands.html#when-a-background-command-ends","x":"In the conversation, one line: When nothing else is going on, the agent starts a turn to read it, and the line says so: While a turn is running, the result reaches the model at its next step instead; nothing is interrupted. When the terminal is not in front, a desktop notification says which command finished. Not every","m":"single"},{"p":"Background commands and monitors","h":"What happens: wake, next or off","u":"background-commands.html#what-happens-wake-next-or-off","x":"`harness.job_notices` decides: Value What it does `wake` (default) The agent is told at once: during a turn at its next step, and when idle a turn starts for it `next` The agent is told at its next step, or with your next message. It never starts a turn by itself `off` Nothing is said. The agent can still read a comman","m":"single"},{"p":"Background commands and monitors","h":"Watching a command: monitors","u":"background-commands.html#watching-a-command-monitors","x":"A monitor runs a command and tells the agent about its output line by line: `tail -f` on a log, a CI run that prints as it goes, a test watcher. The agent can ask to hear every line, or only lines that match a pattern (for example `FAIL|panic`), and to stop at a line that says the work is over (for example `^ok|DONE`).","m":"single"},{"p":"Background commands and monitors","h":"A monitor asks first","u":"background-commands.html#a-monitor-asks-first","x":"Important A monitor keeps running and feeds what it reads to the model unasked, so it always asks before it starts , in every mode, `auto` and `yolo` included, unless a permission rule allows it. Rules match the command, like rules for `bash`. To let the agent tail logs without asking each time: YAML A command your rul","m":"single"},{"p":"Background commands and monitors","h":"Seeing and stopping them","u":"background-commands.html#seeing-and-stopping-them","x":"In the agents tray (`Ctrl+T`, or `↓` from an empty message box), after the subagents, there is a row for each command or monitor that is running, or that finished in the last 20 seconds: `$` is a command, `~` a monitor; the mark's colour says whether it is running, waiting for a heavy-run slot, passed, or failed or was","m":"single"},{"p":"Background commands and monitors","h":"Its whole output, in a file","u":"background-commands.html#its-whole-output-in-a-file","x":"Every background command and monitor also writes everything it prints to a file of its own, so a long test run can be looked back through later. The file is kept small enough to be harmless: past 16 MB the oldest half is dropped, and the file says so at its top. The files are in a private temporary folder (only you can","m":"single"},{"p":"Background commands and monitors","h":"A command that runs past its timeout","u":"background-commands.html#a-command-that-runs-past-its-timeout","x":"A command the agent runs in the foreground is stopped when it reaches its timeout (2 minutes unless the agent asks for more). With `harness.bash.background_on_timeout: on`, it moves to the background instead: it keeps running as `bg_N`, with what it printed so far and everything after, the same caps and heavy-run slot,","m":"single"},{"p":"Background commands and monitors","h":"Technical details","u":"background-commands.html#technical-details","x":"The tools. `bash` with `run_in_background: true` starts a command and answers at once with its id (`bg_1`, `bg_2`, …). `bash_output` reads what it printed since the last read, and `kill_shell` (or `agent_stop`) stops it and everything it started. `agent_read` reads its last lines and `agent_wait` waits for it to end. `","m":"single"},{"p":"Background commands and monitors","h":"See also","u":"background-commands.html#see-also","x":"Background agents Helpers that keep working after the turn, and report back on their own. Memory caps and heavy runs The caps every command runs under, and why test runs take turns. Sandbox What a command the agent runs can and cannot reach. Harness settings Every `harness.*` setting, including `job_notices`.","m":"single"},{"p":"Amazon Bedrock","h":"Amazon Bedrock","u":"bedrock.html","x":"Use the models in your company's AWS account through Amazon Bedrock: Claude, Amazon Nova, Llama, Mistral and the others Bedrock offers. Requests are signed with your own AWS credentials and billed to your AWS account under your AWS agreement. Maple stores no AWS key: on a laptop it uses your AWS profile or SSO sign-in,","m":"both"},{"p":"Amazon Bedrock","h":"In plain words","u":"bedrock.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Claude, Amazon Nova, Llama and the other models in your company's AWS account, through Amazon Bedrock, billed to that account. Why you want it. No new key: Maple signs each request with the AWS sign-in you already have (a profile, SSO, or the pod's role on Kubernetes). What to do. Sign in to AWS as usual, t","m":"both"},{"p":"Amazon Bedrock","h":"On a laptop","u":"bedrock.html#on-a-laptop","x":"Sign in to AWS the way you already do, then add Bedrock to the proxy: Shell `bedrock models` lists the text models and inference profiles the region offers your account, and which Bedrock API the proxy uses for each. `bedrock add` checks the models you name against that list and writes the provider into `config.yaml` (","m":"both"},{"p":"Amazon Bedrock","h":"Where the credentials come from","u":"bedrock.html#where-the-credentials-come-from","x":"The proxy uses the AWS SDK's own credential chain, in its usual order, and the SDK refreshes temporary credentials before they expire: Environment variables: `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`, `AWS_SESSION_TOKEN`, or `AWS_PROFILE`. The shared files `~/.aws/config` and `~/.aws/credentials`: a profile (`bedroc","m":"both"},{"p":"Amazon Bedrock","h":"Permissions","u":"bedrock.html#permissions","x":"The identity needs `bedrock:InvokeModel` and `bedrock:InvokeModelWithResponseStream` on the models (and on the inference profiles you use), plus `bedrock:ListFoundationModels` and `bedrock:ListInferenceProfiles` for `bedrock models` and `bedrock add`.","m":"both"},{"p":"Amazon Bedrock","h":"Model access","u":"bedrock.html#model-access","x":"A model must be available to your AWS account in the region before it answers. Some model providers ask for a one-time use-case form or an AWS Marketplace subscription first; open the Amazon Bedrock console in that region to check. Until then, requests fail as not authorised , and the message names the model, the regio","m":"both"},{"p":"Amazon Bedrock","h":"Regions and inference profiles","u":"bedrock.html#regions-and-inference-profiles","x":"One provider is one region with one set of credentials: an account in Maple's sense. Several regions are several providers: Shell This writes `bedrock-us-east-1` and `bedrock-us-west-2` and chains them with `provider_fallbacks` : a throttled region (HTTP 429), a spent service quota or an outage moves the request to the","m":"both"},{"p":"Amazon Bedrock","h":"Which Bedrock API","u":"bedrock.html#which-bedrock-api","x":"Claude Opus 4.7 and later go through Bedrock's Messages API endpoint (`bedrock-mantle`), which speaks Anthropic's Messages format; every other model, earlier Claude models included, goes through Bedrock's Converse API. Both stream, take tools and images, and use prompt caching where the model supports it (cache points ","m":"both"},{"p":"Amazon Bedrock","h":"On Kubernetes (EKS)","u":"bedrock.html#on-kubernetes-eks","x":"The Kubernetes samples include a component for IAM roles for service accounts in `config/cloud-identity/eks/`. Add it to your overlay: YAML Create an IAM role whose trust policy allows the cluster's OIDC provider for `system:serviceaccount:maple:maple-proxy`, with the Bedrock permissions above. Put the role's ARN in `c","m":"both"},{"p":"Amazon Bedrock","h":"Bedrock's own API through the proxy","u":"bedrock.html#bedrocks-own-api-through-the-proxy","x":"A tool that speaks Bedrock's runtime API (InvokeModel, Converse, their streams) can use it through the proxy with a pass-through route : the proxy checks the caller, signs the request with this provider's AWS credentials, and records the tokens the answer reports. The tool needs no AWS credentials of its own. Off until","m":"both"},{"p":"Amazon Bedrock","h":"Cost and usage","u":"bedrock.html#cost-and-usage","x":"Bedrock does not state a price on its replies, so the proxy records the tokens each call used (input, cached input and output) and no price; your AWS bill is the authority. For Claude models on the Messages API, `estimate_cost: true` on the provider adds an estimate from Anthropic's list prices, labelled as an estimate","m":"both"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"Measure with bench","u":"bench.html","x":"`maple-harness bench` answers one question on your own work: does a setting change what a task costs, and does the result stay as good? It replays the same tasks under different settings (\"arms\"), several times each, in fresh directories, and compares the arms task by task with the uncertainty shown. It is an advanced ","m":"single"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"In plain words","u":"bench.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. A command that runs the same tasks several times with and without a setting, and compares what they cost and whether the result stayed as good. What to do. Try it with `--dry-run` first (it spends nothing), then set a budget and run it for real. There is no setup page step.","m":"single"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"Try it without spending","u":"bench.html#try-it-without-spending","x":"Shell With no `--yes` the command prints the plan (how many runs, in what arms) and an estimate of the cost from earlier runs of the same suite, or says the cost is unknown. Nothing is run and nothing is spent.","m":"single"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"Run it for real","u":"bench.html#run-it-for-real","x":"Shell `--k 3` runs every task three times under every arm. Three is the least that says anything about variation. `--budget-ai-units` (or `--budget-tokens`) is required with `--yes`. The bench stops before the run that would pass it, judged by the average cost so far. One run that costs far more than the average can st","m":"single"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"Write your own tasks","u":"bench.html#write-your-own-tasks","x":"A suite is a folder: `task.yaml`: YAML Instead of `files/` a task can name a repository and a commit to check out (`repo:` and `commit:`). Source files that a build would otherwise pick up as part of its own project can carry a `.tmpl` suffix, which is removed on copy. A task without `verify` has no check, and its qual","m":"single"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"Compare another tool on the same model","u":"bench.html#compare-another-tool-on-the-same-model","x":"An arm can be another program instead of a change to this one. It gets the same tasks, in the same fresh directories, with the same check, so the comparison is like for like. The example below runs GitHub Copilot CLI against a model you host yourself, next to Maple Harness on the same model. YAML No shell. The command ","m":"single"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"Counting on the model server","u":"bench.html#counting-on-the-model-server","x":"Measured in AI units or the tool's own tokens, two programs cannot be compared, because one may report nothing. If your model server is vLLM, point the bench at its metrics and it reads the server's own counters around every run: Shell Before each run it checks that the server is idle (nothing running or waiting). If i","m":"single"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"A check for a person, too","u":"bench.html#a-check-for-a-person-too","x":"Tests can pass on something that does not work. A task may add `verify_browser: check.py` (a script in the task folder; `.js` runs with node, `.py` with python, `.sh` with sh). It runs after the tests, gets the working directory and the machine's numeric network address in `MAPLE_BENCH_LAN_IP`, and is typically a Playw","m":"single"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"Read the results","u":"bench.html#read-the-results","x":"Shell The report shows, for each arm, the median with a 95% interval; then each arm against the baseline, task by task: the change (negative means it used less), its interval, a p-value, and how many tasks it won, lost and tied; then the pass rate, so that a cheaper arm that fails more is visible; then the total each a","m":"single"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"What is recorded","u":"bench.html#what-is-recorded","x":"Each run records tokens, cache reads and writes, AI units, requests, retries, tool calls, wall time, files changed, and whether the check passed. No prompt text, file contents or credentials are recorded, and paths are removed from error messages unless you pass `--anonymize=false`. Each Maple Harness run also keeps a ","m":"single"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"See also","u":"bench.html#see-also","x":"What /cost tells you The session's own bill, request by request, and what to do about each flag. Check it yourself What Maple aims at, and how to measure it on your own work. Settings by goal The settings that serve efficiency, efficacy and effectiveness. See and reduce what a task costs Why long tasks cost what they d","m":"single"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Chain proxies into hubs","u":"chaining.html","x":"One Maple proxy can use another as a provider: the one it uses is its hub . A laptop can go to a team hub, the team hub to a company hub, and every tool on the laptop (Maple Harness, Claude Code, OpenCode) gets the hub's models through the proxy it already talks to. This page covers joining a hub (with your company acc","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"In plain words","u":"chaining.html#in-plain-words","x":"A hub is just another Maple proxy that other proxies use, usually one on a team machine. There are two ways to connect a proxy to a hub, and which one you use depends on who the proxy serves: Your own proxy, on your laptop: sign in with your company account, or paste your personal token. Sign in yourself, in a browser,","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Join your laptop to a team hub","u":"chaining.html#join-your-laptop-to-a-team-hub","x":"You need the hub's sign-in address (the address you open to sign in to the hub, such as `https://maple-admin.example.com`; ask the hub's admin) and your company account. Then, on your own proxy: Open your setup page, Hubs & chaining , Connect this proxy to a parent hub (until your proxy uses a hub, the tab is under Set","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Join with a personal token","u":"chaining.html#join-with-a-personal-token","x":"Without single sign-on, the hub's admin makes you a token on the hub ( People & teams , your Access and tokens , Make a token ) and sends it to you with the hub's address; How they connect lists this first. On your own proxy: Open your setup page, Hubs & chaining , Connect this proxy to a parent hub , under Or paste a ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Connect a team proxy with a chain token","u":"chaining.html#connect-a-team-proxy-with-a-chain-token","x":"A proxy that serves a team connects with a chain token. It takes two people and two minutes: The hub's admin makes a chain token for your proxy, on the hub's setup page: Hubs & chaining , Let a proxy connect to this hub , type a name for your proxy, Make a chain token . The token is shown once. You connect your proxy ,","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"On the hub: let a proxy connect","u":"chaining.html#on-the-hub-let-a-proxy-connect","x":"Making a chain token for design-laptop on the team hub. In this demo every proxy runs on one machine, so the page also says this hub listens only on this machine: on a real hub, open it to the network first ( Share Maple with a team , step 1). After Make a chain token the page shows the token once, with a ready-made sn","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"On your proxy: connect to the hub","u":"chaining.html#on-your-proxy-connect-to-the-hub","x":"The wizard after Test the connection . The token field never shows the token, and the page never reads it back. Test the connection asks the hub, without saving or binding anything: whether it answers, whether it takes the token, whether the token is a chain token (one that may say who asked) or a person's own token (a","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"The hub map","u":"chaining.html#the-hub-map","x":"The team hub's map: its parent above, the two proxies below it, and each one's last seven days. A demo setup with stand-in model services. Above: the hubs this proxy uses, and whether they answer ( Check the hubs above asks them; nothing leaves your machine otherwise). In the middle: this proxy, with its own and inheri","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Day to day on the hub","u":"chaining.html#day-to-day-on-the-hub","x":"Each chain token, the proxy it speaks for, and when that proxy last asked. Unbind when the proxy below was replaced: the next proxy to use the token is the one it speaks for. Revoke to cut a proxy off: it can no longer use the hub, within seconds. A proxy below is marked not heard lately after five minutes without aski","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"The technical view","u":"chaining.html#the-technical-view","x":"Everything above can also be done in `config.yaml` and with `maple-proxy` commands, as below, and the page and the commands can be mixed: a change made in one shows in the other. Command Where you run it What it does `maple-proxy chain connect --sso <address>` Your proxy Signs it in to a hub with your company account (","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Personal token or chain token: which do I need?","u":"chaining.html#personal-token-or-chain-token-which-do-i-need","x":"A proxy below a hub holds one of two kinds of credential for it. A personal token is one person's own token. Your proxy gets one when you sign it in with your company account; an admin can also make one for you (People & teams), or you can make one on My access. The hub counts everything the proxy sends as that person.","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Sign in to a hub with your company account","u":"chaining.html#sign-in-to-a-hub-with-your-company-account","x":"What the plain-words steps do, in detail. It needs a hub that runs Maple 2.2 or later with single sign-on and developer sign-in on, and its sign-in address, which must be `https://` (only the address: no path, query or user).","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Sign in: on the page or in a terminal","u":"chaining.html#sign-in-on-the-page-or-in-a-terminal","x":"There are three ways in, and they are one and the same sign-in: a sign-in started in one shows in the others, and any of them can end it. Step On your proxy's setup page In a terminal on your proxy's machine Start Hubs & chaining , Connect this proxy to a parent hub , Sign in with your company account : type The hub's ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"What your proxy keeps, and what the hub sees","u":"chaining.html#what-your-proxy-keeps-and-what-the-hub-sees","x":"On your proxy: a token for you with the `proxy` scope only (it uses the hub's models; it is never a chain token), kept in your proxy's secret store. `config.yaml` gets a `maple` provider for the hub (the name you chose, `hub` by default) at the model address the hub gives, with only a reference to the token. The hub's ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Where your requests go","u":"chaining.html#where-your-requests-go","x":"The hub tells your proxy where its models are. When that address is on the hub's sign-in address's own domain over `https://` (`https://maple.acme.example:11535` beside `https://sso.acme.example`, say), your proxy uses it. When it is anywhere else (another domain, plain `http://`, an IP address, this machine), nothing ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Status and \"Sign in again\"","u":"chaining.html#status-and-sign-in-again","x":"Your running proxy renews its token quietly before it expires (a token lasts 12 hours), for up to 7 days after you signed in in the browser. A laptop that was off for a night or a weekend renews when it is back, as long as the seven days are not over. Then it stops, and you sign in again: Sign in again on the page, or ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Disconnect","u":"chaining.html#disconnect","x":"On the page, Disconnect next to the sign-in. In a terminal: Shell Disconnecting ends the sign-in at the hub (its token is revoked there), forgets it on your proxy, and removes the hub's provider (unless `--keep-provider`). If the hub cannot be reached, it says so: the sign-in is forgotten here, its renewal ends by itse","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"When the hub or this proxy cannot do it","u":"chaining.html#when-the-hub-or-this-proxy-cannot-do-it","x":"What you see Why What to do `<host> does not offer sign-in from a proxy (a Maple release before 2.2, or not a Maple hub): paste a token from your admin instead.` The hub runs an older release, or the address is not a Maple hub's sign-in address Ask the hub's admin for a token `<host> has sign-in from a proxy turned off","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Limits","u":"chaining.html#limits","x":"Not a parent hub. A personal token uses the hub's models as you. It cannot bring the hub's people and teams down or send usage up: that needs a chain token. A hub you signed in to is never this proxy's `chain.parent`, and the page refuses to make it one (paste a chain token to replace the sign-in instead). One proxy, o","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"The two common setups","u":"chaining.html#the-two-common-setups","x":"","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Your own account first, the hub when it runs out","u":"chaining.html#your-own-account-first-the-hub-when-it-runs-out","x":"The laptop uses its own Copilot account. When that account is out of quota or rate limited, requests go to the hub, and come back to the laptop's account once it answers again. YAML The hub's models are reached as `hub/<model>`, whether or not they are listed under the provider. A hub in the middle of a chain (one with","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"The hub first, your own model when the hub says no","u":"chaining.html#the-hub-first-your-own-model-when-the-hub-says-no","x":"The laptop sends everything to the hub. When the hub refuses (a budget there is spent, the hub is down, or the hub's own accounts are out of quota), a local model answers instead. YAML Each refusal from the hub is named the same way as a refusal from any provider, so the usage log says why the fallback ran: `daily budg","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Set up a hub","u":"chaining.html#set-up-a-hub","x":"On the hub, make a token for each proxy that will use it: Shell The token carries two scopes: `proxy`, to use the hub's models, and `proxy-chain`, which lets the proxy holding it say who asked. The token is shown once. The hub's model surface must be reachable from the other proxy: on another machine that means a liste","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"What the wizard writes","u":"chaining.html#what-the-wizard-writes","x":"On the proxy below, Save writes what the hand-written setup above writes: a provider of `type: maple` with the address (`local: true` when the private-network box is ticked), `api_key: ${secret:provider/<name>/api_key}` with the token in the secret store, the models you ticked, `chain.parent` (unless you untick it), `c","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"What the hub map shows","u":"chaining.html#what-the-hub-map-shows","x":"On the map Where it comes from A hub above: answers, latency, checked Check the hubs above asks each `maple` provider's hub, at most once every ten seconds whoever asks; the answers are shown for ten minutes How many hubs further up What the parent's own answer says This proxy Its name and id, its own and inherited peo","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"The proxy-chain scope","u":"chaining.html#the-proxy-chain-scope","x":"Only a token with the `proxy-chain` scope (and the `proxy` scope) is trusted to say who asked. A request with any other token, or none, is recorded as that token's own: headers claiming another person are ignored and removed. A token for everything (all scopes) does not include `proxy-chain`, and a proxy from before 2.","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Who a hub takes a forwarded request to be from","u":"chaining.html#who-a-hub-takes-a-forwarded-request-to-be-from","x":"One of the hub's own people (defined on the hub, or inherited by it from further up) only when that person signed in below with their own token. The proxy where they signed in sends a proof made from that token with the request, and each hub on the way checks it; the token itself never leaves that proxy. A proxy below ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"People and teams cascade down","u":"chaining.html#people-and-teams-cascade-down","x":"With `chain.parent` set, a proxy fetches its parent's people, teams and their tokens at start, keeps a copy in its configuration folder, and asks again at once whenever the parent says something changed (the parent holds each request for up to 30 seconds until it has news). `chain.directory_interval` (a minute by defau","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"What each proxy records","u":"chaining.html#what-each-proxy-records","x":"Every proxy a request passes through writes its own line in its usage log. On each line: Column On a laptop's own request On the hub, for a request from the laptop `person`, `team` who asked who asked, at the laptop `proxy_id`, `proxy_name` the laptop the hub `origin_proxy` the laptop the laptop `hops` 0 1 (2 one hub f","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Usage from below","u":"chaining.html#usage-from-below","x":"With `chain.parent` set, a proxy sends its usage records, and those its own children sent it, to the parent, every `chain.directory_interval`. Only the usage columns are sent, never a prompt or an answer. If the parent is away, the records are sent when it is back, from where the proxy got to; a record already received","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Limits","u":"chaining.html#limits-1","x":"A request that has passed through more than `chain.max_hops` proxies (4 by default) is refused, and so is one that comes back to a proxy it already passed through. Both are answered with status 508. `chain.max_hops` follows `config.yaml` without a restart. A person's budget is enforced on the proxy where they sign in, ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Settings","u":"chaining.html#settings","x":"Setting Default What it does `server.proxy_name` none A readable name for this proxy in usage records and in other proxies' records: letters, digits, `.`, `_` and `-`, up to 64, starting with a letter or digit. `chain.max_hops` 4 The most proxies a request may have passed through before this one (1 to 16). `chain.paren","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Troubleshooting","u":"chaining.html#troubleshooting","x":"Start here `maple-proxy chain status` on the proxy that has the problem says which hubs it uses, whether each answers, and what each sign-in needs. `maple-harness doctor` names the hub and the problem on its Proxy line. What you see Why What to do `chain.parent \"hub\": no provider has that id` `chain.parent` names a pro","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"See also","u":"chaining.html#see-also","x":"Share Maple with a team The whole team picture, from one shared proxy to a hub of hubs. Teams People, teams, tokens and budgets on a hub. Single sign-on Company accounts on the hub, and developer sign-in for laptops. Which setup do I need? Every setup compared, from one laptop to several replicas. Your usage data Every","m":"teams"},{"p":"Azure and Google Cloud","h":"Azure and Google Cloud","u":"clouds.html","x":"Use the models your company already pays for in its own cloud: Azure OpenAI and Microsoft Foundry (including Claude in Foundry), and Google's Vertex AI, which Google now also calls Agent Platform (Gemini, and Claude on Vertex). Requests are billed to your cloud account under your cloud agreement. Maple stores no cloud ","m":"both"},{"p":"Azure and Google Cloud","h":"In plain words","u":"clouds.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Use the models your company already pays for in Azure (Azure OpenAI, Microsoft Foundry) or Google Cloud (Vertex AI), billed to your cloud account. Why you want it. Your company's agreement, region and data rules apply, and you usually need no new key: on a laptop Maple uses your `az login` or `gcloud` sign-","m":"both"},{"p":"Azure and Google Cloud","h":"Azure OpenAI and Microsoft Foundry","u":"clouds.html#azure-openai-and-microsoft-foundry","x":"One provider is one Azure resource. Its deployments are its models : the model id is the deployment name you chose in the Azure portal or Foundry. YAML On the setup page: Add provider , type Azure OpenAI / Foundry , the resource's endpoint as the base URL, then add the deployment names ( Detect models lists them when t","m":"both"},{"p":"Azure and Google Cloud","h":"Signing in","u":"clouds.html#signing-in","x":"An API key : put it in the provider's API key field on the setup page (it goes to the secret store, and the configuration keeps a `${secret:...}` reference). Keys are under Keys and Endpoint on the resource in the portal. Microsoft Entra ID : leave the key empty. The proxy asks Entra ID for a token when it needs one, k","m":"both"},{"p":"Azure and Google Cloud","h":"All the settings","u":"clouds.html#all-the-settings","x":"YAML `api_version: v1` uses Azure's versionless `/openai/v1` API. A dated version uses the older `/openai/deployments/<name>/chat/completions?api-version=...` route, for a resource or gateway that needs it. `client_id` works with `managed-identity` and `workload-identity`; with `auto`, set `AZURE_CLIENT_ID` instead.","m":"both"},{"p":"Azure and Google Cloud","h":"Google Vertex AI (Agent Platform)","u":"clouds.html#google-vertex-ai-agent-platform","x":"One provider is one Google Cloud project in one location : a region (`us-east5`, `europe-west4`), a multi-region (`us`, `eu`) or `global`. YAML Models whose id starts with `claude` go to Anthropic's models on Vertex; the rest to Google's Gemini models. Use the model ids from the Model Garden page of each model. On the ","m":"both"},{"p":"Azure and Google Cloud","h":"Signing in","u":"clouds.html#signing-in-1","x":"The proxy uses Google's application default credentials , found in this order: The file named by `GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS`: a service account key, or a workload identity federation configuration (for a pod or runner outside Google Cloud). Your own sign-in, on a laptop: Shell The metadata server of the machine or","m":"both"},{"p":"Azure and Google Cloud","h":"Vertex's own API through the proxy","u":"clouds.html#vertexs-own-api-through-the-proxy","x":"A tool that speaks Vertex's own API (`generateContent`, Claude's `rawPredict`) can use it through the proxy with a pass-through route on the provider (`paths: [\"/publishers\"]`): the proxy checks the caller, adds this provider's Google token, and records the tokens the answer reports. Only publisher model calls in the p","m":"both"},{"p":"Azure and Google Cloud","h":"Regions and accounts as fallbacks","u":"clouds.html#regions-and-accounts-as-fallbacks","x":"Each provider is an account in Maple's sense, so several regions, projects or resources work like several Copilot accounts: list them in `provider_fallbacks` , assign them to teams , and share them through a hub . YAML `vertex-us/gemini-2.5-pro` asks for one region's copy. A plain `gemini-2.5-pro` goes to one of them, ","m":"both"},{"p":"Azure and Google Cloud","h":"On Kubernetes","u":"clouds.html#on-kubernetes","x":"The Kubernetes samples include a component for each cloud's workload identity in `config/cloud-identity/`. Add it to your overlay: YAML","m":"both"},{"p":"Azure and Google Cloud","h":"AKS (workload identity)","u":"clouds.html#aks-workload-identity","x":"Enable the OIDC issuer and workload identity on the cluster, and create a user-assigned managed identity with the Cognitive Services OpenAI User role on the resource. Federate it with the proxy's service account, `system:serviceaccount:maple:maple-proxy` (`az identity federated-credential create --issuer <the cluster's","m":"both"},{"p":"Azure and Google Cloud","h":"GKE (workload identity federation)","u":"clouds.html#gke-workload-identity-federation","x":"Enable workload identity federation for GKE on the cluster and its node pools. Grant the proxy's Kubernetes service account the Vertex AI User role, either directly (`principal://iam.googleapis.com/projects/<number>/locations/global/workloadIdentityPools/<project>.svc.id.goog/subject/ns/maple/sa/maple-proxy`) or throug","m":"both"},{"p":"Azure and Google Cloud","h":"Cost and usage","u":"clouds.html#cost-and-usage","x":"Neither cloud states a price on its replies, so the proxy records the tokens each call used (input, cached input and output) and no price. For Claude models, `estimate_cost: true` on the provider adds an estimate from Anthropic's list prices, labelled as an estimate; your cloud bill can differ (commitments, regional pr","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Commands and keys","u":"commands.html","x":"Everything you can type in Maple Harness, in one place: the keys and permission modes of a session, the slash commands, the commands you run from a shell, and the flags that start it. Each entry says what it does, its default and its trade-off. Settings in `config.yaml` are on Harness settings , and the proxy's on Prox","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"In plain words","u":"commands.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. The list of everything you can type: slash commands in a session (`/model`, `/cost`, `/remote`), the keys, the modes, and the shell commands of both programs. What to do. `/help` in a session lists the commands, `?` on an empty message box lists every key, and `Ctrl+P` finds any command by what it does (`fo","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Modes and keys","u":"commands.html#modes-and-keys","x":"Permission modes decide what happens when the model wants to write a file or run a command. Mode What happens Helps `ask` (default) You are asked before each write or command. `deny` rules are absolute Efficacious `auto` Writes and commands run without asking; `deny` rules still apply. Unlike `yolo`, it can be chosen w","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Steer or queue while it works","u":"commands.html#steer-or-queue-while-it-works","x":"The message box is never disabled: type while the model works. What you type then either steers the work in progress or queues for after it: Steers (`↳ steering`): a correction or an addition, such as \"also handle the empty list\", \"use the existing helper instead\", \"don't touch the migrations\", or any short message wit","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Reading the conversation","u":"commands.html#reading-the-conversation","x":"In plain words: each thing the model does is one short line, named the way you would say it (`Read(main.go)`, `Bash(go test ./...)`, `Search(pattern: \"TODO\")`), with a mark that turns green when it worked and red when it failed. What came back sits under it. Long results are folded to a summary you can open, an edit is","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"How the interface looks","u":"commands.html#how-the-interface-looks","x":"In plain words: Maple Harness keeps its maple colours and its sense of humour. Lists open as a framed box over the conversation, the permission mode is a coloured chip at the start of the status bar, `/theme` lets you try every theme on the whole screen before you keep one, and a long turn that went well ends with a sh","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Slash commands","u":"commands.html#slash-commands","x":"A slash command that this session does not have (not built in, and not one of your own commands or skills) is never sent to the model: Maple Harness says so and sends nothing. To send a message that starts with `/`, begin it with `//`; a path such as `/etc/hosts` at the start of a message is sent as it is.","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Session","u":"commands.html#session","x":"Command What it does Helps `/help` Shows the commands and keys `/clear` Starts over: clears the screen and the conversation. Memory notes are kept Efficient `/quit`, `/exit` Exit. With background agents still working they are stopped; `/quit keep` keeps them working until they finish (see When you quit with agents out ","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Models and effort","u":"commands.html#models-and-effort","x":"Command What it does Helps `/model` Opens a list of the models the proxy serves. Bare `/model` also shows what automatic choice would use for each kind of work Effective `/model <name>` Switches straight to that model or alias. In the plain line mode (`--repl`), bare `/model` prints the list Effective `/model auto` Cho","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Context and cost","u":"commands.html#context-and-cost","x":"Command What it does Helps `/context` What the model can see: memory notes, the project index, the tools it is offered, the window used, and what the session has spent Efficient `/cost` What the session has spent: totals, the last requests with their cache hit rate (and where a model change or a low hit rate lost the c","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Memory, project and tools","u":"commands.html#memory-project-and-tools","x":"Command What it does Helps `/memory [list|show <name>|rm <name>|path]` The agent's notes about the project and you Effective `/graph [stats|reindex|connections <id>|path]` Queries and rebuilds the project index of notes and code symbols the harness looks things up in Efficient `/jobs` Lists background commands started ","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Safety","u":"commands.html#safety","x":"Command What it does Helps `/sandbox` How the `bash` tool is contained: the mode, the backend, what is writable and hidden. See The shell sandbox Efficacious `/sandbox auto`, `/sandbox strict`, `/sandbox off` Contains commands when a backend is available; refuses to run them without one; or no containment for this sess","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Agents","u":"commands.html#agents","x":"Command What it does Helps `/agents` Manage subagents: list, view, create, edit, delete Effective `/agent <name> <task>`, `@name <task>` Runs a subagent on a task Efficient, Effective","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Remote and notifications","u":"commands.html#remote-and-notifications","x":"Command What it does Helps `/remote` Shares this session with a phone or another Maple Harness. See Control it from your phone Effective `/remote pair` Opens a list of addresses, then makes a short pairing code and one QR for the address you pick. `--via`, `--token` and `--fixed` change what it makes; `--watch-only` ma","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Agents and the agents tray","u":"commands.html#agents-and-the-agents-tray","x":"Subagents are helper agents you define in `.claude/agents/<name>.md` (or `~/.claude/agents`), or use from the built-in set. The model can hand them jobs, and you can run one yourself with `@name <task>` (`Tab` completes the name). While any run, a tray sits just above the message box: a `main` row and one row per subag","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Your own commands and skills","u":"commands.html#your-own-commands-and-skills","x":"`.claude/commands/<name>.md` becomes `/name` (with `$ARGUMENTS`, or `$1` to `$9`, substituted). Its front matter's `description` and `argument-hint` show in completion; a hint may be written as Claude Code's documentation writes it, without quotes (`argument-hint: [file]`, or `[pr] [priority]`). `.claude/skills/<name>/","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Commands you run from a shell","u":"commands.html#commands-you-run-from-a-shell","x":"`maple-harness` with no command starts the full-screen interface. The short name is `mpl`. On a terminal, commands that print lists or status use tables and your theme's colours, fitted to the window. Piped into another program or a file, the output is plain text with no colour codes and no cut-off cells. `--no-color` ","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Command-line flags","u":"commands.html#command-line-flags","x":"Flag What it does `--prompt \"text\"` Runs one turn and exits, printing the answer. Everything else goes to the error stream, so `> out.txt` captures just the answer. Answers print as they arrive; when a check sends one back and the answer that stands is not the last one printed, it is printed again at the end (the error","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Troubleshooting","u":"commands.html#troubleshooting","x":"What you see Why What to do `Shift+Enter` sends the message instead of starting a new line Some terminals send `Shift+Enter` as a plain `Enter` `\\` then `Enter`, `Alt+Enter` or `Ctrl+J` all make a new line `Shift+Tab` never reaches `yolo` yolo exists only in a session started with `--yolo`; no config file can turn it o","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"See also","u":"commands.html#see-also","x":"Harness settings Every `harness.*` setting, also editable with `/settings`. What /cost tells you Reading the spend, the cache and the most expensive tool results. Subagents Helpers, the agents tray and the lineup. Control it from your phone `/remote`, the gateway, pairing and sharing one session. Drive it from another ","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Harness settings","u":"configuration.html","x":"The harness reads the settings under `harness:` in `config.yaml` (usually `~/.config/maple-harness/config.yaml`). The proxy's settings are in the same file and are covered in the Proxy settings . Changes under `harness:` apply the next time you start the harness; the proxy picks up its own changes without a restart. Ev","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"In plain words","u":"configuration.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Every setting of the agent, the part of `config.yaml` under `harness:`. What to do. Most people never edit it: `/settings` in a session lists every setting and changes every one of them, and `/configuration set <key> <value>` any single value. The lists of entries with several fields each (permission rules,","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Lists in /settings","u":"configuration.html#lists-in-settings","x":"In plain words. Pick one of `permission.rules`, `mcp_servers`, `lsp_servers`, `skills.sources` or `notifications.sinks` in `/settings` and press Enter: you see its entries. Enter changes the one picked in a form of its fields, `a` adds one, `d` removes the one picked (after you press `y`), and for the two whose order m","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Settings at a glance","u":"configuration.html#settings-at-a-glance","x":"Key Default Helps `default_model` none Effective `compress_with_model` `off` Efficient `compress_model`, `compress_scope` none (chosen for you), `same-account` Efficient, Efficacious `digest_key_points` `on` Effective `auto_model`, `auto_model_map`, `auto_avoid`, `auto_model_resolution`, `auto_first_weight` off, none, ","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Per-project overrides","u":"configuration.html#per-project-overrides","x":"A project can carry its own `.mapleharness/config.yaml` (or `.mapleharness/config.json`) that changes some of `harness:`'s settings just for that project, on top of your global `config.yaml`. It only needs the keys it changes: YAML Anything the project's file does not mention keeps your global value — setting `loop_gua","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Model choice","u":"configuration.html#model-choice","x":"Key Default What it does Trade-off Helps `default_model` none The model or alias a session starts on. With none set, it is `gpt-6-sol` when the proxy serves it, else the first model that can use tools. If you name one the proxy does not serve, the harness starts on the first model it does serve that can use tools, and ","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Long conversations","u":"configuration.html#long-conversations","x":"When a conversation outgrows the model's window, older messages leave the model's view. What leaves it is kept and searchable: Older parts of a long conversation stay searchable. Every message is stored, word for word, beside the saved session before it leaves the model's view, and the model can look any of it up again","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Permissions and safety","u":"configuration.html#permissions-and-safety","x":"Key Default What it does Trade-off Helps `permission.mode` `ask` The starting mode: `ask`, `auto` or `yolo`. `yolo` is honoured only when you start with `--yolo` Efficacious `permission.rules[].pattern`, `permission.rules[].decision` shipped rules Rules matched in order: `decision` is `allow`, `ask` or `deny`, and `den","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Runaways and long output","u":"configuration.html#runaways-and-long-output","x":"Key Default What it does Trade-off Helps `loop_guard.mode` `stop` What to do when the same problem keeps failing after different attempts: `off`, `warn` (one note to the model), or `stop` (the note, then the turn ends with a summary for you) `stop` may end a run you would have let continue; `warn` never ends one Effici","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Cost preview","u":"configuration.html#cost-preview","x":"Off by default, and quiet when on: it is shown only for the messages you asked about, never sent to the model, and never kept in the conversation. Key Default What it does Trade-off Helps `cost_preview` `off` Before a message is sent, shows about what its first request is likely to cost, always labelled estimated , wit","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Language servers and shell writes","u":"configuration.html#language-servers-and-shell-writes","x":"Key Default What it does Trade-off Helps `lsp` `auto` With no `lsp_servers`, finds language servers from the project's marker files and `PATH`, and adds their errors to the result of each file write or edit, so the model fixes its own mistakes. `off` turns detection, the diagnostics and the lookup tools off The first e","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Tools, skills and hooks","u":"configuration.html#tools-skills-and-hooks","x":"Key Default What it does Helps `tools.apply_patch` `auto` Whether the agent is offered `apply_patch`, which edits files with a patch in the format OpenAI's Codex models write. `auto`: offered to the models trained on it, told by the model's name (GPT-4.1 and later, GPT-5 and later, o3, o4, Codex, gpt-oss). `on`: to eve","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Hook files","u":"configuration.html#hook-files","x":"With `hooks.files` empty, hooks are read from these files, in this order, each adding to the ones before: `hooks.json` in the configuration folder (yours), then the project's `.mapleharness/hooks.json`, `.claude/hooks.json`, and the `hooks` key of Claude Code's project settings, `.claude/settings.json` and `.claude/set","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Subagents and remote control","u":"configuration.html#subagents-and-remote-control","x":"Key Default What it does Trade-off Helps `subagents.max_parallel` `3` (in `auto`: what the backends serve, when the proxy or `capacity` says for every subagent model) How many subagents run at once (1 to 16). More wait for a free slot. Set, it wins in every mode A local model may not serve several at once Efficient, Ef","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Notifications","u":"configuration.html#notifications","x":"Messages to your phone or desktop when a session needs approval, stops with an error, or needs your guidance. They say which project and what happened, and nothing else: never a command, a file's content or an error's text. Off until you list a destination. Try it with `maple-harness notify test`. Separately from these","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Housekeeping and appearance","u":"configuration.html#housekeeping-and-appearance","x":"Key Default What it does Helps `retention.sessions_days` `30` How old a saved session must be before `cleanup` treats it as old Efficient `retention.keep_per_dir` `5` How many recent sessions per directory `cleanup` always keeps Efficient `retention.auto_prune` off Run what `cleanup --yes` would, once a day, when a ses","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Where the harness finds the proxy","u":"configuration.html#where-the-harness-finds-the-proxy","x":"Key Default What it does `version` `1` The configuration file's format version `server.openai_compat` `127.0.0.1:11535` The proxy's OpenAI-compatible address, which the harness talks to `server.anthropic_compat` `127.0.0.1:11537` The proxy's Anthropic-compatible address `server.webui` `127.0.0.1:11539` The proxy's setu","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"An annotated example","u":"configuration.html#an-annotated-example","x":"YAML Read next: Commands and keys , Proxy settings , and Settings by goal .","m":"both"},{"p":"Content export and callbacks","h":"Content export and callbacks","u":"content-export.html","x":"The usage log and the metrics never hold what people wrote. Some teams want that, for debugging prompts or for an evaluation tool such as Langfuse or Phoenix. This page is about the two ways to send requests elsewhere: content export , which sends the prompts and answers of the people and teams you name, and callbacks ","m":"both"},{"p":"Content export and callbacks","h":"In plain words","u":"content-export.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Content export copies the prompts and answers of the teams and people you choose to a tool you run or subscribe to. Callbacks copy each request's usage line, the same one the usage log keeps, to other places. Why you want it. To see why an agent went wrong, to score answers in an evaluation tool, or to keep","m":"both"},{"p":"Content export and callbacks","h":"Content export","u":"content-export.html#content-export","x":"YAML Store a header's value with `maple-proxy secrets set otel/content-authorization`; the file holds only the reference. Who is covered. A request is exported when its person is in `people`, its team is in `teams`, or `everyone` is on. A request with no person or team (a proxy alone, without people ) is exported only ","m":"both"},{"p":"Content export and callbacks","h":"The request log","u":"content-export.html#the-request-log","x":"With `keep_local` above 0, the latest covered requests are also kept in the proxy's memory, and the card's Request log shows them with their content, newest first, and can find one by its request id. Each request is one line: when it was answered (in your browser's time), the account and model, the person and team, and","m":"both"},{"p":"Content export and callbacks","h":"The playground","u":"content-export.html#the-playground","x":"The same card ends with a Playground : choose a model name (the box offers your model groups, aliases and models, and takes any other name you type) and write a message, select Send , and see the answer with its input and output tokens. Use it to check that a model answers, or what a guardrail does with a message. The ","m":"both"},{"p":"Content export and callbacks","h":"Callbacks","u":"content-export.html#callbacks","x":"Each finished request's usage line also goes to every callback, in batches (100 lines, or every 10 seconds, by default). The line is exactly the one the usage log writes. A callback with `content: on` (webhook and Langfuse only) also gets the content of the requests content export covers, as a separate item naming its ","m":"both"},{"p":"Content export and callbacks","h":"Technical details","u":"content-export.html#technical-details","x":"Content goes through one port, handed the request as it was sent to the provider and what came back, after the usage line is written; it is asked first whether that person or team is covered, so nothing is prepared for anyone else. Streams are gathered as they pass (text, reasoning and tool calls, at most 4 MB) and han","m":"both"},{"p":"Content export and callbacks","h":"Related","u":"content-export.html#related","x":"Metrics and telemetry export Prometheus and OpenTelemetry, from what the proxy already knows. Proxy settings: Content export Every content export and callback key. Teams People, teams, access tokens, budgets and rate limits on one proxy. See and filter your usage The usage log on your own disk: filters, views and Grafa","m":"both"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"What /cost tells you","u":"cost.html","x":"`/cost` is the session's own bill, read back to you in plain numbers: what has been spent, request by request, and which parts of the conversation are costing the most by being sent again and again. This page walks through each part of its output and, for each thing it can flag, what to actually do about it. It makes n","m":"single"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"In plain words","u":"cost.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. `/cost`, typed in a session, is the session's own bill so far: tokens, AI units or an estimated dollar figure, request by request. Why you want it. It points at what costs the most (often something re-sent on every turn) and says what to do about it. What to do. Type `/cost` whenever you wonder. The whole p","m":"single"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"The totals line","u":"cost.html#the-totals-line","x":"The first line is the session so far: input and output tokens, how many requests were billed, and — where the provider states one — AI units (Copilot) or an estimated dollar figure (see Anthropic and estimated cost below; for Grok , the price xAI's reply stated). Under it: the model in use, how many requests were bille","m":"single"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"While a turn runs","u":"cost.html#while-a-turn-runs","x":"The line above the message box says what the turn has cost so far, next to how long it has run and the tokens it has generated and sent: `0.75 AI units` when the proxy states AI units, `~$0.0300 est.` when it estimates dollars, both when both apply. The status bar's `spent:` shows the session's total in the same form. ","m":"single"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"Subagents","u":"cost.html#subagents","x":"If this session delegated any work with `@agent` or the `delegate` tool, `/cost` ends with a \"Subagents\" line — how many ran, and what they cost on their own — and a \"Session + subagents\" line, which is the true total: the main conversation's own spend plus every subagent's. This is the number that matches what your ac","m":"single"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"The last requests table","u":"cost.html#the-last-requests-table","x":"A row per recent request: input, cached, written and output tokens, AI units, the cache hit rate for that one request, and the model. Two things get flagged automatically: \"model changed\" — a different model answered this request than the one before it. A new model starts with nothing cached, so the whole conversation ","m":"single"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"Where the next request's input goes","u":"cost.html#where-the-next-requests-input-goes","x":"An estimated breakdown of what the next request would send, by kind: the system prompt, the tools on offer, your own messages, anything looked up on your behalf, the model's own replies and tool calls, and tool results. It is a token-count estimate (about four characters to a token), useful for proportions rather than ","m":"single"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"Most expensive tool results","u":"cost.html#most-expensive-tool-results","x":"A tool's result is not paid for once. It stays in the conversation and is sent again on every request after it, until it is trimmed or the session ends — so a single large result from early in a long session can end up costing far more than its own size ever suggested. This section ranks the tool results still in the c","m":"single"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"Anthropic and estimated cost","u":"cost.html#anthropic-and-estimated-cost","x":"Copilot states a price for every call it serves. Claude through Anthropic's own API does not — its responses carry token counts only, never a price. `/cost` reflects that honestly: an Anthropic request shows its tokens like any other, and no price, unless the provider is set up to estimate one. An operator can turn on ","m":"single"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"When Maple can't tell which Claude model a name is","u":"cost.html#when-maple-cant-tell-which-claude-model-a-name-is","x":"The estimate needs to know which Claude model a call went to. Maple reads it from the model's name. A name such as `claude-sonnet-5-5` or `claude-opus-4-6-20260101` is clear, and so is a deployment name that includes the model and its version (`my-sonnet-5-prod`). Some names are not clear: a name that looks like a mode","m":"single"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"Before a large message: the cost preview","u":"cost.html#before-a-large-message-the-cost-preview","x":"With `cost_preview` set to `large` or `on` (see Harness settings ; it is off by default), the harness says, before a message is sent, about what its first request is likely to cost. It looks like this (an illustration of the format, not a measurement): The line goes to the plain output, and the status bar shows a short","m":"single"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"When the output limit goes on reasoning","u":"cost.html#when-the-output-limit-goes-on-reasoning","x":"A reasoning model thinks before it answers, and its thinking counts against the same output limit as the answer. When it uses the whole limit thinking, the reply stops with no answer at all. The proxy says so: Maple Harness shows a warning (\"the output limit was used up by reasoning before any answer; raise max_tokens\"","m":"single"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"Related","u":"cost.html#related","x":"See and reduce what a task costs Why long tasks cost what they do, and the settings that help. Proxy settings Every card on the setup page and every key in `config.yaml`. Commands and keys Everything you can type in a session, every key, and the shell commands. Settings by goal The settings that serve efficiency, effic","m":"single"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","u":"data-lake.html","x":"The lake is where your usage history goes to stay. The daily files and PostgreSQL keep a window (90 days unless you change it); the lake keeps every record it was given, in a form that is quick to query over months or years, and lets you read it as it was at an earlier point. This page sets it up on one machine and for","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"In plain words","u":"data-lake.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. A place for your whole usage history, quick to query over months or years, that you can also read as it was on an earlier day. Why you want it. The daily files keep 90 days by default; the lake keeps everything. The clicks. On the setup page, Data & storage , Usage data , set Analytics files to keep a lake,","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"What DuckLake is","u":"data-lake.html#what-ducklake-is","x":"DuckLake is an open table format from the makers of DuckDB . It has two parts: data files : your usage records as Parquet files, in a folder or in object storage (S3 and compatible); a catalog : a small database that lists those files and every change made to them. On one machine it is a DuckDB file; for a team it is a","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"Before you start","u":"data-lake.html#before-you-start","x":"Install DuckDB's command line, from duckdb.org , and put `duckdb` on your `PATH` (or pass `--duckdb /path/to/duckdb`). These pages were tested with DuckDB 1.5.6. Install the extensions the lake needs, once: Shell The proxy runs `duckdb` with automatic extension downloads turned off, so a missing extension is an error, ","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"On one machine","u":"data-lake.html#on-one-machine","x":"`maple-proxy usage lake` makes a lake from your daily files, or adds to it what they gained since the last run. By default it lives in `analytics/lake` inside your usage folder: the catalog `maple.ducklake` and a `data` folder, both private to you. See what it would do first; `--dry-run` writes nothing: The next day, o","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"Let the proxy keep it current","u":"data-lake.html#let-the-proxy-keep-it-current","x":"Set Analytics files to Monthly Parquet and a DuckLake on the setup page's Usage data card, or in `config.yaml`: YAML The proxy then refreshes the lake and runs its upkeep when it starts and once a day after the usage cleanup, with your `duckdb` program. It also packs the closed days into monthly Parquet files (`parquet","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"Query it","u":"data-lake.html#query-it","x":"`--emit-sql attach` prints what to run in `duckdb`: it attaches the lake read-only. Shell Inside, `main.usage` and `main.events` hold every record, and the named views (`maple.daily_spend`, `maple.sessions`, `maple.people` and the rest) work as they do over the files. Recipes are in Query your usage .","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"For a team: PostgreSQL and a shared lake","u":"data-lake.html#for-a-team-postgresql-and-a-shared-lake","x":"This section is for central mode : several copies of one proxy sharing PostgreSQL and Redis. A team on one shared proxy with daily files uses the single-machine lake on the hub. Storage mode redis (several replicas, no PostgreSQL) has no lake: `usage lake` is refused there; export the usage (`usage export`) to keep his","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"What the move does","u":"data-lake.html#what-the-move-does","x":"It takes the rows PostgreSQL stored since the last move, in the order PostgreSQL stored them, and only those stored at least an hour before the run (`--settle 1h`), so a write still in progress is never skipped. A line that arrives late with an old time (a hub's backlog from below, a replica that could not reach Postgr","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"Try it by hand first","u":"data-lake.html#try-it-by-hand-first","x":"With `MAPLE_POSTGRES_DSN` holding a connection string (it is read from the environment when the SQL runs, and never written into the SQL). Keep the password out of it, in `PGPASSWORD`: DuckDB can repeat the connection string in an error message. Shell What a first move printed, and a second one right after it: (`--cata","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"The Kubernetes CronJobs","u":"data-lake.html#the-kubernetes-cronjobs","x":"The Kubernetes samples run the move hourly and the upkeep daily as two CronJobs (`manifest/maple-lake`, and the overlay `config/ha-lake` on top of `config/ha`). They run the official DuckDB image on SQL the proxy writes for you. Generate it, put it in the sample's `sql/` folder, and apply: Shell Then apply the `config/","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"Credentials","u":"data-lake.html#credentials","x":"PostgreSQL. The job reads its connection string from `MAPLE_POSTGRES_DSN` (a Secret in the sample); put the password in `PGPASSWORD` from the same Secret rather than in the string. Give the job its own role rather than the proxy's: it needs to read the usage tables and to own the catalog's schema, nothing else. As the ","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"Read-only access for analysts","u":"data-lake.html#read-only-access-for-analysts","x":"Give analysts a role that can only read. For the lake, it needs to read the catalog's schema (and, with `ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES`, the tables the lake adds to it later), and read access to the data files: SQL To also query the live data in PostgreSQL, add `GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA public TO analyst` and `GRANT SELECT ON ","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"Maintenance","u":"data-lake.html#maintenance","x":"Each move adds a little: up to `--inline-rows` rows (10,000 by default) are kept in the catalog itself rather than written as a new Parquet file, so frequent small moves create no small files. Once a day the upkeep: writes the rows kept in the catalog out as Parquet; merges small files towards `--target-file-mb` (32 MB","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"The lake on the setup page","u":"data-lake.html#the-lake-on-the-setup-page","x":"The setup page's Data & storage tab shows the lake as its own jobs last left it: Last move and Rows moved then : when rows last went in, and how many. Rows in the lake , Data files (Parquet files and their size; rows still kept in the catalog are in no file until the upkeep writes them out) and Snapshots . Last upkeep ","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"Time travel and snapshots","u":"data-lake.html#time-travel-and-snapshots","x":"Every load, move and upkeep is a snapshot. List them, then read a table as it was at one: SQL SQL `AT (TIMESTAMP => TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-10-01 00:00:00+00')` reads the lake as it was at a time instead. Use it to reproduce a report exactly as it was first run, or to see what a late batch changed. Snapshots older than `--ex","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"Hubs: every proxy's data in one lake","u":"data-lake.html#hubs-every-proxys-data-in-one-lake","x":"A hub keeps the usage the proxies below it send up (see Usage from below ), and its lake takes both: its own lines with `source` `local` and the ones sent up with `source` `child`. On a top hub with two proxies below it: The views that count each request once (`maple.client_requests`, `maple.origins`, `maple.proxies`) ","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"Backups","u":"data-lake.html#backups","x":"One machine. The lake is its catalog file and its data folder (`analytics/lake`). Copy both together while no refresh is running (not during the proxy's daily run). The daily files can rebuild only the days they still hold, so the lake is the one copy of anything older: back it up if that history matters. Shell Restore","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"Troubleshooting","u":"data-lake.html#troubleshooting","x":"`the duckdb command line was not found` Install DuckDB's command line and put it on the `PATH`, or pass `--duckdb`. With `usage_analytics: lake` or `duckdb` and no `duckdb`, the proxy makes only the monthly Parquet files and logs this once. `... not an absolute path` / `... can be written by any user` / `found only thr","m":"both"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"How developers sign in","u":"developer-access.html","x":"A developer on a shared proxy needs a token and a few lines that point their tools at the proxy. There are two ways to get them: a token from an admin (works everywhere, no extra setup), or signing in with the company account through the proxy's single sign-on address (the developer makes their own tokens). Both can ru","m":"teams"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"In plain words","u":"developer-access.html#in-plain-words","x":"Your admin can give you a token. They make it on the setup page's People & teams tab, copy it to you once, with the lines to paste into your tools. Nothing else to set up. With Maple on your laptop, you paste it on your own proxy's Hubs & chaining tab: your proxy joins the hub as you, and Maple Harness keeps using your","m":"teams"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"Step by step, with pictures","u":"developer-access.html#step-by-step-with-pictures","x":"The whole sign-in as a developer sees it, from the first visit to a revoked laptop. The pictures come from a demo setup: a hub called `acme-hub` whose sign-in address is `https://sso.acme.example:12464`, ben's laptop proxy `ben-laptop`, and a stand-in identity provider in place of your company's. Your company's sign-in","m":"teams"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"1. The admin turns developer sign-in on","u":"developer-access.html#1-the-admin-turns-developer-sign-in-on","x":"In plain words: nothing below works until an admin says who may sign in and whether they are let in at once. On the setup page, Single sign-on , step 6. Developers (optional) : Developer sign-in is Automatic (people in the listed domains or groups are added at their first sign-in) or Ask an admin (a first sign-in waits","m":"teams"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"2. Your first visit","u":"developer-access.html#2-your-first-visit","x":"In plain words: open the address your admin gave you, sign in the way you sign in at work, and you land on your own page, My access . The address shows one button, Sign in with your company's name. It is the same page admins sign in on, and it says so: admins manage the proxy there, developers get their own access ther","m":"teams"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"3. When an admin must accept you first","u":"developer-access.html#3-when-an-admin-must-accept-you-first","x":"In plain words: with Ask an admin , your first sign-in only sends a request. Wait for an admin, then sign in again. What a developer (cara, in the demo) sees while she waits. The admin sees the request on People & teams , under Asked to use this proxy , and presses Accept (as a new person, with the team her groups sugg","m":"teams"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"4. Make a token","u":"developer-access.html#4-make-a-token","x":"In plain words: under Make a token , say what it is for, press Make a token , then Copy . It is shown once: paste it where it goes before you leave the page. Name the token, then Make a token. Shown once: Copy takes the whole token (a narrow screen shows only its start).","m":"teams"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"5. Sign your laptop's proxy in","u":"developer-access.html#5-sign-your-laptops-proxy-in","x":"In plain words: run one command on your laptop, open the link it prints (a phone works), type the code, check the proxy's name, and confirm. Your proxy then reaches the hub as you, and keeps itself signed in for a week. In a terminal on the laptop (the setup page's Sign in with your company account does the same): The ","m":"teams"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"6. Afterwards: see it, revoke it","u":"developer-access.html#6-afterwards-see-it-revoke-it","x":"In plain words: My access lists your proxy as your proxy (renews itself) . Revoke cuts it off at once; the laptop then asks you to sign in again. Your proxy's token next to the one you made. After Revoke , the laptop proxy's next request to the hub is refused, and its Hubs & chaining tab says so: Revoked at the hub: si","m":"teams"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"7. When a sign-in is refused","u":"developer-access.html#7-when-a-sign-in-is-refused","x":"In plain words: if your account is not one the admin allowed (another domain, a group not on the list), the hub says so and nothing is made for you. Ask the admin. An account outside the developers' domains. A demo setup. The admin sees it under Refused sign-ins on People & teams ( What admins see ).","m":"teams"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"A token from an admin","u":"developer-access.html#a-token-from-an-admin","x":"On the setup page, People & teams , open the person's Access and tokens : Make a token : what it is for (their laptop, CI), and when it expires (never, by default, or 7, 30 or 90 days, or a year). It has the `proxy` scope only and is tied to the person at once, so their budget, models and accounts apply from the first ","m":"teams"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"Signing in with your company account","u":"developer-access.html#signing-in-with-your-company-account","x":"When your admin turns on developer sign-in ( Single sign-on, Developer sign-in ), open the single sign-on address and sign in. Depending on how it is set up: Automatic : you are added as a person at your first sign-in (your team comes from your groups), and land on My access. Ask an admin : your first sign-in says Requ","m":"teams"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"Sign your own proxy in","u":"developer-access.html#sign-your-own-proxy-in","x":"On your own machine, Maple Harness talks to its own proxy, and your other tools can too. To use the team hub, that proxy signs in to the hub with your company account, and the hub gives it a token for you. There are three ways to start it, and they are the same sign-in: one started in one place shows in the others, and","m":"teams"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"Advanced: a token for CI and containers","u":"developer-access.html#advanced-a-token-for-ci-and-containers","x":"Where no proxy of your own can run (a CI job, a container), Maple Harness can use this proxy directly with a token, from an admin or made on My access: Shell It asks for the token without showing it (or reads it from standard input), keeps it in the secret store and writes `harness.proxy` in its `config.yaml`. `maple-h","m":"teams"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"What admins see","u":"developer-access.html#what-admins-see","x":"On People & teams , each person's Access and tokens says how they sign in (tokens from an admin, or single sign-on with their provider and email) and lists their tokens as above, with Forget their sign-in (their next sign-in is matched again; their tokens work until they expire, and their proxies' sign-ins stop renewin","m":"teams"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"Expiry, renewal and revoking","u":"developer-access.html#expiry-renewal-and-revoking","x":"Token Expires Renewed Made by an admin Never, unless they chose a date (through single sign-on: 90 days unless chosen) No Made on My access After `developers.token_days` (30 by default, at most 90) Rotate gives a fresh one Your proxy's, signed in with your company account 12 hours Quietly, for up to 7 days after the ap","m":"teams"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"Limits","u":"developer-access.html#limits","x":"A developer may hold 5 live tokens (`developers.max_tokens`) and make 10 an hour. Automatic onboarding adds at most 50 people a day; more sign-ins wait for an admin. With settings from a ConfigMap, developers cannot be added or make tokens themselves (My access says so): an admin gives them one. A proxy with replicas, ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"Run the proxy in Docker","u":"docker.html","x":"This page runs `maple-proxy` in a container: one container with everything it keeps on one volume, and, when one is not enough, three replicas sharing PostgreSQL and Redis behind a load balancer. The files it uses are samples in the `infra/` folder of the public repository : copy them and change what you need. On your ","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"In plain words","u":"docker.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. The proxy in a container, built from a release you can check, for a server or a team. Why you want it. It runs next to your other services, keeps everything on one volume, and can grow to several copies behind a load balancer. Do you need it? Not on your own computer: there the proxy is one program. If you ","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"What you get","u":"docker.html#what-you-get","x":"One container, its state on one volume. The released binary, verified. The image is built from a release: the build downloads `maple-proxy` for your architecture from the release, checks it against the release's `SHA256SUMS` (and, for a signed release, that file's signature against the project's release key, as the ins","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"Quick start: the published image","u":"docker.html#quick-start-the-published-image","x":"In plain words. From 3.0, every release comes with a ready-made image on GitHub Container Registry, for Intel/AMD and ARM machines. Pull it, give it a token, start it: there is nothing to build. Two Compose files in `infra/docker` do this for you: the proxy alone, or the proxy with PostgreSQL and Redis. Technical. `ghc","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"Step 1: build the image","u":"docker.html#step-1-build-the-image","x":"To build the image yourself (any release; required before 3.0): You need Docker with Buildx. Get the samples and build, naming a published release: Shell For several architectures, pushed to your own registry: Shell `MAPLE_VERSION` is required, so an image is always one known release. If a checksum or the signature doe","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"With Podman","u":"docker.html#with-podman","x":"The Compose files also run with Podman (`podman compose`, or Docker Compose pointed at Podman's socket). Two differences: Podman runs health checks through systemd, so a Podman without it (a static build) never reports a service `healthy`, and a service waiting for one does not start; and `--build` has no BuildKit buil","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"Step 2: run one container","u":"docker.html#step-2-run-one-container","x":"Make a token for your tools, then start it with the sample `compose.yaml`: Shell Each line of `secrets/proxy_tokens` is `label=value`. The proxy adds each one as an access token with the `proxy` scope at start (once: a token already there is left as it is). The proxy runs as user 65532 in the container, so the files mu","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"What a start looks like","u":"docker.html#what-a-start-looks-like","x":"These lines are from a real start of the binary in a sandbox set up like the container (on other ports; a few lines left out):","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"Settings and providers","u":"docker.html#settings-and-providers","x":"The proxy keeps `config.yaml` on the volume, at `/data/config/maple-harness/config.yaml`, and makes a starter one the first time. Provider keys belong in secrets, never in the image or in `config.yaml`: mount each secret as a file in `/run/secrets/maple/`, named after the secret with `/` written as `.`: the key of the ","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"Health checks and stopping","u":"docker.html#health-checks-and-stopping","x":"`GET /healthz` answers `ok` while the proxy runs; `GET /readyz` answers `ok` once providers are loaded (and, with shared storage, while PostgreSQL and Redis answer, Redis as the primary), and `not ready` otherwise. They say nothing more: no version, no reason. They answer without a token, only for `GET`, behind the sam","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"Logs","u":"docker.html#logs","x":"In the container, logs are JSON on standard output, one object per line: Field What it is `time` When, in UTC (RFC 3339, milliseconds) `level` `DEBUG`, `INFO`, `WARN` or `ERROR` `msg` What happened `component` `proxy`, `access` (one line per request), `usage`, `events`, `health`, `chain` or `mcp-hub` `storage`, `proxy_","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"Images and memory","u":"docker.html#images-and-memory","x":"Resizing an image (`image_resize_tool`, `image_resize_user`) decodes it in memory, about width × height × 4 bytes, and as much again for the result. In a container the proxy keeps this inside a share of the container's memory limit, so a burst of large screenshots cannot run it out of memory: `images.resize_memory` (or","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"Search by meaning","u":"docker.html#search-by-meaning","x":"The built-in models for search by meaning are downloaded the way they always are (the setup page, or `docker exec maple-proxy maple-proxy embeddings install`), into `/data/config/maple-harness/embeddings/` on the volume, so a download survives restarts and upgrades. `MAPLE_EMBEDDINGS_DIR` puts them on a volume of their","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"Several replicas","u":"docker.html#several-replicas","x":"When one proxy is not enough, or a restart must not interrupt anyone, run three replicas behind a load balancer (Caddy), sharing PostgreSQL and Redis. The Kubernetes page explains what they share and how they behave; the same applies here. There are three samples: Sample Settings Use it when `compose.ha-shared.yaml` ( ","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"Run several replicas: how settings reach every replica","u":"docker.html#run-several-replicas-how-settings-reach-every-replica","x":"In plain words. The three proxies must run the same settings and accept the same tokens. Both samples keep them in PostgreSQL (`MAPLE_SETTINGS_SOURCE`): change a setting on any proxy's setup page and all three run it within seconds. Every replica runs the same configuration: one that cannot confirm it is current report","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"The MCP hub in a container","u":"docker.html#the-mcp-hub-in-a-container","x":"The image has no sandbox, so the MCP hub refuses to start a stdio server (a program it would run) there. Run each MCP server as a container of its own that serves MCP over HTTP, and add it to the hub by URL; see The MCP hub on the Kubernetes page for the pattern and the `mcp_hub.allow_uncontained_stdio` setting that ov","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"Upgrading and backups","u":"docker.html#upgrading-and-backups","x":"One container: pull (or build) the new release's image and start it on the same volume. Back up the volume (stop the container, or copy `/data` while it runs: the files are written whole). Several replicas: see Upgrades and Backups . Back up PostgreSQL (it holds the settings too); Redis holds nothing that must survive.","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"See also","u":"docker.html#see-also","x":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes Kustomize samples and a Helm chart, from one pod to several replicas. Which setup do I need? One laptop, a team, or a company: a guide to choosing. Security What Maple protects, by default and when you widen it. Proxy settings: Containers and replicas The keys and environment variables a con","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"Monthly dollar budgets","u":"dollar-budgets.html","x":"Give an Anthropic API account a monthly allowance in dollars, with the day it resets, and decide what Maple does when it is spent: warn you, move requests to a fallback, or refuse them. People and teams on a shared proxy can have dollar caps too. Every dollar figure Maple counts itself is an estimate. Maple prices the ","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"In plain words","u":"dollar-budgets.html#in-plain-words","x":"Anthropic doesn't tell an app how much money an API key has left. So if you give Maple a key with a fixed amount behind it (the monthly API credits of a Claude Max or Team plan, or a limit you set yourself), Maple can't know when it runs out until Anthropic starts refusing requests. A dollar budget fixes that from Mapl","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"On the setup page","u":"dollar-budgets.html#on-the-setup-page","x":"Costs & budgets , the Monthly dollar budgets card. The card is shown when the proxy has an account it can price in dollars: Anthropic, Amazon Bedrock, Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure (Claude), and xAI. Any other account is listed once, at the bottom, as \"Not priced in dollars here\": its requests count $0 against a dollar","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"The settings","u":"dollar-budgets.html#the-settings","x":"YAML Key Default What it does `max_usd_per_month` none The month's allowance, in estimated dollars. It counts on its own: `estimate_cost` does not have to be on (that setting only decides whether `/cost` shows a dollar figure) `month_starts_on_day` `1` The day of the month the budget resets, at 00:00 UTC. 1 to 31; a da","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"What counts","u":"dollar-budgets.html#what-counts","x":"Every request Maple sends to the account, priced from the token counts in Anthropic's answer: input, output (thinking included, as Anthropic bills it), cache reads, and cache writes at their 5-minute or 1-hour price. The long-prompt price of Claude Haiku 5.5, US-only inference, fast mode, the batch tier and web searche","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"At 80% and 100%","u":"dollar-budgets.html#at-80-and-100","x":"With budget alerts on (the default once a budget exists), the proxy warns once at 80% and once at 100% of the month, with \"estimated\" and the reset date in the words, for example: anthropic: about $80.40 of $100.00 used this month (estimated at list prices, through this proxy only); resets around 14 Nov The alerts end ","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"Forecast","u":"dollar-budgets.html#forecast","x":"Shell prints the month's estimated spend so far and, once there are at least three days of usage, either At this rate, it runs out around 22 Nov (estimated, range 20-24 Nov, from 7 days of your usage through this proxy). or, when it lasts to the reset, At this rate, it lasts until it resets around 14 Dec, with about $3","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"When Anthropic says the credit is gone","u":"dollar-budgets.html#when-anthropic-says-the-credit-is-gone","x":"Anthropic answers \"credit balance is too low\" when the organisation has no credit left. Without a dollar budget, Maple marks the account as out of credit for an hour, tries its fallbacks meanwhile, and then asks again. With a dollar budget on that account, the mark lasts until the budget's reset day instead, since mont","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"People and teams","u":"dollar-budgets.html#people-and-teams","x":"On a shared proxy, a person or a team can have a dollar cap beside their AI-unit budget. On the setup page, open People & teams , then Edit under the person (or the team, under Teams on the same tab): the Dollar cap (estimated) row has a day and a month , beside Budget (AI units) ; `0` is no cap. Press Save budget . Th","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"Metrics","u":"dollar-budgets.html#metrics","x":"With metrics on, `maple_budget_used_usd_estimated` and `maple_budget_limit_usd` (label `provider`) give each account's month, and with `metrics.per_person` the people's and teams' dollars too. See Metrics and telemetry .","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"Check against Anthropic's bill","u":"dollar-budgets.html#check-against-anthropics-bill","x":"Maple's dollar figures are its own estimate. For an Anthropic API account, Maple can also read what Anthropic itself billed and show the two side by side, so you can see how close the estimate is and what else is spending from the same organisation.","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"In plain words","u":"dollar-budgets.html#in-plain-words-1","x":"Anthropic keeps a cost report for every organisation: what each day cost, model by model, in each workspace (the Console's Cost page shows it). Maple can read that report once a day, and whenever you press Check now , and put it next to its own estimate: Anthropic's bill , labelled measured by Anthropic : what Anthropi","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"Which key reads the bill","u":"dollar-budgets.html#which-key-reads-the-bill","x":"Anthropic serves the cost report from its Usage & Cost API, which answers only some keys: Key Reads the bill? An API key limited to one workspace (the usual kind, and the one Maple recommends for model requests) No An API key that is not limited to one workspace (organization-scoped) Yes An Admin API key (`sk-ant-admin","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"What the card shows","u":"dollar-budgets.html#what-the-card-shows","x":"For each Anthropic account: when the bill was last read, with which key, and up to when it is complete (the day it was read is partial); with a dollar budget : This month so far , which is Anthropic's bill up to the last report plus Maple's estimate since then, said in those words with both labels, and a bar against th","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"The settings","u":"dollar-budgets.html#the-settings-1","x":"YAML Key What it does `usage_admin_key` Optional, on a `type: anthropic` provider. A key used only to read the account's cost report. It must be exactly `${secret:provider/<id>/usage_admin_key}`: the setup page writes it. With secrets kept elsewhere (a mounted Kubernetes Secret, say), give the secret that name there an","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"What it does and does not do","u":"dollar-budgets.html#what-it-does-and-does-not-do","x":"The bill is read at most once a day by itself (after 00:30 UTC, for the day before), and when you press Check now (once a minute at most). In storage mode `central`, one replica reads it and every replica shows it. What is read and kept is the month's figures per day, workspace and model, in the configuration folder (o","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"The Max and Team API credits","u":"dollar-budgets.html#the-max-and-team-api-credits","x":"These are Anthropic's terms, summarised from their page API credits for Max and Team plans , as read on 2026-10-11; check it for the current details. Plan Monthly API credits Max 5x $100 Max 20x $200 Team $20 per Standard seat and $100 per Premium seat, pooled into one balance, up to $500 You claim them by linking your","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"Use your Max or Team credits with Maple","u":"dollar-budgets.html#use-your-max-or-team-credits-with-maple","x":"Link your plan to a Console organisation , as Anthropic's page describes. Note the day your plan bills: that is when the credits arrive. Make a workspace for Maple in that organisation, and give it its own spend limit at or under your credit amount (for example $100 for Max 5x). Anthropic enforces this limit exactly; M","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"Related","u":"dollar-budgets.html#related","x":"Proxy settings: Budgets Daily request and AI-unit limits, and monthly dollar allowances, per provider. Alerts and forecasts A warning before a budget runs out, and when it would at today's pace. Teams: Budgets Budgets for people and teams on a shared proxy. What /cost tells you The session's own bill, request by reques","m":"both"},{"p":"Several edits, patches and renames","h":"Several edits, patches and renames","u":"editing-tools.html","x":"Five tools help the agent change code in bigger steps than one replacement at a time, and find its way around a project the way your editor does: `multi_edit` (several replacements in one file), `apply_patch` (one patch over several files, for the models trained on that format), and three language-server tools, `lsp_re","m":"single"},{"p":"Several edits, patches and renames","h":"In plain words","u":"editing-tools.html#in-plain-words","x":"What they are. Ways for the agent to make a whole change in one go: several edits to a file, a patch over several files, or a rename the language server works out. And two ways to look things up: every place a name is used, and every name a file declares. Why you want them. One question for one change instead of five, ","m":"single"},{"p":"Several edits, patches and renames","h":"Several edits in one file: `multi_edit`","u":"editing-tools.html#several-edits-in-one-file-multi_edit","x":"The agent gives a file and a list of replacements. They are made in order, each against the file as the ones before it left it, so a later edit can change text an earlier one wrote. Each piece of text to replace must be found, and exactly once unless that edit says \"every occurrence\". If one is missing or appears twice","m":"single"},{"p":"Several edits, patches and renames","h":"Patches: `apply_patch`","u":"editing-tools.html#patches-apply_patch","x":"OpenAI's GPT and Codex models are trained to edit code with patches in a format of their own (the \"V4A\" format, the one Codex uses). When one of them is the model, Maple offers `apply_patch` so it can work the way it was taught: Who gets it. `harness.tools.apply_patch` decides: `auto` (the default) offers it to the mod","m":"single"},{"p":"Several edits, patches and renames","h":"Looking things up: `lsp_references` and `lsp_symbols`","u":"editing-tools.html#looking-things-up-lsp_references-and-lsp_symbols","x":"`lsp_references` takes a file, a line and a column (1-based, as `read_file` numbers lines) and lists every place that symbol is used, with the line of code at each, sorted by file and line. Add `include_declaration: false` to leave out where it is declared. `lsp_symbols` with a `path` lists what the file declares as an","m":"single"},{"p":"Several edits, patches and renames","h":"Renames: `lsp_rename`","u":"editing-tools.html#renames-lsp_rename","x":"The agent names the symbol (a file, line and column) and the new name. The language server works out every edit the rename needs, across files, before you are asked. Then: You see the whole rename as one patch: every file, every changed line. Your rules are matched against every file in it, so an `edit_file` deny rule ","m":"single"},{"p":"Several edits, patches and renames","h":"Approvals and rules","u":"editing-tools.html#approvals-and-rules","x":"Your `edit_file` rules cover them. With no rule of its own, `multi_edit`, `apply_patch` and `lsp_rename` follow your rules for `edit_file` (and `apply_patch`, which creates files, your rules for `write_file` too: the stricter of the two). A rule written for the tool itself decides first, but a `deny` rule for `edit_fil","m":"single"},{"p":"Several edits, patches and renames","h":"Settings","u":"editing-tools.html#settings","x":"Setting Default What it does `harness.tools.apply_patch` `auto` `auto`: `apply_patch` is offered to the models trained on its format. `on`: to every model. `off`: never Change it with `/settings` (under Tools) or in `config.yaml`: YAML For the curious","m":"single"},{"p":"Several edits, patches and renames","h":"Technical details","u":"editing-tools.html#technical-details","x":"Positions are 1-based lines and columns, the same numbering `read_file` shows. Language servers count columns in UTF-16 code units; the edits they send back are applied in those units, so a line with emoji or accented letters is changed in the right place. Patch matching. Each `@@` section is looked for after the one b","m":"single"},{"p":"Several edits, patches and renames","h":"See also","u":"editing-tools.html#see-also","x":"Language servers : installing one for your language. Approvals nobody answers : what happens when nobody can say yes. Plan mode : what the agent may use while planning. Configuration : every setting.","m":"single"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"Connect a model server","u":"endpoints.html","x":"Any server that speaks the OpenAI API can be a provider: Ollama, LM Studio, oMLX, Lemonade, Unsloth Studio, vLLM, llama.cpp, an MLX server, or another. Add one from the setup page in three clicks or by editing the configuration file, and every tool that uses the proxy can use its models, with the proxy's fallbacks, bud","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"In plain words","u":"endpoints.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. A model server is a program on your computer or network that runs models itself: Ollama, LM Studio, oMLX, Lemonade, Unsloth, vLLM, llama.cpp and others. Why you want it. Your own models cost nothing per request, and your code never leaves your machine. The clicks. On the setup page, Providers , Find local m","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"From the setup page","u":"endpoints.html#from-the-setup-page","x":"Open `http://127.0.0.1:11539`, go to Providers and choose + Add provider . Pick the type. The usual address for that server is filled in. Press Detect models to list what the server has loaded, then save. It applies immediately. No restart is needed.","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"Find the servers already running","u":"endpoints.html#find-the-servers-already-running","x":"If a model server is already running on this computer, the proxy can find it and add it for you. On the setup page: open Providers and press Find local models . Each server found is listed with its models. Press Add it to add one. On a first run: the Get started box shows any server that is running and not added yet. F","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"By hand","u":"endpoints.html#by-hand","x":"Add a provider to `~/.config/maple-harness/config.yaml`: YAML Field Meaning `id` A name for this provider `type` What kind of server it is (table below) `base_url` The server's address including `/v1` (Lemonade uses `/api/v1`) `optional: true` If the server is not running, skip it instead of failing to start. Good for ","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"Types and their usual addresses","u":"endpoints.html#types-and-their-usual-addresses","x":"`type` Server Address `ollama` Ollama `http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1` `lmstudio` LM Studio `http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1` `omlx` oMLX (Apple silicon) `http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1` `lemonade` Lemonade Server (AMD) `http://127.0.0.1:13305/api/v1` `unsloth` Unsloth Studio (expects an API key) `http://127.0.0.1:8888/v1` `openrouter` ","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"API keys","u":"endpoints.html#api-keys","x":"If a server needs a key, do not paste it into the file. Refer to it: YAML A key you type on the setup page is stored in the keyring for you.","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"Use your models from other tools","u":"endpoints.html#use-your-models-from-other-tools","x":"Any tool that speaks the OpenAI or Anthropic API can use the proxy. For what a tool gains from it (every model the proxy serves, fallbacks, budgets, the usage log and prompt caching) and what stays in Maple Harness, see What the proxy adds for other tools . Point it at the proxy's addresses: API Address Path OpenAI-com","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"Claude Code","u":"endpoints.html#claude-code","x":"Claude Code speaks the Anthropic API. Set these in your shell, or in the `env` block of `~/.claude/settings.json`: Shell Without `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_HAIKU_MODEL`, Claude Code asks the proxy for its own small model by name for background work, which fails unless the proxy serves that name. The Anthropic-compatible addres","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"OpenCode","u":"endpoints.html#opencode","x":"Add a provider to `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json`: JSON Each key under `models` is a name the proxy lists. Pick one with `/models`, or `opencode run -m maple/local \"...\"`.","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"pi","u":"endpoints.html#pi","x":"Add a provider to `~/.pi/agent/models.json`: JSON pi needs the `apiKey` to show the models; any value works on this machine. Pick a model with `/model`, or `pi --model maple/local`.","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"GitHub Copilot CLI","u":"endpoints.html#github-copilot-cli","x":"Copilot CLI's bring-your-own-model mode is set with environment variables, and needs no GitHub sign-in: Shell Leave `COPILOT_PROVIDER_TYPE` and `COPILOT_PROVIDER_WIRE_API` at their defaults (`openai` and `completions`). For a proxy on another machine, put the access token in `COPILOT_PROVIDER_API_KEY`.","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"Hermes","u":"endpoints.html#hermes","x":"Hermes Agent (Nous Research) reads its model from `~/.hermes/config.yaml` (or `$HERMES_HOME/config.yaml`). Point a custom endpoint at the proxy: YAML `hermes model` (choose Custom endpoint ) writes the same settings for you. Hermes needs a model with at least 64,000 tokens of context. For a proxy on another machine, pu","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"Codex CLI","u":"endpoints.html#codex-cli","x":"Codex speaks the OpenAI Responses API, which the proxy serves at the same OpenAI-compatible address. Add a provider to `~/.codex/config.toml` (or `$CODEX_HOME/config.toml`): TOML `model` is a name the proxy lists. Codex warns that it has no metadata for a name it does not know, and then uses its defaults; it works. For","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"Copilot Fusion in these tools","u":"endpoints.html#copilot-fusion-in-these-tools","x":"Every tool above can use Copilot Fusion: give it `github-copilot/fusion`, `github-copilot/fusion-high` or `github-copilot/fusion-max` as the model (in OpenCode and pi, list them under `models` like any other name, then pick `maple/github-copilot/fusion-high`; in Claude Code, `ANTHROPIC_MODEL=github-copilot/fusion-high`","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"Check it","u":"endpoints.html#check-it","x":"Shell If Detect models finds nothing, the server is usually not running or the address is missing its `/v1`. If Find local models finds nothing: The server is not running, or it is still loading a model and does not answer within about a second and a half. Try again. It listens on a port other than its usual one, or on","m":"both"},{"p":"Your first session","h":"Your first session","u":"getting-started.html","x":"From installed to your first change in three steps: start the proxy, sign in to an account (or add a model server of your own), and run `maple-harness` in your project. The short version","m":"single"},{"p":"Your first session","h":"In plain words","u":"getting-started.html#in-plain-words","x":"Three steps: start the proxy (or let the agent start it), sign in to an account or add a model server on the setup page, then run `maple-harness` in your project. The clicks. Open `http://127.0.0.1:11539`. On a first run the Overview shows Get started : sign in to Anthropic or GitHub Copilot there, or add a model serve","m":"single"},{"p":"Your first session","h":"1. Start the proxy","u":"getting-started.html#1-start-the-proxy","x":"Shell The proxy is the one program that talks to models and keeps your sign-ins. The agent starts it for you if nothing is listening, so you can skip this step, but running it yourself lets you watch what it does. On first run it looks for Ollama and Apple's on-device model and writes a working starter configuration. I","m":"single"},{"p":"Your first session","h":"2. Sign in or add a model","u":"getting-started.html#2-sign-in-or-add-a-model","x":"Open `http://127.0.0.1:11539` and either sign in to GitHub Copilot, save an Anthropic API key, or add a local model server. If a model server is already running on this computer, the page offers to add it (see Find the servers already running ). Or do it from the terminal: Shell Details for each are in Sign in and Conn","m":"single"},{"p":"Your first session","h":"3. Start the agent","u":"getting-started.html#3-start-the-agent","x":"Go to a project directory and run: Shell The start screen in a project called acme-api. Type in the box at the bottom; `/help` lists every command. Type what you want done. Useful first commands: Command What it does `/model` Choose a model from the list, or `/model auto` to let the agent choose per message `/help` Lis","m":"single"},{"p":"Your first session","h":"Where things are kept","u":"getting-started.html#where-things-are-kept","x":"Everything lives in `~/.config/maple-harness/` (`%APPDATA%\\maple-harness` on Windows): the configuration file `config.yaml`, saved sessions, memory notes, and your sign-ins. Sign-ins are kept in your system keyring where there is one, not in the configuration file.","m":"single"},{"p":"Your first session","h":"Next","u":"getting-started.html#next","x":"Commands and keys Everything you can type in a session, every key, and the shell commands. Choose a model Which model for which work, and how `auto` picks one. Settings by goal The settings that serve efficiency, efficacy and effectiveness. Harness settings Every `harness.*` setting, with its default and its trade-off.","m":"single"},{"p":"Glossary","h":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html","x":"The words these pages use, in alphabetical order, each with the page that covers it.","m":"both"},{"p":"Glossary","h":"A","u":"glossary.html#a","x":"A2A. The Agent2Agent protocol, a common language agents use to hand each other tasks. `maple-harness a2a serve` takes tasks from other agents, and the proxy's A2A gateway puts several agents behind one address. See Agents that call agents (A2A) . Access token. A secret that lets a device or another machine use what run","m":"both"},{"p":"Glossary","h":"B","u":"glossary.html#b","x":"Background agent. A subagent that keeps working while you and the main agent move on, even after the turn ends; its report lands in the conversation on its own. See Background agents .","m":"both"},{"p":"Glossary","h":"E","u":"glossary.html#e","x":"Efficient, efficacious, effective. The three goals every setting is tagged with in the reference pages. Efficient: fewer tokens or AI units for the same work. Efficacious: the work stays as good, and the safe choice is the default. Effective: whole tasks succeed. See Settings by goal .","m":"both"},{"p":"Glossary","h":"F","u":"glossary.html#f","x":"Fallback. What the proxy tries when a model cannot answer because its account is out of quota or credit, the backend is overloaded, or it is unreachable. `model_fallbacks` lists other models to try; `provider_fallbacks` lists other accounts. It happens only before any output is sent, and only if you add a rule. See Fal","m":"both"},{"p":"Glossary","h":"G","u":"glossary.html#g","x":"Gateway. The one address on your computer that phones and other computers connect to for remote control, on port 11541 by default. `/remote` starts it and it stops by itself after ten idle minutes; `maple-harness gateway start` or a background service keeps it running. See Control it from your phone and Keep it running","m":"both"},{"p":"Glossary","h":"H","u":"glossary.html#h","x":"Heavy run. A test suite or a build. Heavy runs take turns across all your sessions (one at a time by default), so two of them never fight over your memory. See Memory caps and heavy runs . Hub. A proxy that other proxies use as a provider: a team's proxy behind which each person's own proxy sits, or a company's behind ","m":"both"},{"p":"Glossary","h":"I","u":"glossary.html#i","x":"Investigation. A check the proxy runs over its usage log on a schedule, such as recurring errors, loops or a cost spike. Each finding says what it rests on. See Investigations .","m":"both"},{"p":"Glossary","h":"L","u":"glossary.html#l","x":"Lineup. The task list the agents share, on screen (`/tasks` or `Alt+T`): who is on which task, what waits for what, and what is done. See The lineup .","m":"both"},{"p":"Glossary","h":"M","u":"glossary.html#m","x":"MCP. The Model Context Protocol, a common way for tools to offer a model extra tools. The agent can use MCP servers you configure, and `maple-harness mcp serve` offers this project's notes and code map to other tools. See Give other tools this project's knowledge . MCP hub. The proxy running your MCP servers once and o","m":"both"},{"p":"Glossary","h":"O","u":"glossary.html#o","x":"Organization. A group of teams on a shared proxy, with one budget and model list over all of them. See Access control .","m":"both"},{"p":"Glossary","h":"P","u":"glossary.html#p","x":"Pairing. How a phone or another computer gets its own access token: `/remote pair` or `maple-harness gateway pair` shows a short code and a QR code that work once and expire in five minutes. See Control it from your phone . Provider. A place models come from, as the proxy knows it: GitHub Copilot, Anthropic, or a model","m":"both"},{"p":"Glossary","h":"R","u":"glossary.html#r","x":"Remote page. The web page a paired phone or browser opens from the gateway: it lists your shared sessions, shows each one as it runs, lets you send messages and approve tool calls, and starts sessions in folders you have allowed. See Control it from your phone . RPC. `maple-harness --rpc`, which lets another program dr","m":"both"},{"p":"Glossary","h":"S","u":"glossary.html#s","x":"Sandbox. A limit on the commands the model runs in the shell: they can write only in your project and a few build caches, cannot see your logins and keys, and run with secret-looking variables removed. Built in on macOS, bubblewrap on Linux, not available on Windows. See The shell sandbox . Semantic cache. The proxy an","m":"both"},{"p":"Glossary","h":"U","u":"glossary.html#u","x":"Usage log. A file the proxy writes with one line of counts per request (model, tokens, cache figures, AI units, timing), never prompts or answers. It is on by default, one file per UTC day in the `usage` folder of the Maple Harness config folder, and daily files older than 90 days are deleted. See Your usage data .","m":"both"},{"p":"Guardrails","h":"Guardrails","u":"guardrails.html","x":"Guardrails check what goes to a model and what comes back, at the proxy. They can stop a request that holds an API key, mask an email address before it leaves your network, refuse banned words, or flag a tool result that tries to give the model new instructions. You choose what each one checks, what it does when it fin","m":"both"},{"p":"Guardrails","h":"In plain words","u":"guardrails.html#in-plain-words","x":"A guardrail is a rule the proxy applies to every request it serves (or only to some people, teams, models or tagged requests). It looks at the text, and when it finds what it is looking for it does one of three things: block : the request is refused with a clear message, and never reaches the model. For an answer, the ","m":"both"},{"p":"Guardrails","h":"What each type checks","u":"guardrails.html#what-each-type-checks","x":"Type Checks Can mask `secrets` API keys and credentials in known shapes: AWS access keys, GitHub, Anthropic, OpenAI, Slack, Google and Stripe keys, private keys, JSON Web Tokens, and Maple's own access tokens yes `pii` Personal data: email addresses, phone numbers, payment card numbers and IBANs (only numbers whose che","m":"both"},{"p":"Guardrails","h":"When guardrails run","u":"guardrails.html#when-guardrails-run","x":"Phase What is checked `pre_call` The request, before it is sent: its system prompt, messages, tool results and the tool calls in its history `post_call` The answer, before the caller gets it: its text and the arguments of the tools it calls `mcp_call` A tool call made through the MCP hub : its arguments, before the too","m":"both"},{"p":"Guardrails","h":"Streamed answers","u":"guardrails.html#streamed-answers","x":"A streamed answer is checked one of two ways (`stream`): `buffered`, the default: the proxy reads the whole answer, checks it, then sends it (masked where a mask applies). Block and mask both work, and a blocked answer is an ordinary error the client understands. The client sees nothing until the answer is complete. `c","m":"both"},{"p":"Guardrails","h":"Who a guardrail runs for","u":"guardrails.html#who-a-guardrail-runs-for","x":"By default a guardrail runs on every request. `applies_to` narrows it: `people` and `teams`: the request's person is one of `people`, or their team one of `teams` (see People and teams ). `models`: the model name the request asks for matches one of these names or patterns (`gpt-*`, `copilot/*`). It is the name as the c","m":"both"},{"p":"Guardrails","h":"What a client sees","u":"guardrails.html#what-a-client-sees","x":"A blocked request or answer is refused with HTTP 400. On the OpenAI-compatible address the error's type is `guardrail_blocked`; on the Anthropic-compatible address it is an `invalid_request_error`. The message names the guardrail and what kind of thing it found, and, for a service that says why, its reason. Clients do ","m":"both"},{"p":"Guardrails","h":"Your own guardrail service","u":"guardrails.html#your-own-guardrail-service","x":"The `webhook` type calls an address you give with the request's or answer's text, in the shape LiteLLM's generic guardrail API uses, so a service written for LiteLLM (for example one in front of Presidio or Lakera) answers Maple as it is. The proxy sends a JSON `POST`: JSON `input_type` is `request` for what goes to a ","m":"both"},{"p":"Guardrails","h":"The settings","u":"guardrails.html#the-settings","x":"Guardrails live under `guardrails:` in `config.yaml`. The setup page's card writes the same entries. YAML Key Default What it does `name` required How requests, the header and the card name it: letters, digits and `. _ -` `type` required One of the types above `action` `block` `block`, `mask` or `log_only`. `mask` only","m":"both"},{"p":"Guardrails","h":"Privacy","u":"guardrails.html#privacy","x":"Decisions hold labels only: the guardrail, the phase, the outcome, what kind of thing was found, the model and the person. Never the text, what matched, or a service's explanation. Blocks and masks are written to the proxy's audit log the same way, at most one line a minute for each guardrail, outcome and person, with ","m":"both"},{"p":"Guardrails","h":"What guardrails cover","u":"guardrails.html#what-guardrails-cover","x":"What is checked in a request: its text, its tool results, and the arguments of the tool calls in its history. Not checked: images, files and other parts that are not text, and tool definitions. With `messages: latest`, only the turn after the last answer is checked; a caller can write earlier turns itself, so use `all`","m":"both"},{"p":"Guardrails","h":"Not yet verified","u":"guardrails.html#not-yet-verified","x":"The provider guardrails (OpenAI moderation, Azure Content Safety and Prompt Shields, Amazon Bedrock Guardrails, Google Model Armor) and the webhook's compatibility with LiteLLM-shaped services were tested against stand-ins that answer in each service's documented shape, not against the real services. A Google Model Arm","m":"both"},{"p":"Guardrails","h":"See also","u":"guardrails.html#see-also","x":"Access control Who may use which models and routes, from where, and when. Content export and callbacks Send prompts and answers to your tools, masked first. MCP hub Tool calls that guardrails check on the way in and on the way out. Your usage data The usage log, which guardrails never change.","m":"both"},{"p":"Provider health","h":"Provider health","u":"health.html","x":"The proxy watches how each provider and model answers, from the requests that already go through it, and tells you when one is having a bad time. By default it only watches: nothing about where your requests go changes. You can turn on health failover to have a struggling model tried after the other fallbacks you set u","m":"both"},{"p":"Provider health","h":"In plain words","u":"health.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. The proxy watches how each provider and model answers your real requests and marks it healthy, degraded or demoted. Why you want it. You see at once which account or server is failing, and, if you choose, requests can try a struggling model last. The clicks. On the setup page, Health . The card lists each m","m":"both"},{"p":"Provider health","h":"What it shows","u":"health.html#what-it-shows","x":"Each provider and model the proxy has sent requests to gets one of three states: State What it means healthy Answering as it usually does. degraded Failing more than it usually does, or much slower than usual, but not enough to act on. demoted Failing far more than it usually does. With health failover on, it is tried ","m":"both"},{"p":"Provider health","h":"In a terminal","u":"health.html#in-a-terminal","x":"Shell It asks the running proxy on its setup address, like `maple-proxy cache`.","m":"both"},{"p":"Provider health","h":"Clearing a demotion","u":"health.html#clearing-a-demotion","x":"A model's state is kept across a restart, so a model you know is fine again (you fixed its server, its quota was renewed) can stay demoted until a real request tests it. To clear it now: Shell or press Reset beside it on the setup page's Health card ( Reset all clears every model). The model is healthy again with a fre","m":"both"},{"p":"Provider health","h":"On a response","u":"health.html#on-a-response","x":"When a model that health would demote answers a request, the response says so in `X-Maple-Router`: Nothing is added while a model is healthy or degraded.","m":"both"},{"p":"Provider health","h":"In the events log","u":"health.html#in-the-events-log","x":"Every change of state is written to the events log as a `health.change` line: the provider and model, the new state, the previous one and why. With health failover on, `health.demote` and `health.restore` say when a model was moved behind its fallbacks and when it went back.","m":"both"},{"p":"Provider health","h":"How a state is decided","u":"health.html#how-a-state-is-decided","x":"Against the model's own usual figures. A local model is slow by nature, and a busy hosted one fails now and then on a good day, so each model is compared with its own earlier requests, not with a fixed number. Until a model has enough history, a modest usual error rate is assumed, and speed is not judged at all. Never ","m":"both"},{"p":"Provider health","h":"Health failover","u":"health.html#health-failover","x":"Off by default. Turn it on with the Health failover switch in the setup page's Proxy settings, Fallbacks, or in `config.yaml`: YAML It applies to the next request, with no restart. With it on, a demoted model is tried after the other models and accounts you already listed for that request in `model_fallbacks` and `prov","m":"both"},{"p":"Provider health","h":"What it cannot tell you","u":"health.html#what-it-cannot-tell-you","x":"A proxy remembers health across a restart: it saves each model's state and recent figures in `health.json` in the config folder every few minutes and when it stops, so a demoted model is still demoted after a restart and its usual figures are still known. Figures too old to be compared with any more are dropped, as the","m":"both"},{"p":"Provider health","h":"Related","u":"health.html#related","x":"Proxy settings: Fallbacks Where a request goes when its model is down or busy. See and filter your usage The usage log on your own disk: filters, views and Grafana. Investigations Scheduled checks over the usage log: recurring errors, loops, spikes and slow providers. Troubleshooting Start with the checker, then the us","m":"both"},{"p":"Use it with herdr","h":"Use it with herdr","u":"herdr.html","x":"herdr hosts terminal sessions for coding agents and shows, for each pane, whether the agent in it is working, waiting for you, or idle. Maple Harness works with it without any setup. The short version","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with herdr","h":"In plain words","u":"herdr.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. herdr shows, for each terminal pane, whether the agent in it is working, waiting for you, or idle. Maple also tells herdr when it is done (with the answer's first line), notifies you when you are looking at another pane, and can talk to the other agents on your machine: send them messages, read what they sa","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with herdr","h":"What you need to do","u":"herdr.html#what-you-need-to-do","x":"Nothing. Start `maple-harness` (or `mpl`) inside a herdr pane and the pane labels itself `maple-harness` and shows its state as it changes: State What it means working a turn is running blocked it is waiting for you to approve a tool call idle it is waiting for your next message The harness tells herdr itself, over her","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with herdr","h":"Talking to other agents","u":"herdr.html#talking-to-other-agents","x":"Maple Harness sessions on the same machine, and the agents in your herdr panes (Claude Code, Codex and others), can talk to each other. From a Maple session. The agent has four tools for this. Like any tool, each asks before it runs, unless you allow it or run in auto mode: Tool What it does `agents_list` the other Map","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with herdr","h":"For an orchestrator: the control socket","u":"herdr.html#for-an-orchestrator-the-control-socket","x":"In plain words: What it is. Every interactive Maple session on your machine listens on a small local socket. A program that coordinates several agents (an orchestrator, a script, an editor plugin) can find the sessions, send one a message, read its state and last answer, and (where you allowed it) answer its approvals ","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with herdr","h":"Panes, tabs and agents beside the session","u":"herdr.html#panes-tabs-and-agents-beside-the-session","x":"Inside herdr the agent can open terminals beside it, to run a server, a watcher or a long build while it keeps working, and start other agents there. In tmux, WezTerm and kitty the same tools work ( Use it with tmux ). Tool What it does Asks you `pane_open` a split to the right or below this session, or a new tab, in t","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with herdr","h":"What runs outside the sandbox","u":"herdr.html#what-runs-outside-the-sandbox","x":"The agent's own commands (`bash`) run in its sandbox. A pane is an ordinary terminal of yours: what runs in it runs as you, with no sandbox. That is why `pane_run` and `agent_start` ask you every time, show you the exact command line, and are refused when your `bash` deny rules refuse that command. Runs outside the san","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with herdr","h":"If the pane does not pick it up","u":"herdr.html#if-the-pane-does-not-pick-it-up","x":"Check that you started the harness from a shell inside the herdr pane (not a separate terminal window). If herdr uses a non-default socket, make sure `HERDR_SOCKET_PATH` points at it in the environment the harness inherits. By default it looks in `~/.config/herdr/herdr.sock`. Run `maple-harness version` to confirm you ","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with herdr","h":"The terminal signals, with or without herdr","u":"herdr.html#the-terminal-signals-with-or-without-herdr","x":"Independently of herdr, the harness sets the window title and the terminal's progress indicator on every change of state, and sends a notification when a turn finishes or needs a decision. Terminals that do not support one of these ignore it. State Window title Progress indicator idle `● MapleHarness` cleared working `","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with herdr","h":"The detection manifest (advanced)","u":"herdr.html#the-detection-manifest-advanced","x":"herdr can also classify a pane from a detection manifest. Most people do not need this, because the harness reports its own state. If you want the manifest anyway, `maple-harness herdr-manifest` prints it, and `maple-harness herdr-manifest --id <name>` writes a different agent id into it. Whether your herdr version loa","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with herdr","h":"Turning the installer's herdr step off","u":"herdr.html#turning-the-installers-herdr-step-off","x":"The installer needs to install nothing for herdr. `--no-herdr` is accepted and ignored, so older install commands keep working.","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with herdr","h":"See also","u":"herdr.html#see-also","x":"Harness settings: Notifications Terminal notifications and their settings. Commands you run from a shell Including `herdr-manifest`. Use it with tmux Panes and windows beside the agent, in tmux. Background agents Helpers that keep working after the turn, and report back on their own.","m":"single"},{"p":"Overview","h":"Overview","u":"index.html","x":"Maple Harness is a coding agent for GitHub Copilot's models, Claude and models on your own machine: it reads your project, edits files and runs commands, asks before it changes anything, and shows you what every request cost. It comes in three parts: The agent , `maple-harness`: the program you work with in a terminal.","m":"single"},{"p":"Overview","h":"In plain words","u":"index.html#in-plain-words","x":"The agent is where you work: type what you want done in a terminal, and it reads, edits and runs, asking first. The proxy holds your accounts and models. Its setup page, at `http://127.0.0.1:11539`, is where you sign in and change settings, with nothing to edit by hand. Your phone can follow a session and approve what ","m":"single"},{"p":"Overview","h":"Start here","u":"index.html#start-here","x":"Install the programs. Run Your first session . Sign in to GitHub Copilot or Claude, or Connect a model server such as Ollama or LM Studio. Choose a model , or let the agent choose for each message.","m":"single"},{"p":"Overview","h":"Get started","u":"index.html#get-started","x":"Installing, signing in and connecting models. What's new in 3.0 The 3.0 tour, area by area. What's inside Maple What Maple does for you behind the scenes, part by part. Install One line on macOS, Linux or Windows, and how to update. Upgrading to 3.0 What keeps working, what behaves differently, and how to go back. Whic","m":"single"},{"p":"Overview","h":"Guides","u":"index.html#guides","x":"How each part works, and how to use it with other tools. The agent Subagents Helpers the agent hands a job to, the lineup they share, and your own. Background agents Helpers that keep working after the turn, and report back on their own. Questions the agent asks you Short multiple-choice questions when a decision is yo","m":"single"},{"p":"Overview","h":"Cost and usage","u":"index.html#cost-and-usage","x":"Where the spend goes, how to keep it down, and how to check any claim on your own tasks. See and reduce what a task costs Why long tasks cost what they do, and the settings that help. Start here. What /cost tells you The session's own bill, request by request, and what to do about each flag. Settings by goal The settin","m":"single"},{"p":"Overview","h":"Reference","u":"index.html#reference","x":"Every command, key and setting, with what it does, its default and its trade-off. Commands and keys Everything you can type in a session, every key, and the shell commands. Harness settings Every `harness.*` setting, with its default and its trade-off. Proxy settings Every card on the setup page and every key in `confi","m":"single"},{"p":"Overview","h":"Help","u":"index.html#help","x":"When something does not work, and the rest. Troubleshooting Start with the checker, then the usual problems and their fixes. Security What Maple protects, by default and when you widen it. Glossary The words these pages use, each with the page that covers it. Upgrading to 2.0 What kept working, and what changed, from 1","m":"single"},{"p":"Install","h":"Install","u":"install.html","x":"On every system Maple Harness is two programs: `maple-harness`, the agent, and `maple-proxy`, the proxy, which holds your sign-ins and talks to the models. On macOS and Linux the installer also adds the short names `mpl` and `mplp`. On a Mac with Apple silicon there is a third program, `maple-sidecar`, a small helper f","m":"both"},{"p":"Install","h":"In plain words","u":"install.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. One command puts the two programs on your computer: `maple-harness`, the agent you talk to, and `maple-proxy`, which holds your sign-ins and talks to the models. Why this way. It needs no account, it checks every file it downloads against the release's checksums, and it refuses anything that does not match.","m":"both"},{"p":"Install","h":"One line","u":"install.html#one-line","x":"macOS and Linux: Shell Windows (PowerShell): PowerShell No account or token is needed. The installer detects your system, downloads the release, checks every file against the release's `SHA256SUMS`, and refuses to install anything that does not match. From 3.0, each release's `SHA256SUMS` is also attested by the releas","m":"both"},{"p":"Install","h":"Platforms","u":"install.html#platforms","x":"System Agent and proxy Apple on-device model macOS, Apple silicon yes yes (macOS 26 or newer) macOS, Intel yes no Linux, amd64 and arm64 yes no Windows, amd64 yes (less tested) no macOS. The shell sandbox is built in: nothing to install. The on-device model needs a Mac with Apple silicon and macOS 26 or newer; the agen","m":"both"},{"p":"Install","h":"Optional: contain the shell (bubblewrap)","u":"install.html#optional-contain-the-shell-bubblewrap","x":"The agent can run the commands its shell tool executes inside a sandbox. On macOS that is built in. On Linux it needs bubblewrap, which is optional : without it the shell still works, uncontained. System Command Arch, Manjaro `sudo pacman -S bubblewrap` Debian, Ubuntu `sudo apt install bubblewrap` Fedora, RHEL `sudo dn","m":"both"},{"p":"Install","h":"Release files","u":"install.html#release-files","x":"Each release on the releases page has these files. The installer picks the right ones; you only need this list to download by hand. File What it is `maple-harness_linux_amd64`, `maple-harness_linux_arm64`, `maple-harness_darwin_arm64`, `maple-harness_darwin_amd64`, `maple-harness_windows_amd64.exe` The agent `maple-pro","m":"both"},{"p":"Install","h":"Update","u":"install.html#update","x":"Shell","m":"both"},{"p":"Install","h":"Uninstall","u":"install.html#uninstall","x":"Shell Caution The second line deletes your configuration, stored sign-ins, memory notes and saved sessions. Skip it if you plan to reinstall.","m":"both"},{"p":"Install","h":"Next","u":"install.html#next","x":"Your first session Start the proxy, sign in or add a model, and run it in your project. Which setup do I need? One laptop, a team, or a company: a guide to choosing. Keep it running in the background The proxy and the gateway as services that start with your computer. Upgrading to 3.0 What keeps working, what behaves d","m":"both"},{"p":"Investigations","h":"Investigations","u":"investigations.html","x":"Investigations are checks the proxy runs over its own usage log on a schedule. They tell you when the same error keeps coming back, when a conversation keeps sending the same step, when spend jumps well above its usual days, when the cache stops hitting the way it did, and when a provider fails or slows more than it us","m":"both"},{"p":"Investigations","h":"In plain words","u":"investigations.html#in-plain-words","x":"The usage log already holds one line per request: when, which model and account, whether it failed and how, how many tokens, how much came from the cache, what it cost. Reading it by hand to notice a pattern is slow. Investigations do that reading for you, every 15 minutes by default, and keep a short list of findings ","m":"both"},{"p":"Investigations","h":"What is checked","u":"investigations.html#what-is-checked","x":"Each check compares like with like, for one subject at a time: a model of one account, a person, a team, a client. The thresholds below are the defaults; every one can be changed (see The settings ). Recurring errors. Five or more requests to one model failing with the same kind of error (rate limited, quota exhausted,","m":"both"},{"p":"Investigations","h":"Where you see them","u":"investigations.html#where-you-see-them","x":"On the setup page: the Health tab, Findings . A red dot on the tab means a finding is open and nobody has acknowledged it yet. Each finding shows its severity, what, why, what to do, its figures and its evidence. What is checked lists each check with its thresholds and whether it could be judged at the last run. Run no","m":"both"},{"p":"Investigations","h":"Looking at the requests behind a finding","u":"investigations.html#looking-at-the-requests-behind-a-finding","x":"The evidence names requests by their request id, and conversations and sessions by their ids. To see them: Use the usage filter the finding shows, on the Usage tab or with `maple-proxy usage query`. When content export keeps requests in memory (`content_export.keep_local`) and you are on the proxy's own machine, each r","m":"both"},{"p":"Investigations","h":"Who may see them","u":"investigations.html#who-may-see-them","x":"On the proxy's own machine, the page shows every finding. Through single sign-on , an admin sees and marks the same findings (each mark is recorded with who made it), but cannot run the checks, turn them on or off, or open kept content: the request log is only ever on the proxy's own machine. A developer's own view doe","m":"both"},{"p":"Investigations","h":"With several replicas","u":"investigations.html#with-several-replicas","x":"In storage mode central , one replica runs the checks for all, over the whole shared usage log, and keeps the findings in the shared store, so every replica's setup page shows the same list. Run now on another replica says that another replica runs them. Acknowledging or dismissing works on any replica: marks made at t","m":"both"},{"p":"Investigations","h":"The settings","u":"investigations.html#the-settings","x":"YAML Any check can be turned off with `off: true` under its name. A setting left out has its default. A change applies at the next run, without a restart. Key Default What it does `investigations.enabled` `off` Turns the scheduled checks on. The Findings card's Turn on investigations sets it `investigations.every` `15m","m":"both"},{"p":"Investigations","h":"How long findings are kept","u":"investigations.html#how-long-findings-are-kept","x":"A finding stays on the list while it holds. When a run finds it no longer holds, it moves to Ended in the last 7 days and is removed after 7 days. If it comes back, it starts a new occurrence: it is shown again, even if the last one was acknowledged or dismissed, and the card says how many times it has come back. At mo","m":"both"},{"p":"Investigations","h":"What it cannot tell you","u":"investigations.html#what-it-cannot-tell-you","x":"A finding is a pattern in the usage log, not a diagnosis. A spike may be planned work; a repeated turn may be a client that retries on purpose. The basis tells you what was counted so you can judge. The checks see what passed through this proxy only. A provider's own console may show more. A cost in US dollars is an es","m":"both"},{"p":"Investigations","h":"Not yet verified","u":"investigations.html#not-yet-verified","x":"The checks were tested against usage logs written for the tests, not against months of real traffic, so the default thresholds may be too quiet or too loud for your work: change them under Investigation thresholds on the Health tab, or in `config.yaml`. Storage mode central was tested with stand-ins for the shared stor","m":"both"},{"p":"Investigations","h":"Related","u":"investigations.html#related","x":"Alerts and forecasts A warning before a budget runs out, and when it would at today's pace. Provider health Each provider's and model's state, from the requests already going through. Your usage data The usage log's columns, exports and DuckDB views. Content export and callbacks Prompts and answers of the people you na","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","u":"kubernetes.html","x":"This page runs `maple-proxy` on Kubernetes from the samples in the `infra/` folder of the public repository : plain manifests with Kustomize overlays for one replica, or three replicas sharing PostgreSQL and Redis. They are starting points: read them and adapt them to your cluster. From 3.0 the same setups also come as","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"In plain words","u":"kubernetes.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Sample manifests to run the proxy on Kubernetes: one replica, or three sharing PostgreSQL and Redis. Why you want it. A team proxy that survives a node going away, configured the way the rest of your cluster is. What you see on the setup page. With several replicas, settings are kept in PostgreSQL and chang","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"The samples","u":"kubernetes.html#the-samples","x":"Folder What it is `manifest/maple-proxy/` The proxy: Deployment, Service, PodDisruptionBudget, an example ConfigMap and an example Secret `manifest/postgres/`, `manifest/redis/` One PostgreSQL and one Redis, for trying it out . In production use your managed or production services, with backups `manifest/maple-mcp-hub/","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"With Helm","u":"kubernetes.html#with-helm","x":"In plain words. From 3.0 there is a Helm chart with the same choices as the samples: one replica that keeps everything on a volume, several replicas sharing PostgreSQL and Redis, or several replicas sharing one Redis that keeps its data (no PostgreSQL), plus the MCP hub, the usage lake jobs, Prometheus scraping and clo","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Storage mode redis with Helm","u":"kubernetes.html#storage-mode-redis-with-helm","x":"In plain words. Pick `mode: redis` when you want several replicas but do not want to run PostgreSQL, and you can give one Redis (or Valkey) a persistent disk and backups. Everything the replicas share (usage, budgets, settings, access tokens) is kept in that one Redis. PostgreSQL and Redis together (`mode: central`) st","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"One replica","u":"kubernetes.html#one-replica","x":"1. Choose the image. From 3.0, use the published one: `kustomize edit set image maple-proxy=ghcr.io/kinncj/maple-proxy:3.0.0` in step 2, and skip this step. Or build and push your own (see Docker ): Shell 2. Set the image and the secrets. In `config/single`: Shell Edit `manifest/maple-proxy/secret.example.yaml` (or cre","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Several replicas","u":"kubernetes.html#several-replicas","x":"Three replicas: no state of their own, PostgreSQL for what lasts, Redis for what is shared and short-lived.","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"When you need it","u":"kubernetes.html#when-you-need-it","x":"One replica is enough for most teams: the proxy is light, and a restart takes seconds. Run several when a restart or a lost node must not interrupt anyone, or when one replica is not enough. Several replicas need PostgreSQL and Redis (`storage.mode: central`): a replica then keeps nothing that matters on its own, so an","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"What lives where","u":"kubernetes.html#what-lives-where","x":"What One replica (`storage.mode: disk`, the default) Several replicas (`storage.mode: central`) Usage log Daily files on the volume PostgreSQL (each line once), and a copy on each replica's own folder Events, alerts fired Files on the volume PostgreSQL; each alert fires once, on one replica Audit log A file on the volu","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Set it up","u":"kubernetes.html#set-it-up","x":"`config/ha` adds PostgreSQL and Redis samples and three replicas, with settings from the ConfigMap; `config/ha-shared`, the recommended one, builds on it and keeps the settings in PostgreSQL (see how settings reach every replica ): Shell Edit the Secret patch in `kustomization.yaml` (and `manifest/postgres/service.yaml","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"On the setup page","u":"kubernetes.html#on-the-setup-page","x":"The setup page's Data & storage tab has a section, Several replicas (central storage) , that explains what it is for and writes the settings for you to copy. Pick where the proxy runs ( Docker Compose , Kubernetes or systemd ), where the settings live ( in PostgreSQL , edited on the page, recommended, or from a file , ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Run several replicas: how settings reach every replica","u":"kubernetes.html#run-several-replicas-how-settings-reach-every-replica","x":"In plain words. Every replica has to run the same settings (`config.yaml`) and accept the same access tokens. There are two ways to do that; pick one: Settings in PostgreSQL (recommended). The replicas keep one copy of the settings in the PostgreSQL they already share. Change a setting on any replica's setup page and e","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Load balancing","u":"kubernetes.html#load-balancing","x":"Round robin is right: nothing needs sticky sessions. A conversation keeps to the provider account that last answered it on every replica (so the provider's prompt cache keeps hitting), signed-in browsers are known to every replica, and rate limits count across replicas. If you want them anyway, hash on the `X-Maple-Ses","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"When PostgreSQL or Redis is away","u":"kubernetes.html#when-postgresql-or-redis-is-away","x":"PostgreSQL away: requests are still served. Requests of people with a budget are refused with `503` until it is back (`storage.budgets_when_down: open` lets them through, uncounted). Usage lines wait in a small queue in the replica's memory (`storage.spool_max_records`, 2000) and are written once it is back, each once.","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Upgrades","u":"kubernetes.html#upgrades","x":"Roll replicas out one at a time (the sample sets `maxUnavailable: 0`). A new release adds to the database's tables and never removes or renames anything that the release before still uses, so old and new replicas run side by side during the rollout; upgrade one release at a time. A replica that stops turns not ready fi","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Backups","u":"kubernetes.html#backups","x":"Back up PostgreSQL (`pg_dump`, or your provider's backups): it holds the usage, events, budgets, settings and audit log. `maple-proxy storage export-config` also writes the shared settings to files. Redis holds nothing that must survive: after losing it, browsers sign in again and caches fill again (and the record of t","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Usage for analysis","u":"kubernetes.html#usage-for-analysis","x":"The shared PostgreSQL holds every replica's usage and events as two read-only views with typed columns: `maple_usage_rows_v9` (every column of the usage record , in its order, after `_day`, `_ingested_at` and `_ingest_seq`; `maple_usage_rows` is the same up to schema 8, kept as 2.x made it so a 2.x replica can start on","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Without PostgreSQL: storage mode redis","u":"kubernetes.html#without-postgresql-storage-mode-redis","x":"In plain words. New in 2.3: several replicas can share one Redis (or Valkey) and no PostgreSQL (`storage.mode: redis`). Everything central mode keeps in PostgreSQL (usage, budgets, events, the audit log, settings and access tokens) is kept in that Redis instead, so you run one database fewer. It is supported, but Postg","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"The MCP hub","u":"kubernetes.html#the-mcp-hub","x":"The hub's servers are programs and sessions that live on one machine, so the hub runs as one replica of its own (`manifest/maple-mcp-hub`, started with `maple-proxy mcp-hub serve`), with its own ConfigMap; clients connect to its Service. The proxy replicas do not serve it. It has a Secret of its own: never mount the pr","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Reaching cloud metadata or link-local services","u":"kubernetes.html#reaching-cloud-metadata-or-link-local-services","x":"The hub never connects an MCP server to a link-local address (`169.254.0.0/16`, `fe80::/10`) or a cloud metadata address (such as `169.254.169.254` or `fd00:ec2::254`), also in the standard IPv6 forms that carry such an address, and `allow_private` does not change that: on a node, the metadata address hands out the nod","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Logs","u":"kubernetes.html#logs","x":"Shell Logs are JSON on standard output, one object per line, with `proxy_instance` naming the pod; see Docker: Logs for the fields, levels and shipping. Probes are left out of the request log.","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Metrics","u":"kubernetes.html#metrics","x":"Each pod can serve its own Prometheus `/metrics` (off by default): set `metrics: {prometheus: \"on\"}` in the ConfigMap, add a metrics token to the Secret (`access.metrics_tokens`, one `label=token` a line), and add the `config/metrics` component to your overlay. It adds a `ServiceMonitor` (Prometheus Operator) that scra","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Images and memory","u":"kubernetes.html#images-and-memory","x":"The pod's memory limit sets how much image resizing may take at once (25% of it by default) and Go's own memory limit (90% of it); see Docker: Images and memory . Raise `limits.memory`, or `images.resize_memory`, if your tools send many large screenshots at once.","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Search by meaning","u":"kubernetes.html#search-by-meaning","x":"Each replica downloads a model on first use into its own folder (an `emptyDir` in `config/ha`). To download once for all, mount one ReadWriteMany volume at `/models` in every replica: replicas installing the same model at the same moment wait for one another, and each download is checked against its pinned checksums be","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"See also","u":"kubernetes.html#see-also","x":"Run the proxy in Docker The published image, Compose quick starts and one volume for everything it keeps. Teams People, teams, access tokens, budgets and rate limits on one proxy. Which setup do I need? One laptop, a team, or a company: a guide to choosing. Security What Maple protects, by default and when you widen it","m":"teams"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"Language servers","u":"language-servers.html","x":"Language servers give the agent compile errors right after every edit. A language server is the program your editor uses to underline errors, jump to a definition and show a type on hover, and Maple Harness can use the same programs. With one installed for your project's language: Errors are shown right after each edit","m":"single"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"In plain words","u":"language-servers.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. The programs your editor uses to underline errors. The agent uses the same ones. Why you want it. The agent sees a compile error right after its edit and fixes it on the next step, instead of finding out when the build fails. What to do. Have the language server for your language installed (the one your edi","m":"single"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"How it finds servers","u":"language-servers.html#how-it-finds-servers","x":"By default (`harness.lsp: auto`), the agent looks at the folder you started it in. If it finds a file that marks a language, and the matching server is on your `PATH`, that server is used for that language. Nothing else is needed. If you name servers yourself in `harness.lsp_servers`, those are used exactly as you wrot","m":"single"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"Check what it found","u":"language-servers.html#check-what-it-found","x":"Type `/lsp` in a session. It lists each server as running , idle (installed, starts when first needed) or missing , and prints the install command for a missing one. `maple-harness doctor` has a \"Language servers\" line too. A missing server is only a warning: everything still works, the agent just doesn't get compile e","m":"single"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"Install a server","u":"language-servers.html#install-a-server","x":"Start the agent in the project's root folder, the one with the marker file. The first two columns say what triggers a language and which server is used. Language Marker files in the project folder Server Install Go `go.mod`, `go.work` `gopls` `go install golang.org/x/tools/gopls@latest` TypeScript, JavaScript `tsconfig","m":"single"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"Gherkin and Cucumber","u":"language-servers.html#gherkin-and-cucumber","x":"If your project describes behaviour in `.feature` files (behaviour-driven development), the Cucumber language server checks them. The most useful thing it does is report a step with no matching step definition , so when the agent writes a scenario, it learns immediately which steps still need code. A typical project: G","m":"single"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"Choose the servers yourself","u":"language-servers.html#choose-the-servers-yourself","x":"Add `lsp_servers` to `config.yaml`. Each entry names the file extensions, the command and its arguments. This replaces detection. YAML","m":"single"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"Turn it off","u":"language-servers.html#turn-it-off","x":"YAML With `lsp: off` no server is started, no errors are added to results and the three lookup tools are not offered. `lsp: auto` is the default.","m":"single"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"When the agent still writes files with echo and cat","u":"language-servers.html#when-the-agent-still-writes-files-with-echo-and-cat","x":"The agent is told to create and change files with its own file tools, because those show you a diff, apply the safety checks and run the language server. Some models still write files through the shell, for example with `cat > file <<EOF`. By default the agent notes it in that command's result so the model can correct ","m":"single"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"Troubleshooting","u":"language-servers.html#troubleshooting","x":"Installed, but `/lsp` says missing. The agent looks on the `PATH` it was started with. A tool installed by `npm i -g` or `go install` lives in a folder that must be on that `PATH` (for Go, `~/go/bin`). Start the agent from a shell where the command works, and check with `which gopls` (or the server's name). No server i","m":"single"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"What has been tried","u":"language-servers.html#what-has-been-tried","x":"Stated plainly, so you know what to expect: The way the agent starts servers, sends them each edit and reads back their errors, and the answers it gives to the requests servers make of an editor, has been tested against a stand-in server in the test suite. The Cucumber language server was run for real: it reported \"Und","m":"single"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"Related","u":"language-servers.html#related","x":"The settings are in Harness settings , and `/lsp` is in Commands and keys . Other problems: Troubleshooting .","m":"single"},{"p":"When a provider's limit runs out","h":"When a provider's limit runs out","u":"limit-resets.html","x":"Every cloud account has limits: requests a minute, tokens a minute, a daily quota, a monthly allowance, a credit balance. When every model a request could go to is out of limit, and no fallback is left, Maple does not just give up. The proxy says when the limit lifts, and the harness pauses, waits for the reset, and th","m":"single"},{"p":"When a provider's limit runs out","h":"In plain words","u":"limit-resets.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Out of Anthropic's limit until 05:30? The session says so, waits, and at 05:30 picks the work back up: \"Back after the reset, eh.\" No typing \"continue\" at dawn. Why you want it. A long task does not die halfway through because a rate window filled up. Grab a double-double; the work resumes on its own. What ","m":"single"},{"p":"When a provider's limit runs out","h":"What you see","u":"limit-resets.html#what-you-see","x":"In the terminal, the transcript says what ran out and until when: The session waits for the limit's reset; `Esc` stops, `/resume now` tries at once. A demo setup: the stand-in service always answers that its limit is reached. While it waits, the working line says `Paused until 05:30`. A helper agent that hit the same l","m":"single"},{"p":"When a provider's limit runs out","h":"Ways the wait ends","u":"limit-resets.html#ways-the-wait-ends","x":"What happens What the harness does The reset time comes Carries on, a few seconds after it (spread out, so a session and its agents do not all ask at once) You set up a fallback, or change the proxy's setup (a budget raised, a key added) Carries on at once, with the new route The provider's limit clears early (the prox","m":"single"},{"p":"When a provider's limit runs out","h":"On your phone","u":"limit-resets.html#on-your-phone","x":"While the session (or one of its helper agents) waits, the remote page shows a banner: Paused until 05:30: out of anthropic limit (stated by the provider) . On a device that drives the session, Carry on now tries at once, as `/resume now` does. A watch-only device sees the banner without the button; a shared link sees ","m":"single"},{"p":"When a provider's limit runs out","h":"Other ways to run it","u":"limit-resets.html#other-ways-to-run-it","x":"How you run it What happens at a limit The full-screen terminal, `--repl` Waits and carries on; a long wait is asked about. In `--repl`, type `/resume now` while it waits `--prompt` Stops with exit status 75, unless you pass `--wait-for-reset` (below) `--rpc` Waits, and tells the program driving it: a `limit_wait` even","m":"single"},{"p":"When a provider's limit runs out","h":"Settings","u":"limit-resets.html#settings","x":"Setting Default What it does `harness.limits.resume_after_reset` `on` Wait for the reset and carry on. `off` stops the turn and says when the limit lifts `harness.limits.max_reset_wait` `6h` The longest wait taken on its own. Longer ones are put to you; a run nobody watches stops with the date. `15m`, `30m`, `1h`, `2h`","m":"single"},{"p":"When a provider's limit runs out","h":"What Maple reads from each provider","u":"limit-resets.html#what-maple-reads-from-each-provider","x":"Provider What tells Maple when the limit lifts Basis Anthropic `retry-after` (seconds) on a 429; else the `anthropic-ratelimit-{requests,tokens,input-tokens,output-tokens}-reset` time (RFC 3339) of the window that is at 0 stated Anthropic, monthly spend limit No time in the answer: the 1st of next month, 00:00 UTC esti","m":"single"},{"p":"When a provider's limit runs out","h":"Technical details","u":"limit-resets.html#technical-details","x":"The proxy's answer. When every route of a request is refused for a limit, the proxy answers 429 with: `X-Maple-Retry-At`: when the earliest of those routes lifts (RFC 3339, UTC, rounded up to the second); `X-Maple-Retry-Basis`: `stated` or `estimated`; `X-Maple-Retry-Source`: what the time was read from (`retry-after`,","m":"single"},{"p":"When a provider's limit runs out","h":"See also","u":"limit-resets.html#see-also","x":"Fallbacks and models , to give a limit somewhere to go Background agents and the lineup Commands , for `/resume now`, `--wait-for-reset` and the exit statuses Proxy settings , for the headers","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with LiteLLM","h":"Use it with LiteLLM","u":"litellm.html","x":"LiteLLM is a gateway many teams already run in front of their model accounts. The Maple proxy works with it in both directions: it can use a LiteLLM gateway as one of its providers, and a LiteLLM gateway can use the Maple proxy as one of its models. The short version","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with LiteLLM","h":"In plain words","u":"litellm.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Two ways to combine them. Maple in front: your tools talk to Maple, and Maple sends some models' requests to your team's LiteLLM. LiteLLM in front: your team's LiteLLM sends some models' requests to a Maple proxy, which serves them from its own accounts (a GitHub Copilot seat, for example). Why you want it.","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with LiteLLM","h":"Maple in front of LiteLLM","u":"litellm.html#maple-in-front-of-litellm","x":"Add your LiteLLM gateway as a provider, with a LiteLLM key, and list the models you want from it: YAML On the setup page, Providers offers LiteLLM ; Detect models lists what the key may use and fills in each model's context window from LiteLLM's model information. A key that may not read that information still lists it","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with LiteLLM","h":"Telling LiteLLM who asked","u":"litellm.html#telling-litellm-who-asked","x":"In plain words. By default LiteLLM sees every request from Maple as coming from Maple's key. If you want LiteLLM's own spend records and budgets to tell your people and teams apart, say so, and Maple sends their ids along. Nothing else about the caller is sent. Set it below, in `config.yaml`, or on the setup page ( Pro","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with LiteLLM","h":"Serving the models LiteLLM lists","u":"litellm.html#serving-the-models-litellm-lists","x":"In plain words. Instead of writing each model under the provider, Maple can serve whatever your LiteLLM lists, and pick up models added to it later, within a list of names you allow. YAML The technical view. The list is LiteLLM's `/v1/models` for this key, read when the proxy starts and every `refresh_minutes`; each mo","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with LiteLLM","h":"Moving a client from LiteLLM to Maple","u":"litellm.html#moving-a-client-from-litellm-to-maple","x":"A tool set up for a LiteLLM gateway sends the gateway's key in the `x-litellm-api-key` header (for Claude Code, through `ANTHROPIC_CUSTOM_HEADERS`). The Maple proxy reads its access token from that header too, ahead of `Authorization`, so such a tool needs only its address changed and a Maple access token in place of t","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with LiteLLM","h":"LiteLLM's management API","u":"litellm.html#litellms-management-api","x":"Scripts and tools that manage a LiteLLM gateway's keys, users and teams (LiteLLM's `/key`, `/user` and `/team` routes, or its `litellm-proxy` command) can manage a Maple proxy the same way, with its address changed. The routes are on the proxy's OpenAI-compatible address, and need an access token with the manage scope,","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with LiteLLM","h":"LiteLLM in front of Maple","u":"litellm.html#litellm-in-front-of-maple","x":"In LiteLLM's `config.yaml`, add a model whose `api_base` is the Maple proxy. Use LiteLLM's `openai/` prefix with the name the Maple proxy serves: YAML LiteLLM's callers then use `copilot-fusion-high` through LiteLLM's chat completions, Responses API or Anthropic messages endpoint, and the Maple proxy answers from its o","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with LiteLLM","h":"For LiteLLM users: the same ideas, by name","u":"litellm.html#for-litellm-users-the-same-ideas-by-name","x":"In plain words. Most of what you set up in LiteLLM has a Maple counterpart with another name. This table is for finding it; `maple-proxy import litellm` (below) does the translating for a config file. In LiteLLM In Maple Notes `config.yaml`, `model_list` `config.yaml`, `providers` with their `models` One provider per a","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with LiteLLM","h":"A whole config, side by side","u":"litellm.html#a-whole-config-side-by-side","x":"In plain words. Here is a typical LiteLLM `config.yaml` and the Maple settings `maple-proxy import litellm` makes from it, unchanged, followed by what came across differently. Your own file's report says the same for yours. The LiteLLM config: YAML What the importer prints for it (`maple-proxy import litellm litellm-co","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with LiteLLM","h":"Move a LiteLLM config to Maple","u":"litellm.html#move-a-litellm-config-to-maple","x":"In plain words. `maple-proxy import litellm` reads your LiteLLM `config.yaml` and writes the matching Maple settings: your models, their accounts, groups of deployments, fallbacks and MCP servers. It prints what it would add and a report of what came across, what came across a little differently, and what has no equiva","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with LiteLLM","h":"From a running LiteLLM","u":"litellm.html#from-a-running-litellm","x":"In plain words. If your LiteLLM keeps models, teams and users in its database (added on its admin page or through its API), they are not in `config.yaml`. `--from-url` asks the running LiteLLM for them instead, and brings its teams and users across as Maple people and teams , with their budgets and limits. It only read","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with LiteLLM","h":"See also","u":"litellm.html#see-also","x":"Access control Organizations, roles, key rules and end-user budgets, the Maple side of LiteLLM's teams and keys. Guardrails The checks LiteLLM guardrails become, and services in LiteLLM's guardrail shape. Model groups Several deployments under one model name, with routing and retries. MCP hub Where LiteLLM's `mcp_serve","m":"single"},{"p":"Get the most from a local GPU","h":"Get the most from a local GPU","u":"local-gpu.html","x":"A model server on your own GPU (vLLM, SGLang, llama.cpp) can work on several requests at the same time. Up to a point, more at once means more work done in all; past it, every request just waits longer. This page shows how to find that point for your server with `maple-proxy tune`, and which of three settings to pick. ","m":"both"},{"p":"Get the most from a local GPU","h":"In plain words","u":"local-gpu.html#in-plain-words","x":"Run this while nothing else is using the server: Shell It says what it will send and asks before sending anything. Then it sends practice requests the size an agent sends (a long prompt, a medium answer), first one at a time, then 2, 4, 8, 16 and 32 at once, and measures: total speed : how many tokens the server writes","m":"both"},{"p":"Get the most from a local GPU","h":"A speed floor instead of a number","u":"local-gpu.html#a-speed-floor-instead-of-a-number","x":"If what matters is that no answer gets slower than a speed you can read comfortably, give a floor instead of a fixed number: YAML The proxy then works out the number itself as it runs: it lets in one more request at once while every request still gets at least 100 tokens a second, and fewer when they drop below it. `ma","m":"both"},{"p":"Get the most from a local GPU","h":"An example, measured","u":"local-gpu.html#an-example-measured","x":"One run of this kind of measurement on one GPU (96 GB), serving a 27-billion-parameter model with 4-bit weights through vLLM, with prompts of about 9,000 tokens and answers of about 650 tokens. Measured on 2026-11-22, one server and one model ; your GPU, model and request sizes will give other figures, which is why the","m":"both"},{"p":"Get the most from a local GPU","h":"Subagents","u":"local-gpu.html#subagents","x":"The harness's subagents read the proxy's figure for the server, so keep `subagents: {mode: auto}` (the default). It runs at most 16 at once, and the main agent's own requests count too. With one request at a time, subagents wait for each other; `mode: sequential` says so plainly. For the curious","m":"both"},{"p":"Get the most from a local GPU","h":"Technical details","u":"local-gpu.html#technical-details","x":"The requests go straight to the provider with the proxy's own adapters, not through a running proxy: they do not count in the usage log, budgets or the proxy's own limits. Nothing is sent to the proxy's ports. Each level runs closed loop: as many clients as the level, each sending its requests one after another (2 each","m":"both"},{"p":"Loops","h":"Loops","u":"loops.html","x":"A loop is the agent going round again and again until something is done, instead of you typing \"keep going\" each time. Maple Harness has four kinds: a goal it works toward turn after turn until a check passes, a Stop hook that sends it back to work, a scheduled loop that comes back every so often, and a shell loop that","m":"single"},{"p":"Loops","h":"In plain words","u":"loops.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. You hand off one thing. With a goal, you hand off the stop condition : \"keep going until `go test ./...` passes\". With a scheduled loop, the trigger : \"every ten minutes, check the PR\". With a Stop hook, the check : your own script decides whether the agent may stop. With a shell loop, the whole run : a scr","m":"single"},{"p":"Loops","h":"A goal: keep going until it holds","u":"loops.html#a-goal-keep-going-until-it-holds","x":"The first turn starts at once, with the condition as its direction. When the turn ends, the harness runs the check. If it fails, the agent gets another turn on its own, told what the check printed (the end of it, where a test run says what failed). When the check passes, the goal is met and the loop stops: A goal again","m":"single"},{"p":"Loops","h":"Three ways to check a goal","u":"loops.html#three-ways-to-check-a-goal","x":"Option The goal holds when Best for `--check \"<command>\"` The command exits 0, run in the project folder after each turn Anything a program can prove: tests, a build, a linter, `git status --porcelain` being empty `--says \"<phrase>\"` The turn's final answer contains the phrase, word for word A completion promise: the a","m":"single"},{"p":"Loops","h":"Its limits","u":"loops.html#its-limits","x":"Option Default What it does `--max N` `20` At most this many turns in all, the first one included (1 to 100). Past it the goal ends, not met `--budget AIU` none At most this many AI units, its checks included (above 0, at most 100). Checked after each turn, so one turn can go over And these, which you do not set: No pr","m":"single"},{"p":"Loops","h":"See it, clear it","u":"loops.html#see-it-clear-it","x":"`/goal` shows the goal: its condition, how it is checked, the turns and AI units so far, how long it has run and the newest check's reason. After a goal ends it shows the last one and how it ended (`met`, `impossible`, `max_turns`, `budget` or `cleared`). `/goal clear` ends it (`stop`, `off`, `reset`, `none` and `cance","m":"single"},{"p":"Loops","h":"A goal from a script","u":"loops.html#a-goal-from-a-script","x":"`--prompt` runs the whole goal loop in one call and exits when it ends: Shell The goal's lines go to the error stream (`◎ goal not met yet …`), the final answer to standard output, and `--json-summary` gets a `goal` object: `outcome`, `turns` and `ai_units`. `--json-journal` records each check as a guard line (`goal_co","m":"single"},{"p":"Loops","h":"A Stop hook that sends it back","u":"loops.html#a-stop-hook-that-sends-it-back","x":"A Stop hook is a command the harness runs each time the agent is about to stop. It can block the stop and say what is left, and the agent takes one more turn with that as its direction. Unlike a goal, it lives in a hooks file, so it applies to every session in that project. It answers in any of these ways, the same as ","m":"single"},{"p":"Loops","h":"A scheduled loop: come back every so often","u":"loops.html#a-scheduled-loop-come-back-every-so-often","x":"It runs now, then every ten minutes. After each of its turns the check runs, and once it passes the loop is removed on its own: Option The loop ends when `--until \"<command>\"` The command exits 0 after one of its turns `--says \"<phrase>\"` One of its turns' answers contains the phrase `--max N` It has run N times (1 to ","m":"single"},{"p":"Loops","h":"Unattended: a shell loop or a durable job","u":"loops.html#unattended-a-shell-loop-or-a-durable-job","x":"A shell loop keeps its state in a named session, so each run carries on from the last one's conversation: Shell Or let one run do it all, and branch on how it ended: Shell Nobody is there to answer questions, so a tool call your rules do not allow is refused, recorded, and the run goes on without it (see Approvals when","m":"single"},{"p":"Loops","h":"How it differs from Claude Code","u":"loops.html#how-it-differs-from-claude-code","x":"Claude Code Maple Harness `/goal`'s check A small, fast model judges the condition each turn A command (`--check`) or a phrase (`--says`) when you give one, deterministic, with no AI units spent on it; otherwise the session's own model judges, in a request counted in its AI units A turn limit Written into the condition","m":"single"},{"p":"Loops","h":"Technical notes","u":"loops.html#technical-notes","x":"A goal's next turn, and a hook's, are messages appended at the end of the conversation, so the provider's prompt cache keeps matching. The model's judgement of a goal is a separate request with no tools. A goal's check command and a loop's `--until` run in the project folder without asking, so they are contained as the","m":"single"},{"p":"Loops","h":"See also","u":"loops.html#see-also","x":"Scheduled work `/loop`, `/schedule`, wake-ups and durable jobs. Background commands and monitors Wait for slow things without polling. Approvals when nobody answers What an unattended loop does with a question. Commands and keys `/goal`, `/loop` and the `--prompt` exit statuses. Harness settings Hook files, and what a ","m":"single"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"MCP hub","u":"mcp-hub.html","x":"The proxy can run your MCP servers once and offer all of their tools at one address. Maple Harness, Claude Code, OpenCode and any other tool that speaks MCP then point at that address instead of each starting its own copy of every server. Off by default, and stable since 3.0 While it is off nothing listens, nothing sta","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"In plain words","u":"mcp-hub.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. The proxy runs your MCP servers (the add-ons that give an agent extra tools) once, and every tool you use reaches them at one address. Why you want it. One copy of each server instead of one per tool, one place to limit what each person may call, and one place to sign in to hosted servers: once for everyone","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"How it works","u":"mcp-hub.html#how-it-works","x":"You list the servers in `config.yaml` under `mcp_hub.servers`, in the same shape as the agent's `mcp_servers`: a program the proxy starts (stdio), or a server it reaches by URL (`http` or `sse`). The proxy serves one MCP endpoint, `http://127.0.0.1:11545/mcp` by default (Streamable HTTP). Each tool is named `<server>__","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Turn it on","u":"mcp-hub.html#turn-it-on","x":"Add a server , a program the proxy starts or a server it reaches by URL (`maple-proxy mcp-hub add`). Turn the hub on (`maple-proxy mcp-hub enable`). Make a token for each tool that will use it (`maple-proxy mcp-hub token <label>`); it is shown once. Check it (`maple-proxy mcp-hub status`): each server, its state and it","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Point your tools at it","u":"mcp-hub.html#point-your-tools-at-it","x":"Each tool sends the token from `maple-proxy mcp-hub token` as `Authorization: Bearer <token>`. Keep it in an environment variable or your tool's secret store rather than in a file you share.","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Maple Harness","u":"mcp-hub.html#maple-harness","x":"In `config.yaml`: YAML The tools appear as `mcp__hub__<server>__<tool>` and ask for approval like any other tool. To allow one without asking, name it in a rule, for example `- {decision: allow, pattern: mcp__hub__files__read_file}`. Maple Harness sends the token to the hub's address on this machine only after the hub ","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Claude Code","u":"mcp-hub.html#claude-code","x":"Shell or in `.mcp.json`: JSON","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"OpenCode","u":"mcp-hub.html#opencode","x":"In `opencode.json`: JSON","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Settings","u":"mcp-hub.html#settings","x":"Key Default What it does `mcp_hub.enabled` off Turns the hub on `mcp_hub.listen` `127.0.0.1:11545` Where the endpoint is served (path `/mcp`) `mcp_hub.loopback_without_token` off Lets tools on this machine use the hub without a token. Anything that runs on this machine, a sandboxed command included, could then call eve","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Who may use which tools","u":"mcp-hub.html#who-may-use-which-tools","x":"With people set up, each access token belongs to a person, and `mcp_hub.people` says which of the hub's tools each person may see and call: YAML `local` is whoever calls from this machine without a token (only with `loopback_without_token`), `unattributed` a token tied to no person, and `*` anyone not listed. With no `","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Servers that ask you to sign in","u":"mcp-hub.html#servers-that-ask-you-to-sign-in","x":"Hosted MCP servers often want a sign-in with an account instead of a fixed token. The hub signs in to them in one of four ways, set per server under `auth`: One sign-in for everyone (`type: oauth`). You sign the hub in once, in a terminal on the hub's machine, and everyone who may use the server works through that one ","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"In plain words","u":"mcp-hub.html#in-plain-words-1","x":"One account for the team : add `auth: {type: oauth}` to the server, then run `maple-proxy mcp-hub login NAME` on the hub's machine and approve in the browser. The hub's MCP hub tab and `maple-proxy mcp-hub status` show the server as needs a sign-in until then, and running a few seconds after. Everyone their own : add `","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Technically","u":"mcp-hub.html#technically","x":"YAML Sign-in follows MCP's authorization: OAuth 2.1 with PKCE, the server's and the authorization server's metadata, and automatic client registration where the server offers it (or the `client_id` you give). `client_secret` is always a `${secret:…}` or `${env:…}` reference: a secret written into `config.yaml` is refus","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Acting for each person without a browser","u":"mcp-hub.html#acting-for-each-person-without-a-browser","x":"In plain words Some sign-in services (identity providers such as Keycloak, Okta or Microsoft Entra, set up for it) can trade one token for another: \"this is Alice, says the hub; give me a token for the tracker in her name\". With `type: token_exchange`, the hub does that for every call: each person's calls reach the ser","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"More ways to limit access","u":"mcp-hub.html#more-ways-to-limit-access","x":"Besides `people`, the hub reads lists per team, per access token, and named groups of tools: YAML The most restrictive wins. A tool is offered only when every list that applies allows it: the person's, their team's, and their token's. Alice in the `contractors` team gets only the readers' tools. An empty section limits","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Prompts and resources","u":"mcp-hub.html#prompts-and-resources","x":"With `prompts: true` or `resources: true` on a server, the hub offers that server's prompts and resources too, not only its tools. Prompts are named like tools (`docs__summarise`); resources keep their own addresses (`docs://readme`), and a read goes to the server that offers it. The access lists cover them as well: `d","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Pinning tools","u":"mcp-hub.html#pinning-tools","x":"A tool's description is what the model reads, so a server that changes it later changes what your agents are told. With `pin: true`, the hub keeps each tool's name, description and schema (and each prompt and resource it passes on) as it first saw them: One that changes, or a new one, is held back : not listed and not ","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"When a server's tools change","u":"mcp-hub.html#when-a-servers-tools-change","x":"The hub reads a server's lists again when the server says they changed, and every few minutes for servers that never say so. Every open session sees the change at once: there is nothing to restart.","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Cost and limits per server","u":"mcp-hub.html#cost-and-limits-per-server","x":"`cost_per_call: 0.002` (US dollars), or `tool_costs: {expensive_search: 0.05}`, records a cost with every call that reached the server: in the `mcp.call` event (`cost_usd=…`, labelled `cost=configured`) and as a running total per server in `maple-proxy mcp-hub status`. It is your figure, not a measured one. `rate_limit","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Finding A2A agents from an MCP client","u":"mcp-hub.html#finding-a2a-agents-from-an-mcp-client","x":"","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"In plain words","u":"mcp-hub.html#in-plain-words-3","x":"When the proxy's A2A gateway is on as well, the hub offers one tool of its own, `agent_search` . An MCP client (Claude Code, Maple Harness, an agent framework) can ask it \"which agent writes tests?\" and get back the agents behind the gateway that are meant for this caller, with what they do and where to send them tasks","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Technically","u":"mcp-hub.html#technically-2","x":"Name and arguments. `agent_search`, without a `server__` prefix, so it never clashes with a server's tool. `query` (words, up to 2000 bytes; empty lists every agent) and `limit` (1 to 100, default 20). The answer is JSON: `{\"agents\": [...], \"method\": \"meaning\" | \"words\"}`, each agent with its `name`, `description`, `ta","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Letting the proxy run tools for a chat request","u":"mcp-hub.html#letting-the-proxy-run-tools-for-a-chat-request","x":"","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"In plain words","u":"mcp-hub.html#in-plain-words-4","x":"What it is. Normally a tool runs in the program that asked the model (your agent). With the tool loop on, a chat request can instead say \"use these hub servers, and run their tools for me\": the proxy offers the model those tools, runs each call the model makes on the hub, gives the model the result, and asks it again, ","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Turn it on","u":"mcp-hub.html#turn-it-on-1","x":"On the setup page's MCP hub tab, fold The tool loop (it needs a page password), or in `config.yaml`: YAML A running proxy follows the change within a few seconds. Who may (`people`, `teams`, `tokens`): a caller named in any of the three may use the loop; with all three empty, no one may. `*` in `people` is anyone tied ","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Ask for it","u":"mcp-hub.html#ask-for-it","x":"On the OpenAI-compatible address (`/v1/chat/completions`), a tool of type `mcp`, in the shape the OpenAI Responses API and LiteLLM use: JSON On the Anthropic-compatible address (`/v1/messages`), Anthropic's MCP connector shape: JSON Which servers. `server_label` (or `name`) is a hub server's name. `server_url` (or `url","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"What comes back","u":"mcp-hub.html#what-comes-back","x":"One answer: the text of every round (a blank line between them), the model's last message, and the usage of all its model calls added up. `maple_tool_loop` beside it: `rounds` (model calls), `tool_calls` (tools the proxy ran), `stop` (why it ended), `calls` (each call's name and outcome, `cut: true` when its result was","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Limits","u":"mcp-hub.html#limits","x":"Setting Default Ends the loop when `max_iterations` 8 this many model calls were made `max_tool_calls` 16 the next round's calls would go past it (none of that round's run) `max_seconds` 120 the loop has run this long (a model call still going is stopped) `max_tokens` 200000 the model calls' input and output tokens rea","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"What is recorded","u":"mcp-hub.html#what-is-recorded","x":"Every model call is its own line in the usage log, all with the request's id, so totals and budgets count each one once. Every tool call is an `mcp.call` event, as through the hub, with `via=tool_loop`. What each conversation's loops reached is kept for 24 hours, at most 500 conversations per person, token or end user ","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Risks","u":"mcp-hub.html#risks","x":"Caution Mark as `never` (no approval) only tools you would let the model call unattended. The tool loop runs them with nobody watching. A tool's result can try to steer the model (prompt injection): a page or a ticket the tool read may say \"now call delete\". The proxy only runs tools you allowed, for whoever may call t","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Compared with OpenAI, LiteLLM and Anthropic","u":"mcp-hub.html#compared-with-openai-litellm-and-anthropic","x":"OpenAI's Responses API and Anthropic's connector reach MCP servers anywhere by URL, with a token you send. Here only this proxy's hub servers can be named, and the hub signs in to them itself: `server_url` addresses, `authorization`, `headers`, `connector_id` and `authorization_token` are refused. No `mcp_list_tools`, ","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Providers' own MCP connectors","u":"mcp-hub.html#providers-own-mcp-connectors","x":"Some providers reach MCP servers themselves when a request names them: OpenAI's Responses API with an `mcp` tool that has a `server_url` or `connector_id`, or a prompt saved at the provider (`prompt: {\"id\": ...}`, whose tools the proxy cannot see), Anthropic's MCP connector with `mcp_servers` that have a `url`. A reque","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Other ways in","u":"mcp-hub.html#other-ways-in","x":"Behind the same token and checks as `/mcp`: Endpoint What it does `GET` or `POST /mcp-rest/tools/list` The caller's tools as JSON (`?server_id=NAME` for one server) `POST /mcp-rest/tools/call` `{\"name\": \"docs__search\", \"arguments\": {...}}`: one call, with the same checks, limits and records as through MCP `GET /mcp-ser","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Upgrading from 2.x","u":"mcp-hub.html#upgrading-from-2x","x":"Nothing to change: a 2.x `mcp_hub` section works as it is, with the same settings, commands, tool names, tokens and endpoint, with one exception: a server whose `headers`, `env` or `client_secret` name a credential of another of Maple's own features (a stored sign-in, the storage key or its variables, a webhook's or a ","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Security","u":"mcp-hub.html#security","x":"This machine only, by default, and a token on every request. The token has the `mcp-hub` scope, which no other scope includes, so a token made for the model surfaces or for remote control cannot reach the hub. Make one with `maple-proxy mcp-hub token <label>` in a terminal (it is shown once) and revoke it on the setup ","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Reaching cloud metadata or link-local services","u":"mcp-hub.html#reaching-cloud-metadata-or-link-local-services","x":"A server reached by URL is never connected to a link-local address (`169.254.0.0/16`, `fe80::/10`) or a cloud provider's metadata address (such as `169.254.169.254`, `fd00:ec2::254`, `fd20:ce::254` or `100.100.100.200`), whatever name points there, and also when it is written in one of the standard IPv6 forms that carr","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Running it as a service of its own","u":"mcp-hub.html#running-it-as-a-service-of-its-own","x":"`maple-proxy mcp-hub serve` runs the hub alone. Run one copy (the servers it starts are not shared between copies). To put it behind a reverse proxy or a load balancer, forward `/mcp` with the `Authorization` header, give the hub a `listen` address the forwarder can reach (with `tls_cert` and `tls_key`, or `insecure_ht","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Running it in a container","u":"mcp-hub.html#running-it-in-a-container","x":"The container image has no sandbox (one would need user namespaces, which containers rarely get), so there the hub starts a stdio server only if a working sandbox is available, and otherwise refuses it with a message in its log and in `maple-proxy mcp-hub status`. The status shows the sandbox as `container-strict`. Run","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Limits","u":"mcp-hub.html#limits-1","x":"Resources: reading is passed on; subscribing to a resource's changes is not. Sign-in through the hub works with Streamable HTTP servers only; the older SSE transport takes headers. A call through the hub stops after 5 minutes. An older proxy ignores `mcp_hub`: after going back to one, the hub and its limits no longer a","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"Commands","u":"mcp-hub.html#commands","x":"Command What it does `maple-proxy mcp-hub status` What the running hub is doing: its address, each server's state and tools. `--json` `maple-proxy mcp-hub serve` Run only the hub, without the model surfaces or the setup page, from the same `config.yaml` and tokens: for running it as a service of its own `maple-proxy mc","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub","h":"See also","u":"mcp-hub.html#see-also","x":"Give other tools this project's knowledge The harness's own MCP server, for one project. Guardrails Check tool calls and their results on the way through the hub. Agents that call agents (A2A) The gateway whose agents the hub's `agent_search` finds. Run the proxy on Kubernetes The hub as a sidecar or a Deployment of it","m":"both"},{"p":"Give other tools this project's knowledge","h":"Give other tools this project's knowledge","u":"mcp.html","x":"`maple-harness mcp serve` lets any tool that speaks MCP (Claude Code, OpenCode, another Maple Harness) read this project's notes and code map, the same ones the agent uses. Build the index once from the project directory: Shell Then connect a tool. A client normally launches the server itself. The short version","m":"single"},{"p":"Give other tools this project's knowledge","h":"In plain words","u":"mcp.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Other tools (Claude Code, OpenCode, another Maple) can read this project's memory notes and code map, the ones Maple builds as it works. Why you want it. Each tool starts already knowing your project's structure, instead of reading it again. What to do. `maple-harness graph reindex` once in the project, the","m":"single"},{"p":"Give other tools this project's knowledge","h":"Claude Code","u":"mcp.html#claude-code","x":"Shell or in the project's `.mcp.json`: JSON Add `\"--read-only\"` to the arguments to withhold the tools that write notes.","m":"single"},{"p":"Give other tools this project's knowledge","h":"OpenCode","u":"mcp.html#opencode","x":"In `opencode.json`: JSON","m":"single"},{"p":"Give other tools this project's knowledge","h":"Another Maple Harness","u":"mcp.html#another-maple-harness","x":"In `config.yaml`: YAML","m":"single"},{"p":"Give other tools this project's knowledge","h":"Using MCP servers from the agent","u":"mcp.html#using-mcp-servers-from-the-agent","x":"The agent can use MCP servers too, from the `harness.mcp_servers` list or a project's `.mcp.json`. Their tools ask for approval like any other tool. A server that runs as a program on your machine runs with your permissions, so add only servers you trust. A server reached over the network needs `https` unless it is on ","m":"single"},{"p":"Give other tools this project's knowledge","h":"Where a local server starts","u":"mcp.html#where-a-local-server-starts","x":"In plain words. A server that runs as a program starts in the project's folder, as it does in other coding agents, so a server that works on the project finds it. But a program started in a folder can pick up code from it: `npx` prefers a copy of a package the folder's `node_modules` holds, and `python -m` a module the","m":"single"},{"p":"Give other tools this project's knowledge","h":"Credential variables","u":"mcp.html#credential-variables","x":"In plain words. Your shell often holds keys other tools use: a `GITHUB_TOKEN`, cloud keys, a provider's API key, the SSH agent. By default a local MCP server inherits them all, as in Maple 2.x, so a server that reads `GITHUB_TOKEN` from its environment just works. That also means every local server you run (and whateve","m":"single"},{"p":"Give other tools this project's knowledge","h":"Prompts a server offers","u":"mcp.html#prompts-a-server-offers","x":"Some MCP servers offer ready-made prompts as well as tools (\"review this file\", \"summarise the open issues\"). They are a tab of the command palette, MCP prompts (`Ctrl+P`, then `Tab`), one row per prompt, named `server: prompt`, with its arguments (a `*` marks the ones it needs). Picking one puts `/mcp prompt <server> ","m":"single"},{"p":"Give other tools this project's knowledge","h":"Servers that ask you to sign in","u":"mcp.html#servers-that-ask-you-to-sign-in","x":"Some hosted MCP servers (Atlassian's, for example) ask you to sign in with your account instead of taking a fixed token. Maple Harness signs in to them the way Claude Code does: through your browser. When you start Maple Harness in a terminal, it asks right away, naming the server's address and where it is configured: ","m":"single"},{"p":"Give other tools this project's knowledge","h":"See also","u":"mcp.html#see-also","x":"Harness settings: Tools, skills and hooks The MCP servers the agent itself uses. Drive it from another program Sessions as JSON for scripts, editors and other tools. MCP hub Your MCP servers run once, at one address, with sign-in and access lists. Search by meaning A small model on your machine that finds things by wha","m":"single"},{"p":"Memory caps and heavy runs","h":"Memory caps and heavy runs","u":"memory-limits.html","x":"The agent runs commands for you: tests, builds, scripts. Some of them use a lot of memory, and several agents can start several of them at once. Maple Harness caps how much memory those commands may use, and lets only one heavy run (a test suite, a build) go at a time, so the commands it runs can never take your whole ","m":"single"},{"p":"Memory caps and heavy runs","h":"In plain words","u":"memory-limits.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. A ceiling on the memory of each command the agent runs, another on all of one session's commands together, and a queue for heavy runs so two test suites never fight over your memory. Why it is there. A few test suites run at the same time, or one test that reads without a limit, can fill all of a computer's","m":"single"},{"p":"Memory caps and heavy runs","h":"What is capped","u":"memory-limits.html#what-is-capped","x":"Cap What it covers Default Setting Per command One command the agent runs (a `bash` call or a background job) and every process it starts, together Half of your memory, at most 8 GiB `limits.command_memory` Per session Every command one session runs, its helper agents' included, together Half of your memory `limits.ses","m":"single"},{"p":"Memory caps and heavy runs","h":"Why these defaults","u":"memory-limits.html#why-these-defaults","x":"The figures were chosen, not measured: 8 GiB is enough for a large race-enabled Go test suite or a big build, and one heavy run at a time is the rule that kept a busy machine usable once several agents worked on one project. A command that needs more than 8 GiB is rare, and when one does, it is better to hear it plainl","m":"single"},{"p":"Memory caps and heavy runs","h":"What counts as a heavy run","u":"memory-limits.html#what-counts-as-a-heavy-run","x":"A command is a heavy run when its first words are one of these (after `cd … &&`, environment settings such as `CI=1`, and wrappers such as `time`, `nice` or `timeout 600`): `go test`, `go build`, `go install`, `make`, `gmake`, `ninja`, `cmake --build`, `ctest`, `bazel`, `bazelisk`, `cargo test`, `cargo build`, `cargo n","m":"single"},{"p":"Memory caps and heavy runs","h":"What a cap hit looks like","u":"memory-limits.html#what-a-cap-hit-looks-like","x":"In the conversation, under the command, one line for you: The agent reads the full reason before the command's output: the cap, its setting, that it is not a crash, and what to try instead (a smaller part of the test suite, fewer jobs at once such as `go test -p 2` or `make -j2`, or looking for something that reads wit","m":"single"},{"p":"Memory caps and heavy runs","h":"What waiting for a slot looks like","u":"memory-limits.html#what-waiting-for-a-slot-looks-like","x":"A heavy run that finds every slot taken waits, and you see who has it: When it gets its turn it runs, and its result starts with how long it waited. The wait does not count against the command's own timeout. Esc stops the wait like any command; a command that waits longer than `limits.heavy_wait` (30 minutes) is not ru","m":"single"},{"p":"Memory caps and heavy runs","h":"Change the caps","u":"memory-limits.html#change-the-caps","x":"In a session, type `/settings`, then `limits` to filter the menu. Each cap shows its value, what it does and what it accepts. Press `Enter` on one, type the new value (empty goes back to the default), and `Enter` again: it is saved to your own `config.yaml`. Start a new session: the caps are read when a session starts.","m":"single"},{"p":"Memory caps and heavy runs","h":"A cap for one agent","u":"memory-limits.html#a-cap-for-one-agent","x":"An agent's file can give it a lower cap of its own (`limits.memory: 4G`, see Make your own ): each command that agent runs may use at most that much, never more than `command_memory`. It applies even with `command_memory: off`. A command stopped at it says `stopped at this agent's memory cap`, names `limits.memory in t","m":"single"},{"p":"Memory caps and heavy runs","h":"Technical details","u":"memory-limits.html#technical-details","x":"Linux with a user systemd (most desktop distributions): each command runs in its own transient systemd scope with `MemoryMax` set to the command cap, `MemorySwapMax=0` (so a command cannot get around the cap by swapping), and `OOMPolicy=kill` (when the kernel stops the largest process, the rest of that command stops wi","m":"single"},{"p":"Memory caps and heavy runs","h":"Troubleshooting","u":"memory-limits.html#troubleshooting","x":"You see What it means What to do `stopped at the memory cap … command_memory` One command went over its cap Run a smaller part, fewer jobs at once, or raise `limits.command_memory` `stopped at the memory cap … session_memory` The session's commands together went over Stop background jobs you do not need, or raise `limi","m":"single"},{"p":"Memory caps and heavy runs","h":"See also","u":"memory-limits.html#see-also","x":"Sandbox What the commands the agent runs can reach, and what they cannot. Subagents Several agents at once, and how many run on one model. Background commands and monitors Long runs in the background, under the same caps.","m":"single"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Metrics and telemetry export","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html","x":"The proxy can hand what it already knows about each request (counts, timings, tokens, health, budgets) to the monitoring and security tools you run: a Prometheus `/metrics` endpoint, an OpenTelemetry (OTLP) export of traces, metrics and logs, and the audit events as JSON lines for a SIEM. All of it is off by default ; ","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"In plain words","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#in-plain-words","x":"If you already run Prometheus, Grafana, an OpenTelemetry collector or a security tool (a SIEM), the proxy can feed them: how many requests, how fast, how many tokens and AI units, which providers are healthy, how much of each budget is used. You then watch Maple in the same place as everything else, and get its alerts ","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Headers on the page","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#headers-on-the-page","x":"A header's value (an API key, a `Bearer` token) is typed once and goes straight to the secret store, in the slot `otel/<name>` (the name in lower case); `config.yaml` gets the reference `${secret:otel/<name>}`, never the value. The page then shows each header by name only, with where its value comes from: The page says","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Send a test export","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#send-a-test-export","x":"Send a test export sends one span and one log record, both named `maple.test`, with the headers you set, to the collector as saved. Nothing in them comes from a request, prompt or answer. The page then shows where they went (the scheme and host for each signal), the protocol, the names of the headers sent (never their ","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Set it in config.yaml","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#set-it-in-configyaml","x":"The page writes these keys; you can also write them yourself. Every key is in Proxy settings . YAML A header that carries a credential (`authorization`, anything with `token`, `key` or `secret` in its name) must be a `${secret:...}` or `${env:...}` reference: set it on the page, or store it with `maple-proxy secrets se","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Prometheus","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#prometheus","x":"","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Where to scrape","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#where-to-scrape","x":"From Address Token This machine `http://127.0.0.1:11539/metrics` (the setup page's address) none; with a setup-page password, a token with the `metrics` scope Another machine, a container, Kubernetes the OpenAI-compatible address, `/metrics` a token with the `metrics` scope Tip Make a token on the Monitoring tab (step ","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"What is exported","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#what-is-exported","x":"Metric Type Labels `maple_requests_total` counter `provider`, `model`, `outcome` (`ok`, `error`, `canceled`, `cached`), `error_class`, `auto_weight` `maple_request_duration_seconds` histogram `provider`, `model`, `outcome` `maple_time_to_first_token_seconds` histogram `provider`, `model` (streamed answers) `maple_token","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Labels stay bounded","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#labels-stay-bounded","x":"Every label has a fixed set of values or a cap on how many it keeps (a model name past the cap is counted as `other`), so a client sending a new model name with every request cannot grow your Prometheus without end. There is no session, conversation, token or person label. `metrics.per_person: on` adds `person` and `te","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Several replicas","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#several-replicas","x":"Each pod serves its own `/metrics`, counting what it answered. Scrape every pod and add them up in Prometheus (`sum by (provider, model) (rate(maple_requests_total[5m]))`). Gauges such as `maple_budget_used` are each pod's own view. For the Prometheus Operator, the Kubernetes samples have a component, `config/metrics`,","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Grafana","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#grafana","x":"Some starting points: requests a second by outcome: `sum by (outcome) (rate(maple_requests_total[5m]))` error ratio by provider: `sum by (provider) (rate(maple_requests_total{outcome=\"error\"}[5m])) / sum by (provider) (rate(maple_requests_total[5m]))` 95th-percentile latency: `histogram_quantile(0.95, sum by (le, model","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"OpenTelemetry","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#opentelemetry","x":"With `otel.endpoint` set, the proxy sends to an OpenTelemetry collector over OTLP, gRPC (`grpc`, the default, port or HTTP/protobuf (`http`, port 4318; the signal paths `/v1/traces`, `/v1/metrics` and `/v1/logs` are added to the address). `http://` is plain text and `https://` is TLS. Traces: one span per request, in o","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Environment variables","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#environment-variables","x":"The standard `OTEL_*` variables fill in what `otel:` leaves unset: `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL`, `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS`, `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TIMEOUT`, `OTEL_SERVICE_NAME` (default `maple-proxy`), `OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES`, `OTEL_BSP_SCHEDULE_DELAY`, `OTEL_BSP_MAX_QUEUE_SIZE`, `OTEL_BSP_MAX_EXPORT_BATCH_SIZE` an","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Collector","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#collector","x":"A minimal OpenTelemetry Collector configuration that receives from the proxy: YAML","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Audit events for a SIEM","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#audit-events-for-a-siem","x":"The audit events are what was done to the proxy's access and settings: a token made or revoked, refused and rate-limited requests, setup-page sign-ins and settings written, secrets moved, and MCP hub tool calls. With `log.audit_lines: on` (or `MAPLE_LOG_AUDIT=on`) each is also written to standard output as one JSON lin","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"What is never exported","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#what-is-never-exported","x":"prompts, answers, files, images, tool definitions, tool arguments or results; error text from a provider (only its class); keys, tokens, header values, setup-page passwords; session ids, conversation ids, request fingerprints and access-token labels in metrics and spans; people and teams, unless `metrics.per_person` is","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Related","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#related","x":"Proxy settings: Observability export Every metrics and telemetry key. See and filter your usage The usage log on your own disk: filters, views and Grafana. Provider health Each provider's and model's state, from the requests already going through. Alerts and forecasts A warning before a budget runs out, and when it wou","m":"both"},{"p":"Choose a model","h":"Choose a model","u":"models.html","x":"Pick the model that answers your messages, switch at any time with `/model`, or let `auto` choose a heavier or lighter one for each message. The list holds what you configured: the models your Copilot plan allows, the ones your Anthropic key can call, and whatever your own model servers have loaded. Switch models at an","m":"single"},{"p":"Choose a model","h":"In plain words","u":"models.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Which model answers your messages. `/model` in the agent shows the list and switches. Let it choose. `/model auto` picks a heavier or lighter model for each message from the same provider, and says what it picked and why. On the setup page. Models & routing , Auto mode , chooses what `auto` means for every ","m":"single"},{"p":"Choose a model","h":"Automatic choice","u":"models.html#automatic-choice","x":"`/model auto` chooses a model for each message from your current provider's own ranking of its models, and tells you what it chose and why. For example, the status bar reads `gpt-4.1 (auto: heavy)` and the first message prints a line such as `model: auto → gpt-4.1 (heavy; no powerful model on this account; used versati","m":"single"},{"p":"Choose a model","h":"Auto in other tools, through the proxy","u":"models.html#auto-in-other-tools-through-the-proxy","x":"The proxy has the same idea built in, for every tool that talks to it: ask for the model `auto` and the proxy chooses a model for each request, or ask for `auto-heavy`, `auto-medium` or `auto-light` to fix the weight. With nothing configured it uses the first provider in `config.yaml` that ranks its models, and never m","m":"single"},{"p":"Choose a model","h":"Copilot Auto","u":"models.html#copilot-auto","x":"GitHub Copilot has an automatic mode of its own, in which Copilot picks the model for each request. The proxy lists it as a model of each Copilot account, named after the account, for example `github-copilot/auto`: In the `/model` list, and on the status bar once chosen, it reads `Copilot Auto (github-copilot)`, with t","m":"single"},{"p":"Choose a model","h":"Copilot Fusion","u":"models.html#copilot-fusion","x":"In plain words. Copilot Fusion is GitHub Copilot's newer automatic mode. Instead of you picking a model, Copilot plans each task: for most tasks one model does the work; for harder ones a model does the work, a second look (a review) checks the result, and a stronger model takes over when the review is not satisfied. Y","m":"single"},{"p":"Choose a model","h":"A default model","u":"models.html#a-default-model","x":"Set the model the agent starts with in `config.yaml`: YAML If the configured model is not one your proxy serves, the agent starts on the first usable model it does serve and prints a one-line note listing the real names.","m":"single"},{"p":"Choose a model","h":"Effort and thinking","u":"models.html#effort-and-thinking","x":"","m":"single"},{"p":"Choose a model","h":"See also","u":"models.html#see-also","x":"Harness settings: Model choice Every model setting, with its default. Fallbacks Another model or account when one cannot answer. See and reduce what a task costs Why long tasks cost what they do, and the settings that help. Connect a model server Ollama, llama.cpp, vLLM, LM Studio and other tools, through the proxy.","m":"single"},{"p":"More API routes: rerank, images, audio, files and pass-through","h":"More API routes: rerank, images, audio, files and pass-through","u":"more-endpoints.html","x":"Besides chat, the proxy's OpenAI-compatible address serves the other routes tools ask for: reranking, moderation, images, audio, files and batches. It can also pass a provider's own API through, for a tool that speaks Anthropic's, Gemini's or OpenAI's native API. Each one goes to a provider that offers it, with the sam","m":"single"},{"p":"More API routes: rerank, images, audio, files and pass-through","h":"In plain words","u":"more-endpoints.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. More of the OpenAI API at the proxy's address (`http://127.0.0.1:11535/v1`): `/v1/rerank`, `/v1/moderations`, `/v1/images/...`, `/v1/audio/...`, `/v1/files` and `/v1/batches`, and Anthropic's `/v1/messages/batches`. Each goes to the provider that serves the model you name. Reranking also works with the buil","m":"single"},{"p":"More API routes: rerank, images, audio, files and pass-through","h":"Which providers","u":"more-endpoints.html#which-providers","x":"A route is sent to the provider that serves the model asked for, at that provider's own address and with its key: Provider type These routes `openai-compatible` and the local servers (vLLM, llama.cpp, LM Studio, Ollama and others), `openrouter`, `xai`, `gemini`, `litellm`, a Maple hub (`maple`) Yes, for whatever the se","m":"single"},{"p":"More API routes: rerank, images, audio, files and pass-through","h":"Rerank","u":"more-endpoints.html#rerank","x":"`POST /v1/rerank` (also `/v2/rerank`) takes the Cohere and Jina request shape and answers in it: Shell `model: \"builtin\"`, the built-in model's own name, or no model ranks with the model set up for search by meaning , on your machine. Its score is how close each document is to the query in meaning: good for ordering, n","m":"single"},{"p":"More API routes: rerank, images, audio, files and pass-through","h":"Moderations, images and audio","u":"more-endpoints.html#moderations-images-and-audio","x":"Route Body Notes `POST /v1/moderations` JSON No `model` means `omni-moderation-latest`, which must then be a name the proxy serves. The built-in model and local classifiers answer on your machine ( below ) `POST /v1/images/generations` JSON `POST /v1/images/edits`, `/v1/images/variations` form upload Up to 64 MB `POST ","m":"single"},{"p":"More API routes: rerank, images, audio, files and pass-through","h":"Moderation on your machine","u":"more-endpoints.html#moderation-on-your-machine","x":"`/v1/moderations` can also be answered without sending the text to a cloud service: by the proxy's built-in guardrails, or by a classification model you run yourself. Off until you set it up, on the setup page ( Models & routing , fold Moderation on this machine , which needs a page password) or with `moderations:` in ","m":"single"},{"p":"More API routes: rerank, images, audio, files and pass-through","h":"Files and batches","u":"more-endpoints.html#files-and-batches","x":"`/v1/files` and `/v1/batches` work as OpenAI's do, on a provider account you name, for example an OpenAI account with `id: openai`: Shell Name the account with `?provider=<id>`, the `X-Maple-Provider` header, or a `provider` field in the upload form. Calls about a file or batch you made (read, download, delete, cancel,","m":"single"},{"p":"More API routes: rerank, images, audio, files and pass-through","h":"Anthropic's Message Batches","u":"more-endpoints.html#anthropics-message-batches","x":"`/v1/messages/batches` works as Anthropic's does, on an Anthropic account with an API key, at both the OpenAI-compatible address and the Anthropic-compatible one (`http://127.0.0.1:11537`), so the Anthropic SDKs' `messages.batches` work pointed at the proxy: Shell Which account. The one serving the requests' models (a ","m":"single"},{"p":"More API routes: rerank, images, audio, files and pass-through","h":"Pass-through routes","u":"more-endpoints.html#pass-through-routes","x":"A pass-through route serves a provider's own API at `/passthrough/<name>/` on the proxy's OpenAI-compatible address. A tool that speaks Anthropic's Messages API, Gemini's `generateContent` or another native API reaches the provider through the proxy: the proxy checks the caller, adds the provider's key, and records wha","m":"single"},{"p":"More API routes: rerank, images, audio, files and pass-through","h":"Bedrock and Vertex","u":"more-endpoints.html#bedrock-and-vertex","x":"A route on a `bedrock` or `vertex` provider reaches one model's calls, signed with that provider's own cloud credentials, exactly as its chat requests are: the proxy signs a Bedrock request with AWS's SigV4 (or sends the provider's Bedrock API key), and gives a Vertex request a Google token. A tool that speaks the clou","m":"single"},{"p":"More API routes: rerank, images, audio, files and pass-through","h":"Technical details","u":"more-endpoints.html#technical-details","x":"Checks, in order: the surface's guards (address, token, same-origin, rate limit), the person's and team's model list and accounts, their rate limits and budgets, then the provider's daily and dollar budgets, exactly as for a chat request for the same model. A request is held to the provider it is admitted for. Size lim","m":"single"},{"p":"More API routes: rerank, images, audio, files and pass-through","h":"See also","u":"more-endpoints.html#see-also","x":"Use your models from other tools Point Claude Code, Codex CLI and other tools at the proxy. What the proxy adds for other tools What Claude Code, Codex CLI, OpenCode and others gain through the proxy. Guardrails Block, mask or log secrets, personal data and your own patterns, at the proxy. Search by meaning A small mod","m":"single"},{"p":"Notes, files, MCP resources and loading tools","h":"Notes, files, MCP resources and loading tools","u":"notes-files-and-resources.html","x":"Four tools for a long run, a busy phone and a big tool box, eh. `notify_person` tells you there is a note from the agent, wherever you are. `send_file` offers a file to the phone or browser you drive the session from. `mcp_resources` reads what your MCP servers offer as resources. And `tool_search` keeps a large set of","m":"single"},{"p":"Notes, files, MCP resources and loading tools","h":"In plain words","u":"notes-files-and-resources.html#in-plain-words","x":"A note when it matters. In a long run you are not watching, the agent can leave you a note: \"the migration is done\", \"the deploy needs your eyes\". Your notifications (desktop, ntfy, a webhook, the bell) and a push to your phone say only \"&lt;project&gt;: note from the agent\" . The note itself is shown only in the sessi","m":"single"},{"p":"Notes, files, MCP resources and loading tools","h":"A note to you: notify_person","u":"notes-files-and-resources.html#a-note-to-you-notify_person","x":"Argument What it does `message` The note, a sentence or two (at most 2000 characters). Shown in the session's transcript only `urgency` `normal` (the default) or `high`: high asks ntfy and the phone's push service to deliver it at once Where the notice goes: Your notification sinks , from `notifications.sinks` and `not","m":"single"},{"p":"Notes, files, MCP resources and loading tools","h":"A file for your phone: send_file","u":"notes-files-and-resources.html#a-file-for-your-phone-send_file","x":"Argument What it does `path` The file to offer `note` A line about what it is, shown on the card What happens: The tool checks that the session is shared and that a device that may drive it is paired. If not, it says why (`this session is not shared with a phone or browser (/remote shares it)`, or `no device that may d","m":"single"},{"p":"Notes, files, MCP resources and loading tools","h":"MCP resources: mcp_resources","u":"notes-files-and-resources.html#mcp-resources-mcp_resources","x":"Argument What it does `action` `list` (the default), `templates` or `read` `server` The MCP server, by its name in your configuration. With only one server offering resources, it is that one `uri` For `read`: the resource's address, as `list` shows it `offset` For `list`: the first entry (100 a page). For `read`: the b","m":"single"},{"p":"Notes, files, MCP resources and loading tools","h":"Loading tools: tool_search","u":"notes-files-and-resources.html#loading-tools-tool_search","x":"With `tools.defer` on (or `auto` and a large set of MCP tools), the requests carry, instead of every MCP tool's full description, one `tool_search` tool whose description lists each of them by name and one line. The agent loads the ones it needs: Argument What it does `query` Exact tool names (comma-separated), or a fe","m":"single"},{"p":"Notes, files, MCP resources and loading tools","h":"Which tools are offered","u":"notes-files-and-resources.html#which-tools-are-offered","x":"Some tools can work only in some sessions. The agent is offered each of them only when it can work, so a small model is not tempted by a tool that would only refuse: Tool Offered when `send_file` A device that may drive the session is paired and the session is shared (`/remote`) `notify_person` A note can reach you: a ","m":"single"},{"p":"Notes, files, MCP resources and loading tools","h":"A tool call written as text","u":"notes-files-and-resources.html#a-tool-call-written-as-text","x":"A small model sometimes writes the call it means to make as text, `write_file(\"hello.txt\", \"violet\")`, and makes no call. When a whole reply is such calls to tools it was offered, the agent is told once, in that message, to make the call itself. Nothing in the text is ever run: the call it then makes goes through your ","m":"single"},{"p":"Notes, files, MCP resources and loading tools","h":"Settings","u":"notes-files-and-resources.html#settings","x":"Setting Default What it does `notifications.agent_notes_per_hour` `6` The most notes `notify_person` sends you in an hour; past it a note stays in the transcript. 1 to 60 `notifications.events` includes `note` Leave `note` out to send no notice of notes anywhere `remote.send_file_max_mb` `10` The largest file `send_fil","m":"single"},{"p":"Notes, files, MCP resources and loading tools","h":"Technical notes","u":"notes-files-and-resources.html#technical-notes","x":"What leaves the machine for a note is the event kind, the project's folder name and, for ntfy, a priority. The note's text exists in the session's transcript (on this machine, and in the paired phone's app over your gateway). A file's bytes are kept in the session's memory and served at the session's own address behind","m":"single"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"See and filter your usage","u":"observability.html","x":"Every request that goes through the proxy is written to your usage log, on your own disk. This page is the guide to using it: asking questions with `maple-proxy usage query`, exporting only the requests you care about, reading it in DuckDB with ready-made views, and the events log that sits next to it. The full referen","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"In plain words","u":"observability.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Every request through the proxy leaves one line in a log on your own disk: which model, how many tokens, how long, how it ended. Never the prompt or the answer. Why you want it. Answer your own questions: what did last week cost, which tool used most, how often did a provider fail. What to do. On the setup ","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"What is recorded, and what never is","u":"observability.html#what-is-recorded-and-what-never-is","x":"One line per request, with counts and labels only: what was asked and who answered: the model the client asked for (`auto`, an alias), the provider and model that answered, and whether a fallback stepped in; how big it was: input, output and cached tokens, and the AI units the provider reported; how long it took: the w","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Ask a question: usage query","u":"observability.html#ask-a-question-usage-query","x":"`maple-proxy usage query` with no options prints the totals for everything in the log. `--group-by` splits them, and the filters narrow them down: Shell Grouped by `tag`, a request counts under each of its tags (one without tags is `-`), and once in the total: with requests that carry several tags, the rows add up to m","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Filters","u":"observability.html#filters","x":"Every filter takes one value or several (comma-separated, or the flag repeated) and matches any of them. Different filters must all match. Ask about Flags A time range `--since`, `--until` (a UTC day such as `2026-10-01`, or a time; `--until` is exclusive), or `--last 7d` (whole days, from midnight in the proxy's time ","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Choose the figures","u":"observability.html#choose-the-figures","x":"`--metrics` picks the columns. The default is requests, errors, the four token counts that matter most, AI units, the list-price estimate and the 95th percentile of latency; `maple-proxy usage query --help` lists them all. Some useful ones: Metric What it is `error_rate`, `fallbacks`, `cached_responses` How often thing","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Table, JSON or CSV","u":"observability.html#table-json-or-csv","x":"`--format table` (the default) is for reading. `--format json` gives the same groups and totals with a `meta` part that says what was read, for a script; `--format csv` is for a spreadsheet: Shell CSV","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"See what it read","u":"observability.html#see-what-it-read","x":"`--timing` adds a line after the answer: how long it took, how many daily files it read, how many came from the daily summaries (see below ), how much it read, and how many lines matched. It goes to standard error, so it never ends up in a file you redirect the answer into. Time to first token and time on the provider,","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Notes on every answer","u":"observability.html#notes-on-every-answer","x":"Every answer ends with notes on what its figures cannot show: that percentiles are approximate, that figures named `_estimated` are estimates, that some matching lines were written before a column existed (and so have no time to first token, for example), or that a line could not be read. Read them before you compare t","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Export only what you need","u":"observability.html#export-only-what-you-need","x":"`usage export` takes the same filters, so the file holds only the matching requests: Shell Exports read and write a day at a time, so a long range does not have to fit in memory. Without a filter, an export is exactly what it was before this release. Formats and columns: Export to CSV or Parquet . `usage status` takes ","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Ready-made views in DuckDB","u":"observability.html#ready-made-views-in-duckdb","x":"For your own questions in SQL, DuckDB reads the log directly. `--views` adds a set of named views, in a schema of their own called `maple`, so they never replace a view of yours: Shell View One row per Good for `maple.requests` request Every column, plus the UTC `day` and the `outcome` `maple.daily_spend` day, provider","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Lots of history? Use the DuckDB file or monthly Parquet","u":"observability.html#lots-of-history-use-the-duckdb-file-or-monthly-parquet","x":"The views above read one daily file per day. Over months of history, or on a hub that keeps what the proxies below it send up, that is many files. Pack them instead: Shell Both hold exactly what the daily files hold, and both follow the cleanup. Set Analytics files on the Usage data card to have the proxy refresh them ","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Several pods? Query Postgres","u":"observability.html#several-pods-query-postgres","x":"In central mode the usage of every replica is in PostgreSQL. The same views over it, the connection string read from an environment variable when you query: Shell See Several pods? .","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Analytics with DuckLake","u":"observability.html#analytics-with-ducklake","x":"A DuckLake keeps your usage history as Parquet data files and a catalog, with snapshots you can read as they were, long after the daily files' retention has removed them. `maple-proxy usage lake` makes one and keeps it current with your own `duckdb` command line; the proxy never installs or downloads DuckDB. On one mac","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"The events log","u":"observability.html#the-events-log","x":"Things that happen that are not requests go to their own daily files, `events-YYYY-MM-DD.jsonl`, in the same folder as the usage log. They follow the usage log: on when it is on, off when it is off, and kept for the same number of days. The usage files still hold requests only, so counting their lines still counts requ","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Why the cache missed","u":"observability.html#why-the-cache-missed","x":"When a provider's prompt cache misses, the usage log says why in `cache_reason`, and since this release one level finer in `cache_detail`. `maple-proxy usage cache-misses` sums it up: how often the cache missed, by client, client version and person, the most common reasons, and an estimated cost of the misses. It takes","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Compare before and after","u":"observability.html#compare-before-and-after","x":"`maple-proxy usage compare` sets two windows of your usage side by side, per request, to see what changed after you changed a setting: Shell Every answer says measured on your traffic, observational, not a controlled test , and means it. Two weeks differ in more than one setting: the work, the provider's caching and pr","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"How auto did","u":"observability.html#how-auto-did","x":"`maple-proxy usage auto-report` says how requests routed by `auto` turned out, per weight and model: how often they failed, needed a fallback, were sent again within a minute, or were followed by you switching model (Maple Harness tells the proxy when you switch with `/model`; other clients do not), and how long their ","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"The daily summaries","u":"observability.html#the-daily-summaries","x":"To answer quickly over months of data, `usage query` keeps a summary of each finished UTC day in your user cache folder: `~/.cache/maple-harness/usage-index/` on Linux (or under `$XDG_CACHE_HOME`), `~/Library/Caches/maple-harness/usage-index/` on macOS. They are private to you, and they are never put next to the usage ","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"What it cannot tell you yet","u":"observability.html#what-it-cannot-tell-you-yet","x":"The newer columns start with this release. Time to first token, time on the provider, the error class, the routing reason and the client's version are recorded from schema version 4 on. Lines written by earlier releases have them empty (NULL), never 0, and an answer's notes say how many matching lines are like that. Pe","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Related","u":"observability.html#related","x":"Your usage data The usage log's columns, exports and DuckDB views. Query your usage A cookbook of questions about your usage, each with its command. Your data Where your usage data lives, and which store to query. The Maple data lake Years of usage history in DuckLake, on one machine or for a team. Proxy settings: The ","m":"both"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"What the proxy adds for other tools","u":"other-tools.html","x":"Claude Code, OpenCode, pi, GitHub Copilot CLI, Hermes and Codex CLI can send their requests through the Maple proxy instead of straight to a provider. Use your models from other tools has a tested setup for each. This page says what they get from it, and what stays in Maple Harness. Other tools that speak the OpenAI or","m":"single"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"In plain words","u":"other-tools.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Claude Code, OpenCode, pi, Copilot CLI, Hermes and Codex can send their requests through the Maple proxy instead of straight to a provider. Other tools that let you change their model address can too. Why you want it. They get every model the proxy serves, its fallbacks when an account runs out, its budgets","m":"single"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"At a glance","u":"other-tools.html#at-a-glance","x":"What each tool sent was checked with Claude Code 2.1.284, OpenCode 1.18.33, pi 0.87.1, Copilot CLI 1.0.89 and Hermes Agent 0.19.0; Codex CLI 0.170.0 was checked for tool calls, streaming and the usage log. Other versions may send different headers or cache marks. Claude Code OpenCode pi Copilot CLI Hermes Codex Every m","m":"single"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"One address for many models","u":"other-tools.html#one-address-for-many-models","x":"The tool asks for a model by name, and the proxy sends the request to whichever provider serves it: your GitHub Copilot account, an Anthropic account, or a model server such as Ollama, LM Studio or oMLX. The proxy translates between the two APIs in both directions. Claude Code, which speaks the Anthropic API, can use a","m":"single"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"Keeps going when an account runs out","u":"other-tools.html#keeps-going-when-an-account-runs-out","x":"Fallbacks apply to every request through the proxy, whatever tool sent it. With `model_fallbacks`, a model that cannot answer (out of chats or credit, overloaded, unreachable) hands the request to the next name on its list. `provider_fallbacks` moves one account's requests to another account. A model that has just said","m":"single"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"Budgets","u":"other-tools.html#budgets","x":"A daily limit on a provider (requests, AI units, or both) counts every request to that provider through the proxy, whichever tool sent it. At the limit, requests are refused or handed to a fallback. See Daily budgets .","m":"single"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"See what each tool spends","u":"other-tools.html#see-what-each-tool-spends","x":"Every request through the proxy gets one line in the usage log: the model asked for and the model that answered, tokens in and out, tokens read from and written to the cache, AI units when the provider reports them, and estimated cost and savings columns. The log keeps counts and labels, never prompts or answers. You c","m":"single"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"Prompt cache","u":"other-tools.html#prompt-cache","x":"Providers charge less for the start of a request they have already seen. For Claude models, reached through an Anthropic account or through Copilot, the provider caches only the parts of a request that are marked for caching. The proxy marks them (`prompt_cache`, on by default): Claude Code marks its own system prompt.","m":"single"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"Settings you can turn on","u":"other-tools.html#settings-you-can-turn-on","x":"These are off by default. When on, they apply to every tool. Image shrinking (`image_resize_tool`, `image_resize_user`, `image_superseded`) sends smaller images. Small text in a screenshot may become harder to read. Snapshot trimming (`snapshot_trim`) shortens browser page snapshots from Playwright MCP. It only acts on","m":"single"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"Using it from another machine","u":"other-tools.html#using-it-from-another-machine","x":"To reach the proxy from another machine, give the tool an access token as its API key, in place of the placeholder. The token's label then names the tool in the usage log's `client` column. The setup page itself is reachable only from the proxy's own machine, and you can protect it with a password . See Using it from a","m":"single"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"What stays in Maple Harness","u":"other-tools.html#what-stays-in-maple-harness","x":"These belong to the agent, not the proxy. Pointing another tool at the proxy does not give it any of them: `history_search` over earlier conversations, and the session journal of what each turn read, changed and ran. Memory notes and the project index as the agent uses them: recalled during a turn, and kept current aft","m":"single"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"Tools without a tested setup","u":"other-tools.html#tools-without-a-tested-setup","x":"In plain words: Cursor, Gemini CLI, Qwen Code and Crush were not run against the proxy for this guide. What follows is what the proxy offers each of them and what to give the tool. Where a tool keeps these values, and what its settings are called, is in its own documentation. If you try one, the Check it steps and the ","m":"single"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"Orchestrators","u":"other-tools.html#orchestrators","x":"An orchestrator is a program that starts agents in terminal panes, hands each one a task, watches whether it is working, waiting for you or done, and carries on from there. MAPLE is one; herdr hosts the panes and shows their state. Maple Harness has no code for any one orchestrator: it offers the same commands and file","m":"single"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"Measure it yourself","u":"other-tools.html#measure-it-yourself","x":"The measured comparison on this website is between Maple Harness and Copilot CLI. What the proxy changes for these tools on their own has not been measured, so this page gives no figure. To measure it on your own work, use `maple-harness bench` . It can run another tool as one arm, with the same tasks and the same chec","m":"single"},{"p":"Plan mode","h":"Plan mode","u":"plan-mode.html","x":"Measure twice, cut once. In plan mode the agent looks around the project and writes a plan, and it cannot change anything while it does. When the plan is ready it hands it to you: you approve it and the work starts, ask for more planning, or edit the plan yourself first. The short version","m":"single"},{"p":"Plan mode","h":"In plain words","u":"plan-mode.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. A mode where the agent only looks: it reads files, searches, asks the project's index and asks you questions, then writes a plan. Everything that would change something (writing a file, running a command, opening a terminal pane, starting another agent, sending a message to one) is held back until you appro","m":"single"},{"p":"Plan mode","h":"What you see","u":"plan-mode.html#what-you-see","x":"In the terminal, the dialog is the same one as the agent's questions : the plan sits beside the options on a wide terminal (`p` hides it), and behind `p` on a narrow one. `1`, `2`, `3` or the arrow keys and Enter choose; `n` adds a note; Esc , then y , closes it without approving (plan mode stays on). The plan beside i","m":"single"},{"p":"Plan mode","h":"Commands and settings","u":"plan-mode.html#commands-and-settings","x":"Command or setting What it does `/plan`, `/plan on` Plan mode on, from the next message `/plan off` Plan mode off: the tools that change things are back `/plan show` The plan approved last, again `harness.approvals.timeout` How long a plan waits for your answer (10 minutes by default; `off` waits as long as it takes). ","m":"single"},{"p":"Plan mode","h":"Your MCP servers in plan mode","u":"plan-mode.html#your-mcp-servers-in-plan-mode","x":"In plain words. Maple cannot tell what another program's tools do, so while planning it holds back every MCP server's tools. If a server only looks (documentation search, an issue tracker you only read, a read-only database view), mark it read-only and plan mode offers its tools. YAML Technically. `read_only: true` is ","m":"single"},{"p":"Plan mode","h":"Technical details","u":"plan-mode.html#technical-details","x":"","m":"single"},{"p":"Plan mode","h":"What plan mode offers","u":"plan-mode.html#what-plan-mode-offers","x":"Plan mode offers a fixed list of tools that only look, and holds back everything else, including any tool added later that has not been put on the list: Offered Withheld `read_file`, `grep`, `glob`, `graph_search`, `graph_connections`, `history_search`, `recall`, `skill`, the language servers' `lsp_*` look-ups (includi","m":"single"},{"p":"Plan mode","h":"`plan_ready`","u":"plan-mode.html#plan_ready","x":"The agent calls it with the whole plan (Markdown, at most 64 KB) and an optional one-line `summary`. It gets back JSON: `status` `plan_mode` Meaning `approved` `off` You approved it (or your edited plan, returned in `plan`). `added` says which tasks it put on the lineup `keep_planning` `on` You want more planning; your","m":"single"},{"p":"Plan mode","h":"`plan_enter`","u":"plan-mode.html#plan_enter","x":"The agent calls it with a one-line `reason`. You are asked; Keep going keeps it working as it was. A skipped question, or nobody answering in time, switches to plan mode, and the conversation says how it was decided. With nobody to ask at all, it is refused (`\"status\": \"refused\"`, `plan_mode` `off`, with the reason) an","m":"single"},{"p":"Plan mode","h":"See also","u":"plan-mode.html#see-also","x":"Questions the agent asks you The dialog plan mode's choices use, and where else you can answer. Harness settings `harness.approvals.timeout`, which a plan waits by. Commands Every slash command, `/plan` among them. MCP servers Connecting servers, and marking one read-only.","m":"single"},{"p":"Check it yourself","h":"Check it yourself","u":"proof.html","x":"Maple Harness is built to make a task cost fewer tokens without making the work worse. This page says what that means, how to check it on your own tasks, and what we hold ourselves to. It makes no promise about your bill: that depends on your tasks and your models. The short version","m":"single"},{"p":"Check it yourself","h":"In plain words","u":"proof.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. How to check, on your own tasks, whether a setting saves tokens without making the work worse. What to do. Watch `/cost` and the setup page's Cache tab, then compare runs with `maple-harness bench`. Nothing here is a promise about your bill.","m":"single"},{"p":"Check it yourself","h":"What we aim for","u":"proof.html#what-we-aim-for","x":"Efficient. Maple Harness is built to spend fewer tokens for the same work: it keeps requests in a form your provider can reuse, keeps the conversation lean, and stops waste before it compounds. Efficacious. It does not trade quality for tokens: it does not ask the model to talk in shorthand, what the model sees is cut ","m":"single"},{"p":"Check it yourself","h":"Don't take our word for it","u":"proof.html#dont-take-our-word-for-it","x":"Please be diligent. A saving that only shows up in one request, or one run, is not a saving. Here is how to check. Compare whole runs, three against three. Pick a task you run often. Run it three times with a setting on and three times with it off, for example trimming of old tool output, or letting the agent choose th","m":"single"},{"p":"Check it yourself","h":"What we hold ourselves to","u":"proof.html#what-we-hold-ourselves-to","x":"We publish results only from repeated, paired runs on real tasks, including the runs where a setting did not help, with the uncertainty shown. A technique is on by default only if quality is not worse. Nothing here is a promise about your bill.","m":"single"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Keep it running in the background","u":"proxy-service.html","x":"By default the agent starts the proxy for you when you open it, and it stops when you are done. Run the proxy as a service when you want it to be already there: before you open a terminal, for other tools that use your models, or for your phone. `maple-harness service install` does it in one command. It writes one smal","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"In plain words","u":"proxy-service.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. A way to keep the proxy (and the phone gateway) running in the background, started when you log in, instead of only while the agent is open. Why you want it. Your phone, Claude Code or another tool can use your models without a Maple session open. What to do. `maple-harness service install`, once. `maple-ha","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"In short","u":"proxy-service.html#in-short","x":"`maple-harness service` keeps the proxy and the phone gateway running in the background and starts them when you log in. On Linux it writes systemd user units; on macOS, launchd agents . Shell You do not need this to use the agent: it starts the proxy itself, and `/remote` starts the gateway. Install a service when you","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"What you get","u":"proxy-service.html#what-you-get","x":"Linux macOS The file it writes `~/.config/systemd/user/maple-proxy.service` `~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.kinncj.maple-proxy.plist` Starts when you log in (at boot too, after one extra command below) when you log in If it stops with an error restarted 3 seconds later restarted 5 seconds later Logs `journalctl --user -u m","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Debian and Ubuntu","u":"proxy-service.html#debian-and-ubuntu","x":"Tested on: written from how the command works and standard systemd behaviour; not yet run on Debian or Ubuntu. Install the programs. Shell This puts them in `/usr/local/bin` and asks for `sudo` once. To avoid `sudo`, add `-s -- --install-location ~/.local/bin` after `bash` and make sure `~/.local/bin` is on your `PATH`","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Arch Linux","u":"proxy-service.html#arch-linux","x":"Tested on: Arch Linux. The service files it writes pass `systemd-analyze verify`. The install and enable steps themselves have not been run on a real machine yet. Install the programs. Shell Optional: shell sandbox. Shell Install the service. Shell Check it. Shell Start at boot, not only at login. Shell Arch has no fir","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Fedora","u":"proxy-service.html#fedora","x":"Tested on: written from how the command works and standard systemd behaviour; not yet run on Fedora. Install the programs. Shell Optional: shell sandbox. Shell Install the service. Shell Check it. Shell Start at boot, not only at login. Shell SELinux. A user service that runs a program from `/usr/local/bin` or `~/.loca","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"macOS","u":"proxy-service.html#macos","x":"Tested on: written from how the command works and standard launchd behaviour; not yet run on a Mac. Install the programs. Shell The programs are not notarised by Apple. The installer downloads them with `curl`, which normally does not trigger Gatekeeper. If macOS still refuses to open one, remove the quarantine mark fr","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Keep the phone gateway running too","u":"proxy-service.html#keep-the-phone-gateway-running-too","x":"The gateway is the address your phone connects to ( Control it from your phone ). To keep it up as well: Shell This also switches remote control on and starts the gateway listening on your network (port 11541) with HTTPS: a real certificate when Tailscale is signed in, otherwise a self-signed one that works after a bro","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Machines with no desktop","u":"proxy-service.html#machines-with-no-desktop","x":"Your sign-ins and keys go in the system keyring when there is one, and the configuration file holds only a reference to them. A server usually has no keyring: no Secret Service is running on your user session, or it cannot be unlocked without someone at the keyboard. Then the proxy falls back to a private file in the c","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Reach the setup page from another machine","u":"proxy-service.html#reach-the-setup-page-from-another-machine","x":"The setup page listens on `127.0.0.1` only and refuses to listen anywhere else, so do not try to expose it. To use it on a server, forward the port over SSH and open it in your own browser: Shell Then browse to `http://127.0.0.1:11539` on your own machine. Over Tailscale the same forward works with the machine's Tailsc","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Update","u":"proxy-service.html#update","x":"Each program updates itself, then the service needs a restart to run the new copy: Shell If `update` says permission denied for `/usr/local/bin`, run it with `sudo`, or install into `~/.local/bin` the first time. If you moved the programs, run `maple-harness service install` again so the file points at the new path.","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Troubleshooting","u":"proxy-service.html#troubleshooting","x":"`service install` says the systemd user manager is not reachable. There is no user session to attach to, typically over SSH or in a container. Log in through a normal session, or follow the SSH steps in the Debian and Ubuntu section. `maple-harness service print proxy` shows the file it would write so you can place it ","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Remove it","u":"proxy-service.html#remove-it","x":"Shell This stops the services and removes the files it wrote. It leaves your configuration and sign-ins alone. On Linux, if you turned lingering on and no longer want it: `loginctl disable-linger \"$USER\"`.","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Advanced: one system-wide service","u":"proxy-service.html#advanced-one-system-wide-service","x":"Tested on: the unit text was checked with `systemd-analyze verify` on Arch Linux. Nothing else here has been run. Use the per-user service above unless you need this. A per-user service is the right default. If you want the proxy to run under its own account, for example on a server that nobody logs in to, you can writ","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Proxy settings","u":"proxy-settings.html","x":"This is the complete list of what you can change on the proxy: the cards on its setup page, the keys in `config.yaml`, the commands, and the response headers you can read. For a guided tour, read The proxy and its setup page first. The proxy and the harness share one `config.yaml`. A save on the setup page keeps the `h","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"In plain words","u":"proxy-settings.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. The complete list of the proxy's settings, cards, commands and response headers. What to do. On the setup page you rarely need this page: each card explains itself, Advanced lists every setting with its default and when it applies, and Find a setting finds one by a word. Come here for the exact key, the def","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Settings at a glance","u":"proxy-settings.html#settings-at-a-glance","x":"On the setup page, the Proxy settings and Auto mode cards are on the Models & routing tab, and Providers is its own tab. Every key in this table is also listed on Advanced , which edits the ones that have no card of their own and says where to change the rest. Find a setting , in the page's menu, finds any of them by n","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Listeners and ports","u":"proxy-settings.html#listeners-and-ports","x":"Key Default What it is for `server.openai_compat` `127.0.0.1:11535` The OpenAI-compatible API (`/v1`). The harness, and any tool that speaks that API, use it `server.anthropic_compat` `127.0.0.1:11537` The Anthropic-compatible API. Claude Code and similar tools use it `server.webui` `127.0.0.1:11539` The setup page. It","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Providers and models","u":"proxy-settings.html#providers-and-models","x":"A provider is a backend that serves models. The setup page's Providers card builds these for you; this is what the keys mean if you edit the file. Key Default What it does `providers[].id` required The provider's name, for example `github-copilot` or `ollama-main` `providers[].type` required What it is: `github-copilot","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Model server requests","u":"proxy-settings.html#model-server-requests","x":"In plain words: a model server you run often understands more request settings than the standard OpenAI ones, such as vLLM's and SGLang's `min_tokens`, `top_k`, `repetition_penalty`, `guided_json` or `chat_template_kwargs`. The proxy now sends those on to the server exactly as your tool wrote them, so a tool that sets ","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Caching","u":"proxy-settings.html#caching","x":"Long conversations resend the same beginning on every turn. Providers that cache charge far less for the part they have already seen. These settings help that happen; the Cache card shows whether it did. Key Setup page Default What it does Trade-off Helps `providers[].prompt_cache` Caching, a switch per provider `auto`","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Images and browser snapshots","u":"proxy-settings.html#images-and-browser-snapshots","x":"A model is charged for an image by its size in pixels, not by its file size, and a large image is scaled down by the provider before the model looks at it. Browser tools also return page snapshots (a text outline of the page) that are often the largest thing an agent resends. These settings shrink both. All of them are","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Cost visibility","u":"proxy-settings.html#cost-visibility","x":"Copilot states what a call cost, in AI units, on every response. Anthropic's own API never does — it reports token counts only. `/cost` (see What /cost tells you ) still shows a per-request figure for Anthropic when this is on, but it is a guess from Anthropic's published prices, not a bill, and is always shown labelle","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Fallbacks","u":"proxy-settings.html#fallbacks","x":"Key Setup page Default What it does Trade-off Helps `model_fallbacks` Fallbacks none For a model or alias, the names to try in order when it cannot answer: its account is out of chats or credit, the backend is overloaded, or it is unreachable A fallback may be a weaker or a different model, so results can differ. It ha","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Model groups","u":"proxy-settings.html#model-groups","x":"A model group serves one name from several deployments: the same model on several accounts, regions or local servers. Callers ask for the group's name; each request goes to one deployment, and the next takes over when it cannot answer. YAML Which deployment first. `round-robin` (the default) takes them in turn; `ordere","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Auto rules","u":"proxy-settings.html#auto-rules","x":"Your own rules for the proxy's `auto` (see Your own rules for auto ). Both are read on the next request after a save; neither needs a restart. Key Default What it does Helps `auto_keywords[].words` required Words or short phrases (up to 64 characters each, no quotes, up to 50 a rule). A request for `auto` whose last me","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Concurrency","u":"proxy-settings.html#concurrency","x":"How many requests one provider's server works on at once, and which waiting request goes next (see How many requests a model server takes at once ). Keyed by provider id. An entry always wins. A provider without one is asked (a local server's own count), and otherwise learned: nothing waits until the server answers bus","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Pass-through routes","u":"proxy-settings.html#pass-through-routes","x":"`passthrough:` serves a provider's own API at `/passthrough/<name>/` on the OpenAI-compatible address, with the proxy's checks and the provider's key. Off unless set, on the setup page ( Providers , Pass-through routes , which needs a page password) or in `config.yaml`. See Pass-through routes . Key Meaning `passthroug","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Moderation on this machine","u":"proxy-settings.html#moderation-on-this-machine","x":"`moderations:` answers `/v1/moderations` on this machine: the model `builtin` (built-in guardrails) and local classification servers. Off unless set, on the setup page ( Models & routing , Moderation on this machine ) or in `config.yaml`. See Moderation on your machine . Key Meaning `moderations.builtin` The built-in g","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Guards","u":"proxy-settings.html#guards","x":"Key Setup page Default What it does Trade-off Helps `context_guard` Guards, a three-way choice `off` For tools that do not know a model's window: `off` sends the request as it is, `truncate` drops the oldest whole turns until it fits, `error` refuses it with a plain message `truncate` silently forgets old turns; `error","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Guardrails","u":"proxy-settings.html#guardrails","x":"Checks on what a request, its answer and MCP hub tool calls hold: secrets, personal data, banned terms, injected instructions, size, your own service or a provider's. None until configured. The whole guide, with examples: Guardrails . Key Setup page Default What it does `guardrails[].name`, `guardrails[].type` Models &","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Budgets","u":"proxy-settings.html#budgets","x":"Key Setup page Default What it does Trade-off Helps `budgets[].provider` Budgets none Which provider the limit applies to Efficient `budgets[].max_requests_per_day` Budgets none (no limit) The most requests a day for that provider (days are UTC) When reached, requests are refused, or handed to that provider's fallback ","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Alerts","u":"proxy-settings.html#alerts","x":"Alerts only warn: they never refuse, reroute or slow a request (budgets do the refusing). Each alert fires once per rule and period, is written to the events log, and is sent to clients in `X-Maple-Alert` while it is in force; Maple Harness shows it once. With no budget and nothing set here, there are no alerts. See Al","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Investigations","u":"proxy-settings.html#investigations","x":"Scheduled, read-only checks over the usage log that find recurring errors, loops, cost spikes, cache regressions and an account failing or slowing more than it used to, shown as findings on the Health tab. Off until enabled. They never change a request and never read a prompt or an answer. See Investigations . Key Setu","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"People and teams","u":"proxy-settings.html#people-and-teams","x":"All optional, and off until set: without them every request is served as before. See Teams . Key Setup page Default What it does Trade-off Helps `people[].id` People none A person, as the usage log's `person` column names them. `local` is a caller on this machine without a token Efficacious `people[].team` People none ","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Access control","u":"proxy-settings.html#access-control","x":"All optional, and off until set. Set on the setup page ( People & teams , card Access control ; the two switches on Advanced ), in `config.yaml`, or through the management API. On the page they need a page password. See Access control . Key Setup page Default What it does Trade-off Helps `organizations[].id` People & t","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Chained proxies","u":"proxy-settings.html#chained-proxies","x":"All optional: a proxy takes part in a chain only once it has a provider of type `maple` or a `proxy-chain` token. See Chain proxies into hubs . Key Setup page Default What it does Trade-off Helps `providers[].type: maple` Providers none Another Maple proxy used as a provider (a hub). `base_url` is its OpenAI-compatible","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"MCP hub","u":"proxy-settings.html#mcp-hub","x":"Off until `mcp_hub.enabled` is set; while off nothing listens and nothing starts. See MCP hub . Key Setup page Default What it does Trade-off Helps `mcp_hub.enabled` MCP hub, the switch off Serves the configured MCP servers' tools at one MCP endpoint A bridge from that endpoint to programs on this machine Efficient `mc","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Limits and hosts","u":"proxy-settings.html#limits-and-hosts","x":"These are read when the proxy starts. After changing them, stop and start `maple-proxy`. Key Setup page Default What it does Helps `server.rate_limit.enabled` Limits and hosts unset: off for this machine, on for other addresses Force the limit on or off Efficacious `server.rate_limit.requests_per_minute` Limits and hos","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"The usage log","u":"proxy-settings.html#the-usage-log","x":"Key Setup page Default What it does Helps `server.usage_ledger` Usage data, Location (or Limits and hosts, Usage log) on: `usage/maple_harness_proxy_usage_ledger.jsonl` in your Maple Harness config folder Where the usage log goes: a file that receives one line of numbers per request (model, tokens, cache figures, AI un","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Search by meaning","u":"proxy-settings.html#search-by-meaning","x":"Which model ranks the harness's searches by meaning. See Search by meaning for what it does, both models, their licences (EmbeddingGemma is under the Gemma Terms of Use and the Gemma Prohibited Use Policy , and its download needs a Hugging Face token from an account that accepted them) and hardware guidance: EmbeddingG","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"The setup page, card by card","u":"proxy-settings.html#the-setup-page-card-by-card","x":"Card What it shows and does Account (Providers tab) Sign in to GitHub Copilot (a browser flow) or save an Anthropic API key. The Copilot plan and, where the plan has one, the chats left and when they reset Providers (Providers tab) The configured backends and their models. Add a server, press Detect models , edit or de","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Single sign-on for the setup page","u":"proxy-settings.html#single-sign-on-for-the-setup-page","x":"For teams: admins sign in to the setup page with their company account, on an address of its own, and developers too when developer sign-in is on. Off unless `setup_page.sso.enabled` is true. Set on the Single sign-on tab's wizard on the proxy's own machine (never through single sign-on), or in `config.yaml`. Read at s","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Observability export","u":"proxy-settings.html#observability-export","x":"Prometheus metrics, an OpenTelemetry (OTLP) export and audit lines for a SIEM: counts, timings and labels, never content. All off by default; the setup page's Monitoring tab sets them up step by step, applied without a restart. The full guide, with what is exported and what never is: Metrics and telemetry export . Key ","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Content export and callbacks","u":"proxy-settings.html#content-export-and-callbacks","x":"Off until set. The prompts and answers of the people and teams you name, as OpenTelemetry GenAI spans to a collector of their own, and each request's usage line to webhooks, Langfuse, Datadog or object storage. The guide: Content export and callbacks . Key Setup page Default What it does Helps `content_export.endpoint`","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Containers and replicas","u":"proxy-settings.html#containers-and-replicas","x":"For running the proxy in a container or as several replicas; see Docker and Kubernetes . They are read when the proxy starts, and none of them is on the setup page: `storage` names where secrets come from and is never shown there. The environment variable in brackets wins over the file. Key Setup page Default What it d","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Commands","u":"proxy-settings.html#commands","x":"`maple-proxy` with no command starts the server. The others: Command What it does `maple-proxy serve` Start the proxy (the default). Flags: `-config` a config file; `-logs` where logs go (`false`, `true`, `stdout`, or a file path); `-log-bodies` also log prompt and answer text, which is off by default because a prompt ","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Response headers","u":"proxy-settings.html#response-headers","x":"The proxy adds these to responses so you can see what happened to a request. Header Meaning `X-Maple-First-Output-Timeout` On the model list (`GET /v1/models`): the longest time, in seconds, the proxy waits for any listed model's provider to start answering (`providers[].first_output_timeout_seconds`). The proxy sends ","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Files and environment","u":"proxy-settings.html#files-and-environment","x":"Where What `version` The configuration file's format version, currently `1` `~/.config/maple-harness/config.yaml` The proxy's and the harness's settings `~/.config/maple-harness/embeddings/` The installed built-in model for search by meaning, one folder per model, and the cache of computed vectors in `cache/`, private ","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"See also","u":"proxy-settings.html#see-also","x":"Commands and keys Everything you can type in a session, every key, and the shell commands. Harness settings Every `harness.*` setting, with its default and its trade-off. Settings by goal The settings that serve efficiency, efficacy and effectiveness. The proxy and its setup page The proxy's ports, its setup page, and ","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"The proxy and its setup page","u":"proxy.html","x":"`maple-proxy` is the program that holds your sign-ins and talks to the models. The agent uses it, and so can any other tool that speaks the OpenAI or Anthropic API: one place for your accounts, fallbacks when one runs out, and a record of what every tool spent, with a setup page in your browser to run it all. Start it ","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"In plain words","u":"proxy.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. The proxy is the one program that holds your sign-ins (GitHub Copilot, Claude, your own model servers) and talks to the models. Maple Harness uses it, and so can Claude Code, OpenCode or any tool that speaks the OpenAI or Anthropic API. Why you want it. One place for your accounts and models; when one accou","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"What it listens on","u":"proxy.html#what-it-listens-on","x":"Address What it is Who may use it `127.0.0.1:11539` The setup page This machine only. It refuses to listen anywhere else `127.0.0.1:11535` OpenAI-compatible API (`/v1`) This machine; other machines only with an access token `127.0.0.1:11537` Anthropic-compatible API This machine; other machines only with an access toke","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"The setup page","u":"proxy.html#the-setup-page","x":"The setup page is the proxy's control panel, in your browser. Open `http://127.0.0.1:11539` on the machine running the proxy. Nothing to install, and it only answers on this machine. Watch tells you what is happening: how many requests, what they cost, how the cache and each provider are doing. Configure is where you c","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"The menu","u":"proxy.html#the-menu","x":"The tabs are in two groups. The address keeps the tab you are on (`#tab-usage`), so a tab can be bookmarked, and the arrow keys move between tabs. Group Tab What it holds Watch Overview Live tiles (requests per second, in flight, today's requests and AI units, the cache hit rate, failures in the last hour), a live char","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"What's configured","u":"proxy.html#whats-configured","x":"At the bottom of the Overview, What's configured lists the basics, each on , off or needs attention , with a link to where it is set: providers, auto mode, the usage log, budgets and alerts, search by meaning, remote control and the page password. Features you set up (sharing, hubs, single sign-on, monitoring, central ","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Set up more","u":"proxy.html#set-up-more","x":"Set up more is where the features one person does not need begin: Share this proxy with a team , Connect to a hub, or serve other proxies , Single sign-on for admins , Monitoring and alert webhooks , Several replicas (central storage) and MCP hub . Each card says whether it is on and opens its own tab, with a step-by-s","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Advanced, and saved settings that wait for a restart","u":"proxy.html#advanced-and-saved-settings-that-wait-for-a-restart","x":"Advanced shows every setting the proxy has, grouped by tab, with a filter box. The ones without a card of their own (guards, caching, alert rules, where the proxy listens and its limits, trusted load balancers, chaining intervals, log format and level, image limits) can be changed there. Each says when it applies (at o","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Charts and live figures","u":"proxy.html#charts-and-live-figures","x":"Every chart says whether its figures are measured or estimated , prints the notes the proxy gives with them (how many requests and daily files it read, and what the figures cannot show), and has a Table button that shows every number. Hover over a chart, or focus it and use the arrow keys, to read each point; click a l","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"What you can do from the Configure tabs","u":"proxy.html#what-you-can-do-from-the-configure-tabs","x":"Sign in to GitHub Copilot (a browser flow) or save an Anthropic API key, on Providers . See Sign in . Add model servers on Providers : pick the type (Ollama, LM Studio, oMLX, Lemonade, Unsloth, or any OpenAI-compatible server), press Detect models , and save. See Connect a model server . See your Copilot allowance : th","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Filter the charts and cards","u":"proxy.html#filter-the-charts-and-cards","x":"Above the data tabs, pick a time range: Live (the default), Today , 7, 30 or 90 days, a year, or Custom for a range of UTC days. Live is today, as it happens: every card and chart covers today, from midnight, local time (the same span as Today , so the two always agree), and updates as requests arrive. The day buttons ","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Provider health","u":"proxy.html#provider-health","x":"On the Health tab, the charts show the time to an answer and to the first output per day (the median, with the 95th percentile as a band), failures by class, and the share of each model's requests that failed. The Health card lists each provider and model that has answered through this proxy since it started: its state","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Alerts and forecasts","u":"proxy.html#alerts-and-forecasts","x":"On the Costs & budgets tab, the charts show AI units and the estimated list-price cost per day (with a line at an alert amount you set) and today's use of each budget, with a typical day once there are three days of usage. The Alerts card warns when a budget reaches 80% and 100% (the default), and, if you set them unde","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Compare two ranges","u":"proxy.html#compare-two-ranges","x":"On the Costs & budgets tab, Compare two ranges sets the days before and after a change you made and compares cost and tokens per request between them, on your own traffic, narrowed by the filter bar. Requests are compared like for like (the same kind of client, model and size of work), and the answer refuses rather tha","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"People and teams","u":"proxy.html#people-and-teams","x":"To share one proxy with other people, open Set up more > Share this proxy with a team and add a person. The People & teams tab then stays in the menu, with charts of AI units by person and requests by team, each budget's use today and this month, and the People card: each person, their team, their tokens (by label, nev","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"MCP hub","u":"proxy.html#mcp-hub","x":"Once `mcp_hub:` is set up in `config.yaml` (or with `maple-proxy mcp-hub add`), the MCP hub tab shows whether the hub is on, its address, and each server with its state and tools. You can switch the hub or a server on and off there. Servers cannot be added from the page: a server can be a program the proxy runs, so the","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Protect the setup page with a password","u":"proxy.html#protect-the-setup-page-with-a-password","x":"By default the setup page has no password: any program or browser on this computer can open it and change what it manages. To require one, open Security & access > Protect this page , type a password twice (at least 8 characters) and press Turn on . The browser you did it in stays signed in for 30 days; any other brows","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Settings you may want","u":"proxy.html#settings-you-may-want","x":"Everything below is a card under Proxy settings , on the setup page's Models & routing tab (the settings without a card are on Advanced ). Each control has a one-line explanation and a link back here. You do not need to edit any file. When you change something, nothing is written yet. Press Review changes to see exactl","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Prompt caching","u":"proxy.html#prompt-caching","x":"Long conversations resend the same beginning on every turn, and providers that cache charge much less for the part they have already seen. The Caching card has a switch per provider, shown only where it can be controlled (Claude through the Anthropic API or through Copilot); it is on by default. Keys: `prompt_cache`, `","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Model aliases","u":"proxy.html#model-aliases","x":"An alias is a short, fixed name for a model, so a tool can ask for `local` or `cloud` and you can change what that means later without touching the tool. Set them on the Model aliases card, or with `model_aliases` and a model's own `alias` in the file. See Providers and models .","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Fallbacks","u":"proxy.html#fallbacks","x":"A fallback tells the proxy what to try when a model cannot answer: its account is out of chats or credit, the backend is overloaded or unreachable, or it answers that it does not serve that model (from 2.1.2). `model_fallbacks` lists other models to try for a model; `provider_fallbacks` sends one account's requests to ","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Model groups","u":"proxy.html#model-groups","x":"A model group serves one name from several deployments, such as the same model on two Copilot accounts: each request goes to one of them, picked in turn (or by weight, by the fewest requests in flight, or by the fastest recent answers), a conversation keeps to the deployment that answered it, and one that cannot answer","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Auto mode at the proxy","u":"proxy.html#auto-mode-at-the-proxy","x":"The proxy has four built-in model names that choose the model for you. Any tool that talks to the proxy can ask for them: Name What answers `auto` The proxy looks at each request and sends it to the heavy, medium or light model below `auto-heavy` The model for planning, diagnosing and deciding `auto-medium` The model f","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Set up auto mode","u":"proxy.html#set-up-auto-mode","x":"Open the setup page (`http://127.0.0.1:11539`) open Models & routing and choose Auto mode in the row of links at the top of the tab. With nothing set, each weight shows Use defaults with the model it uses, the fallbacks under Then try (defaults) , and a preview of where a request goes. The note above the weights names ","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Your own rules for auto","u":"proxy.html#your-own-rules-for-auto","x":"Two optional settings let you steer `auto` without giving up its judgement for everything else. Keyword rules. When the person's last message contains one of your words, the request goes to the weight you chose for it: \"typo\" or \"rename\" to light, \"architecture\" to heavy. Whole words only, in any case (\"typo\" does not ","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Copilot Auto","u":"proxy.html#copilot-auto","x":"GitHub Copilot's own automatic mode, where Copilot picks the model for each request, is listed as a model of each Copilot account: `github-copilot/auto`, when your account offers it. It never takes the plain name `auto`, which is the proxy's own auto mode (or your alias of that name). You can choose it in two ways: As ","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"See what auto chose","u":"proxy.html#see-what-auto-chose","x":"Four places tell you where each request went and which model answered. The auto report. On the setup page, the Models & routing tab shows, above the Auto mode card, where auto sent requests (by weight and the model that answered) and what happened after each choice: how often the request failed, fell back to another mo","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Guards","u":"proxy.html#guards","x":"The Context guard (`context_guard`) is for tools that do not know a model's window: it sends the request as is, drops the oldest turns until it fits, or refuses it. The Repeat guard (`degenerate_guard`, off by default) stops a reply that has collapsed into repeating itself. See Guards .","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Keeping the cache useful","u":"proxy.html#keeping-the-cache-useful","x":"Two settings on the Guards card help a provider's prompt cache on long conversations: the Prefix stabiliser (`prefix_stabilizer`, on by default) keeps the start of each request the same from turn to turn, and Trim old tool results (`context_trim`, off by default) replaces old, large tool results with a short note. Resp","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Images and browser snapshots","u":"proxy.html#images-and-browser-snapshots","x":"Four choices on the Guards card, all off by default, shrink images and browser page snapshots before they are sent: `image_resize_tool`, `image_resize_user`, `image_superseded` and `snapshot_trim`. Smaller images cost fewer tokens, and small text in a screenshot may become harder to read. See Images and browser snapsho","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Seeing how the cache is doing","u":"proxy.html#seeing-how-the-cache-is-doing","x":"On the Cache tab, the charts show the share of input read from the cache per day, the tokens read from and written to it, what reading from it saved and what writing to it cost (both estimated), and how each request did. The Cache card shows today's hit rate, the tokens read from and written to the cache, a table per m","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Seeing cost per session","u":"proxy.html#seeing-cost-per-session","x":"The Sessions card groups AI units, tokens and requests by session. Maple Harness sends a random session id with every request (`X-Maple-Session`), and a subagent sends its parent's id, so its spend counts toward the session that started it. Any other client can send the header too. See What /cost tells you .","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Seeing what it saves","u":"proxy.html#seeing-what-it-saves","x":"The Savings card, first on the page, shows what the proxy's techniques saved and what they cost, across every session on this proxy. Every figure is tagged measured (a count, or what the provider billed) or estimated (a value in AI units, estimated from what your own requests cost). Its headline says what the prompt ca","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Prices for each model","u":"proxy.html#prices-for-each-model","x":"In plain words. The Savings card puts a value on what the cache saved. For GitHub Copilot it uses the prices Copilot itself states and publishes, so a model is valued from its first request. For other providers it learns each model's prices from your own requests, so a new model has no value until it has had a few requ","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Discounts","u":"proxy.html#discounts","x":"In plain words. GitHub Copilot already tells the proxy what each request cost, discounts included, and that is what the usage log and your budgets count. If your organisation pays a different rate (a negotiated discount, say), you can write it down, and the setup page and the reports show what each request costs at you","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Daily budgets","u":"proxy.html#daily-budgets","x":"For each provider you can cap requests per day, AI units per day, or both (days are UTC). At the limit, requests are refused or handed to its fallback. Key: `budgets`. See Budgets .","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"How many requests a model server takes at once","u":"proxy.html#how-many-requests-a-model-server-takes-at-once","x":"A model server can work on only so many requests at the same time. llama.cpp runs a fixed number of slots; vLLM and SGLang work on many at once until their memory is full; a cloud account has rate limits. Sent more, a server queues them itself, in the order they came, so a quick question you are waiting on can sit behi","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Limits and hosts (restart required)","u":"proxy.html#limits-and-hosts-restart-required","x":"The rate limit (off for this machine, on for other machines by default) and allowed hosts (extra names the proxy answers to) are read when the proxy starts, so restart it after saving. The usage log , one line of counts per request, is on by default and applies as soon as you save. See Limits and hosts , The usage log ","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Using it from another machine","u":"proxy.html#using-it-from-another-machine","x":"By default only this machine can reach the model APIs. To let another machine on your network use them, set the listen address and create an access token with the `proxy` scope on the setup page: YAML The proxy refuses to start on a non-loopback address until such a token exists, and callers send it as `Authorization: ","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Connect the harness to a proxy","u":"proxy.html#connect-the-harness-to-a-proxy","x":"In plain words. Maple Harness uses its own proxy on this machine, as it always has: it starts it when nothing is running, and you set nothing. That is the normal way, also in a team: signing in to a team hub happens at your local proxy (on its setup page, with `maple-proxy chain connect --sso`, or with the shortcut `ma","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Verified and unverified proxies","u":"proxy.html#verified-and-unverified-proxies","x":"In plain words. Maple Harness checks, every time it connects, that the proxy on this machine is your own. When it is, you see nothing: that is the normal case. When it is not (it is an older release, or it runs as another user, in a container, or with a different settings folder), the harness calls it unverified . Befo","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Check the cache yourself","u":"proxy.html#check-the-cache-yourself","x":"Providers cache the start of a request that they have seen recently, and reading from that cache costs a fraction of processing it again. Whether that is working for your account and your model is easy to see: Shell It sends the same small request twice, a few seconds apart, through the running proxy, and prints what e","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Other useful commands","u":"proxy.html#other-useful-commands","x":"Shell","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"Query your usage","u":"querying.html","x":"A cookbook of questions people ask of their usage, each with the command and what it printed. Every recipe here was run as shown; the output comes from a synthetic sample log (made-up people, models and traffic), so your figures will differ. Which store to query when is on Your data . Figures are AI units as the provid","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"In plain words","u":"querying.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Recipes: the questions people ask of their usage, each with the command and its output. What to do. Copy a recipe and run it. `maple-proxy usage query` needs nothing else; the DuckDB recipes need the `duckdb` program. The setup page's Data & storage tab has the commands to start DuckDB on your own log.","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"Before you start","u":"querying.html#before-you-start","x":"`maple-proxy usage query` needs nothing else. It reads the daily files on this machine, and in central mode the shared PostgreSQL, so run it on a replica there. It works whether the proxy is running or not. DuckDB recipes need DuckDB's command line ( install it ), plus `INSTALL ducklake` once for the lake and `INSTALL ","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"With usage query","u":"querying.html#with-usage-query","x":"The filters and `--group-by` keys are listed in Filter your data ; `--metrics` picks the columns (`maple-proxy usage query --help` lists them all).","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"Spend by person this week","u":"querying.html#spend-by-person-this-week","x":"On a team's proxy (here a replica in central mode, reading the shared PostgreSQL): The same question by team, on a replica in central mode: every replica's usage, from PostgreSQL. `unattributed` is requests no person was recorded for: sent before people were set up, or with an access token tied to no person. `local` (n","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"Top models by cost","u":"querying.html#top-models-by-cost","x":"`--limit` keeps the biggest groups by request count. A local model reports no AI units (`0.000`), and a provider without list prices has no `cost_estimated` (`-`). `list_cost_usd` is the request priced at the provider's published list prices whether or not `estimate_cost` is on (the figure monthly dollar budgets count,","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"Prompt-cache hit rate by tool","u":"querying.html#prompt-cache-hit-rate-by-tool","x":"`hit_rate` is the share of input tokens read from the provider's prompt cache; `lost` counts requests whose cache was lost. Why a cache missed: Why the cache missed .","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"Errors by provider in the last 24 hours","u":"querying.html#errors-by-provider-in-the-last-24-hours","x":"`--last 24h` is a rolling window; `--last 1d` would be today since midnight UTC. The Health tab and Provider health show the same over time.","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"Fallbacks, and why","u":"querying.html#fallbacks-and-why","x":"Each line is a request a fallback answered; `route_reason` names the class of error that sent it there. Add `model` to `--group-by` to see which model stepped in.","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"What auto chose","u":"querying.html#what-auto-chose","x":"`maple-proxy usage auto-report` and How auto did go further.","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"A roll-up per hub","u":"querying.html#a-roll-up-per-hub","x":"On a top hub with proxies below it , by the proxy each request started on: Use `client_requests` and `ai_units_spent` to count each request and its spend once; `requests` counts a line on every proxy a request passed through. Add `person` to `--group-by` for who asked.","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"With DuckDB","u":"querying.html#with-duckdb","x":"The same records, in plain SQL. Each source below gives you a `usage` table or view and the named views (`maple.requests`, `maple.daily_spend`, `maple.sessions`, `maple.people` and the rest), so a query written for one works on the others.","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"The daily files","u":"querying.html#the-daily-files","x":"Shell Spend by provider, week by week (saved as `weekly-provider.sql`): SQL The cache hit rate by tool, counted in requests rather than tokens (saved as `cache-by-tool.sql`): SQL Over many months the daily files are many files; `maple-proxy usage compact` packs them into one Parquet file per month, and `--source parque","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"The DuckDB file","u":"querying.html#the-duckdb-file","x":"`maple-proxy usage duckdb` loads the daily files into one DuckDB file and, run again, adds only what they gained: The two biggest models of each month (saved as `monthly-model.sql`): SQL July starts part way through: the DuckDB file follows the daily files, and the 90-day cleanup had deleted the days before July 11. `-","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"The lake","u":"querying.html#the-lake","x":"Attach it read-only ( how ), and the same query reaches back past the cleanup: The lake in DuckDB: its snapshots, the lake as it was at one of them, and history from before the 90-day cleanup. Time travel: the same table as it was at an earlier snapshot (saved as `then-and-now.sql`; list the snapshots with `FROM maple_","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"PostgreSQL","u":"querying.html#postgresql","x":"In central mode, two ways to read the shared PostgreSQL from DuckDB, both read-only. Use a role that can only read ( the grants ). In storage mode redis there is no PostgreSQL: `usage schema --source postgres` is refused and says so; `usage query` and `usage export` on a replica read every replica's usage. The named vi","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"Into a spreadsheet","u":"querying.html#into-a-spreadsheet","x":"A summary as CSV. Any `usage query` prints CSV with `--format csv`; the notes go to the terminal, not the file: Every request as CSV (or Parquet), with every column of the usage record : An Excel file from DuckDB , from any source above. DuckDB's `excel` extension writes `.xlsx` (`INSTALL excel` once). From the lake, a","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"Related","u":"querying.html#related","x":"Your data Where your usage data lives, and which store to query. The Maple data lake Years of usage history in DuckLake, on one machine or for a team. See and filter your usage The usage log on your own disk: filters, views and Grafana. Your usage data The usage log's columns, exports and DuckDB views.","m":"both"},{"p":"Questions the agent asks you","h":"Questions the agent asks you","u":"questions.html","x":"Some decisions are yours, not the agent's: which storage to use, whether a test belongs in one suite or another, what a screen should say. When the agent reaches one of those, it can ask you a short question with a few options to pick from, instead of guessing or writing a paragraph that asks you to type an answer. You","m":"single"},{"p":"Questions the agent asks you","h":"In plain words","u":"questions.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. A small dialog over the conversation: one to four questions, each with two to four options, an Other… row for your own words, and room for a note. Sometimes a preview (code, a diff, a mockup) beside the options shows what an option means. Why you want it. The agent asks only when the choice is really yours,","m":"single"},{"p":"Questions the agent asks you","h":"What you see","u":"questions.html#what-you-see","x":"In the terminal the dialog is framed over the conversation, which is dimmed behind it. Its title says who asks: Title When `A quick question, eh` The agent asks one question `A few quick questions, eh` The agent asks two to four `<agent> has a question` A subagent asks; its question line starts with `[<agent>]` Each qu","m":"single"},{"p":"Questions the agent asks you","h":"Keys","u":"questions.html#keys","x":"Key What it does `↑`/`↓` or `k`/`j` Move between the options `1` to `4` Pick that option (one-answer question: and go on to the next), or tick it (pick-any question) `Space` Tick or untick the option under the cursor `Enter` Answer and go to the next question; on All set? , send `Tab`, `←`/`→` Switch between the questi","m":"single"},{"p":"Questions the agent asks you","h":"On a narrow terminal","u":"questions.html#on-a-narrow-terminal","x":"Under 80 columns or 20 rows, with `harness.dialogs: stacked`, or in screen reader mode, the questions appear as a panel above the message box instead, with the same keys. There, and whenever the dialog is too narrow for two columns, `p` opens the preview full width and `p` or `Esc` closes it.","m":"single"},{"p":"Questions the agent asks you","h":"What stays in the conversation","u":"questions.html#what-stays-in-the-conversation","x":"One line for the call and one for the answers: When the answer came from somewhere other than this terminal, the line says where: `· answered on phone (pixel)`, or `· answered by agent:reviewer, another program, not you`. A skipped dialog shows `skipped`; one asked where nobody could answer shows `nobody to answer`.","m":"single"},{"p":"Questions the agent asks you","h":"Where else you can answer","u":"questions.html#where-else-you-can-answer","x":"The same question goes everywhere that can answer it. The first answer wins ; the others close and say where it was answered. Your phone. On the remote control page , the question is a card: a button per option, an Other… field and a note field per question. Review answers shows what you picked, then Send answers sends","m":"single"},{"p":"Questions the agent asks you","h":"In the plain REPL","u":"questions.html#in-the-plain-repl","x":"In the line-by-line REPL (`maple-harness --repl` at a terminal) the questions come as a numbered list, one question after another: Type a number and Enter; for a question that takes several, numbers with commas (`1,3`). Other asks for your own words on the next line. A number that is not on the list is asked again.","m":"single"},{"p":"Questions the agent asks you","h":"When nobody answers","u":"questions.html#when-nobody-answers","x":"A run with nobody there to answer (`maple-harness --prompt`, or a REPL whose input is not a terminal) never waits: the model is told at once that nobody can answer, and to choose and say what it chose. The same happens when you turn questions off. By default a question waits for you as long as it takes. With `harness.q","m":"single"},{"p":"Questions the agent asks you","h":"Settings","u":"questions.html#settings","x":"Setting Default What it does `harness.ask_person` `on` `off` does not offer the model the tool at all (from the next session), so it decides on its own `harness.agents.questions` `off` `on` lets another program answer over this session's control socket. Linux only for now. Not settable from a project's config `harness.","m":"single"},{"p":"Questions the agent asks you","h":"Technical details","u":"questions.html#technical-details","x":"","m":"single"},{"p":"Questions the agent asks you","h":"The tool","u":"questions.html#the-tool","x":"The model calls `ask_person` with a list of questions: Field Meaning `questions` 1 to 4 questions `questions[].id` A short id the answer refers to (default `q1`, `q2`, …) `questions[].header` The tab label, at most 12 characters (longer is cut) `questions[].question` The question, one sentence `questions[].multi_select","m":"single"},{"p":"Questions the agent asks you","h":"The answer","u":"questions.html#the-answer","x":"The model gets one JSON object back: JSON Field Meaning `status` `answered`, `declined` (skipped), `no_person` (nobody can answer) or `timed_out` (nobody answered within `harness.questions.timeout`) `answered_by` `terminal`, `phone`, `attach` (a terminal attached with `maple-harness attach`), `rpc`, `a2a:<caller>` or `","m":"single"},{"p":"Questions the agent asks you","h":"Over `--rpc`","u":"questions.html#over---rpc","x":"A question arrives as an event, beside the permission question : JSON Answer it with `question_answer`, the `request_id`, and an answer per question (an option label as written, any case), or `\"decline\": true` to skip: JSON A `question_resolved` event follows, with `request_id`, `status`, `by` and `summary` (the one-li","m":"single"},{"p":"Questions the agent asks you","h":"Over A2A","u":"questions.html#over-a2a","x":"While the session runs an A2A task , a question moves the task to input-required . Its status message says so in words and carries a data part: JSON Answer with a message to the same task holding a data part `{\"answers\": [{\"id\", \"selected\", \"other\", \"note\"}]}`, or `{\"decline\": true}`. A sentence is not an answer: the t","m":"single"},{"p":"Questions the agent asks you","h":"Over the control socket","u":"questions.html#over-the-control-socket","x":"Off by default. With `harness.agents.questions: on` in that session's settings (Linux only for now), the control socket takes two more commands, and `hello` lists them: Shell Option labels after the id answer a dialog with one question; use `--answers` for several. `--other TEXT` gives an answer of your own words. An a","m":"single"},{"p":"Questions the agent asks you","h":"Safety","u":"questions.html#safety","x":"An answer, from anywhere, is information for the model. It approves nothing: every tool call after it asks as your rules and mode say. An answer from another program is always labelled as such, to the model and in the transcript. The model is told never to use a question to ask permission to run a tool; approvals do th","m":"single"},{"p":"Questions the agent asks you","h":"See also","u":"questions.html#see-also","x":"Plan mode The plan the agent hands you to approve, in the same dialog. Subagents Helpers whose questions reach you with their name on them. Remote control Answer questions and approvals from your phone. Drive it from another program The `--rpc` events and commands, questions included. Commands and keys Every key in the","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Control it from your phone","u":"remote-control.html","x":"Share a session and drive it from your phone's browser or from another machine. You can approve tool calls (with a look at exactly what will change), answer the agent's questions, open several sessions in one list, and start new ones in folders you have allowed. The short version","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"In plain words","u":"remote-control.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Follow a session from your phone, approve what the agent wants to do (with the exact change in front of you), and start new sessions in folders you allowed. Why you want it. Leave your desk while a long task runs, and still answer its questions. The clicks. On the setup page, Security & access , Remote acce","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Recommended setup: Tailscale","u":"remote-control.html#recommended-setup-tailscale","x":"Put the computer and the phone on the same Tailscale network. Nothing is exposed to your local network or the internet, the traffic is already encrypted, and Tailscale can issue a real HTTPS certificate for your machine, which is what lets a phone show notifications with the page closed. Install Tailscale on the comput","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Sharing a session","u":"remote-control.html#sharing-a-session","x":"Inside a running session, type: `/remote` starts the gateway if it is not running. The gateway is the one address on your machine that lists every shared session, so a phone sees them all. A session shared through it stays shared until you turn it off or close the session. To start without any session open, use `maple-","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Pairing","u":"remote-control.html#pairing","x":"A pairing code (eight characters like `K7QM-4XPT`) works once and expires after five minutes. The phone trades it for a token of its own, which you can see and revoke in the device list on the setup page at `http://127.0.0.1:11539`. Scan the QR code, open the link, or type the code. Turning Remote control on in the set","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Sharing one session","u":"remote-control.html#sharing-one-session","x":"You can give someone a link to one session, and nothing else. On the remote page, open the session and tap Share (the arrow button at the top; a watch-only device does not have it). Give the share a label, such as who it is for, and choose what they can do: Watch only (the default): they follow the session live and can","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"A device for one session","u":"remote-control.html#a-device-for-one-session","x":"When you want a device of your own (a tablet, a second phone, a work laptop's browser) to reach one session and nothing else, pair it for that session only. On the remote page, open the session, tap Share , give it a label (which device it is), and choose My device, this session only . If other sessions are running, yo","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Starting sessions from the phone","u":"remote-control.html#starting-sessions-from-the-phone","x":"The New session sheet starts a session in a folder you have allowed: Shell A phone can only start sessions in those folders, and never anywhere else. A folder inside one of them that does not exist yet, such as a new project, is created when the session starts. Resuming a saved session whose folder has been deleted is ","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Starting in yolo mode","u":"remote-control.html#starting-in-yolo-mode","x":"Yolo mode allows every tool call that would otherwise ask, without asking. Your `deny` rules still apply. Because a stray tap on a phone is easy, starting a session as yolo from the phone is off until you turn it on for the machine: YAML Restart the gateway after changing it. The sheet then offers Start in yolo mode , ","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"When nobody is at the terminal","u":"remote-control.html#when-nobody-is-at-the-terminal","x":"In plain words. A session with nobody at its terminal asks you only while you can answer, on the phone or in a browser. When nobody can, the call is denied at once and the run goes on; nothing is ever approved for you. `maple-harness serve` and a remote host without its terminal. A question about a tool call is asked w","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Notifications","u":"remote-control.html#notifications","x":"Turn on Notify me when this session needs me in the session sheet. When the page is in the background you get a notification, a vibration and a count in the tab title when a session needs approval, stops with an error or finishes. It says only that, plus the project's name, never your commands or paths. With the page c","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Using the page","u":"remote-control.html#using-the-page","x":"Replies render as Markdown as they stream: headings, lists, tables and code blocks. Code blocks and tables have Copy buttons, and each reply has Copy Markdown . Send with in the session sheet chooses the key that sends: Ctrl/Cmd+Enter (the default), Enter, Shift+Enter or a combination you record. The Send button always","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Phones, tablets and foldables","u":"remote-control.html#phones-tablets-and-foldables","x":"The page is laid out from the size of the window, so it works the same in a browser tab, a Home Screen app, a split-screen window or Stage Manager: On a phone the session list is a drawer (the menu button), the conversation fills the screen, and approvals sit above the composer, in thumb reach. From about 900 pixels wi","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Steer or queue from the phone","u":"remote-control.html#steer-or-queue-from-the-phone","x":"A message you send while the session works does what it would do typed in the terminal: a correction or an addition (\"also handle the empty list\") steers the work in progress, and a next task (\"then update the changelog\") queues for after it. To decide yourself, pick Steer or Queue above the composer while the session ","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Attach from another computer","u":"remote-control.html#attach-from-another-computer","x":"Shell","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"If it does not work","u":"remote-control.html#if-it-does-not-work","x":"The phone cannot reach the address. Check that both devices are on the same Tailscale network, or the same local network for the LAN address, and that a firewall is not blocking port 11541. Certificate warning on the Tailscale name. HTTPS certificates are not enabled for your tailnet yet; see step 2. `+ New session` is","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"See also","u":"remote-control.html#see-also","x":"Security What Maple protects, by default and when you widen it. Troubleshooting Start with the checker, then the usual problems and their fixes. Keep it running in the background The proxy and the gateway as services that start with your computer. Questions the agent asks you Short multiple-choice questions when a deci","m":"single"},{"p":"Drive it from another program","h":"Drive it from another program","u":"rpc.html","x":"`maple-harness --rpc` reads commands from another process instead of a person, and writes back everything that process needs to follow along: the model's own output, tool calls and results, questions that need an answer, and the running cost — as it happens, not only once the whole session ends. It is how a CI step, an","m":"single"},{"p":"Drive it from another program","h":"In plain words","u":"rpc.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. A way for another program (a CI job, an editor plugin, your own script) to drive a Maple session, line by line, as the terminal does. Why you want it. Automate the agent and still see every tool call, question and cost as it happens. What to do. Start `maple-harness --rpc` from your program and exchange one","m":"single"},{"p":"Drive it from another program","h":"Start it","u":"rpc.html#start-it","x":"Shell It never starts on its own; it is meant to be the child of whatever is driving it. One JSON object per line comes in on stdin, one JSON object per line goes out on stdout. Nothing else is written to stdout — diagnostics and notes go to stderr, the same convention `--prompt` uses, so a plain `harness --rpc < comma","m":"single"},{"p":"Drive it from another program","h":"Commands","u":"rpc.html#commands","x":"Every line you send is one JSON object with a `cmd` field, and an optional `id` you choose — it comes back on the response so you can match one to the other. `cmd` What it does `prompt` Starts a new turn with `text`. Rejected while one is already running — say `steer` or `follow_up` instead, because they mean different","m":"single"},{"p":"Drive it from another program","h":"Events","u":"rpc.html#events","x":"What the turn itself does streams as further lines, `\"type\": \"event\"`, tagged by `event`: `event` Carries `assistant_delta` / `assistant_done` `text` (and `thinking`, when the model reasoned) `tool_call` / `tool_result` `tool`, `tool_arg` `steered` `text` — a steer (a `steer` command, or a `send` that steered) landed a","m":"single"},{"p":"Drive it from another program","h":"A permission question","u":"rpc.html#a-permission-question","x":"A tool call that needs approval under your permission settings — the same settings `--yolo` and the interface's own mode control (ask/auto/yolo) govern — is not decided for you. It arrives as an event: JSON Answer it the same way a person would: JSON and a `permission_resolved` event confirms it, with `allow` and `alwa","m":"single"},{"p":"Drive it from another program","h":"A question for the person","u":"rpc.html#a-question-for-the-person","x":"When the model asks the person to choose ( Questions the agent asks you ), the dialog arrives as an event, with `subagent` set when a subagent asks: JSON Answer with the option labels picked (any case), an `other` answer in your own words where `allow_other` is true, and a `note`, one entry per question; or `\"decline\":","m":"single"},{"p":"Drive it from another program","h":"Launch it from another program","u":"rpc.html#launch-it-from-another-program","x":"A program that opens the harness in a terminal pane for a person (an orchestrator, an editor, a script that sets up a task) can use these instead of the pipe above: Start with a task. `maple-harness --message \"implement story 12\"` (or the text after the flags) starts the interface with that as the first message, as if ","m":"single"},{"p":"Drive it from another program","h":"What it is not","u":"rpc.html#what-it-is-not","x":"This is a local pipe between a parent process and the child it started — there is no listening address and no token, because whoever can write to this process's stdin already has everything a shell here would give them. If you want a session another device can join over the network instead, see Control it from your pho","m":"single"},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"The shell sandbox","u":"sandbox.html","x":"The agent runs shell commands for you through its `bash` tool. Those commands run as you, with your files, your logins and your network. The sandbox contains them: when it is on, a command the model runs can change your project but not the rest of your computer, and cannot read the places where your credentials live. O","m":"single"},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"In plain words","u":"sandbox.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. A fence around the commands the agent runs: they can change your project, but not the rest of your computer, and cannot see where your keys and logins live. Why you want it. A mistaken or malicious command cannot read your SSH key or your cloud login. What to do. `/sandbox` in a session shows whether it is ","m":"single"},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"What it does","u":"sandbox.html#what-it-does","x":"While the sandbox is on, every `bash` command runs with these limits: What contained commands get Writable Your project folder, a private temporary folder, and the usual build caches under your home folder (`~/.cache`, `~/go`, `~/.npm`, `~/.cargo`, `~/.gradle`, `~/.m2` and similar), so builds and installs into the proj","m":"single"},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"What it breaks","u":"sandbox.html#what-it-breaks","x":"Anything that needs a hidden login, or writes outside the project, fails while the sandbox is on: Tool Why it fails What to do `git push`, `git pull` over SSH, `ssh`, `scp` `~/.ssh` is hidden, so there is no key and no known hosts Push yourself, allow `~/.ssh` for the session, or run that one command outside the sandbo","m":"single"},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"Turning it on","u":"sandbox.html#turning-it-on","x":"Setting What it does `/sandbox auto` Contain commands in this session. Without a backend (see Platforms ) commands still run, uncontained, and the first result says so `/sandbox strict` Contain commands in this session, and refuse to run any command when there is no backend `/sandbox off` No containment in this session","m":"single"},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"Contain commands whenever nobody approves them","u":"sandbox.html#contain-commands-whenever-nobody-approves-them","x":"In `ask` mode you see each command before it runs. In `auto` and `yolo` mode commands run without a person approving each one, and that is where containment matters most. This option turns the sandbox on exactly then: YAML With it on, and `sandbox.mode` not set: In `auto` or `yolo` mode, commands are contained (as with","m":"single"},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"When a command is blocked","u":"sandbox.html#when-a-command-is-blocked","x":"When a contained command fails because of the sandbox, the result says so in plain words, for you and for the model. For example: A `git push` over SSH blocked by the sandbox: the note says why, and the model stops and tells you what it needed. It says \"blocked\" only when the failure names a place the sandbox hides or ","m":"single"},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"Recommended setup","u":"sandbox.html#recommended-setup","x":"Install the backend (below) and run `/sandbox` or `maple-harness doctor` to check it works. If you use `auto` mode, turn on `sandbox.when_unattended`. Set a password for the proxy's setup page ( More > Protect this page , or `maple-proxy setup-password set`). The sandbox keeps contained commands away from the page; the","m":"single"},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"Platforms","u":"sandbox.html#platforms","x":"System Backend Status Linux bubblewrap (optional install, below) Verified. Blocking the setup page needs Linux 6.7 or later; on an older kernel commands can still reach it, and `/sandbox` says so macOS The built-in `sandbox-exec`, nothing to install Built, but not yet run on a Mac by us: treat it as unverified Windows ","m":"single"},{"p":"See and reduce what a task costs","h":"See and reduce what a task costs","u":"saving-tokens.html","x":"Long agent tasks spend most of their money re-reading a growing conversation on every turn. This page shows you where your spend goes, what the numbers mean, and a few habits that keep it down. It makes no promise about how much you will save: that depends on your tasks and your models, so measure your own. The short v","m":"single"},{"p":"See and reduce what a task costs","h":"In plain words","u":"saving-tokens.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Most of a long task's cost is the model re-reading the conversation on every turn. Maple shows you where that goes. Why you want it. You see which habits and settings help on your own work, instead of guessing. The clicks. `/cost` in a session; on the setup page, the Cache tab, which also says why the cache","m":"single"},{"p":"See and reduce what a task costs","h":"See where your spend goes","u":"saving-tokens.html#see-where-your-spend-goes","x":"In the agent: `/cost` shows the session's totals, the most recent requests with their cache hit rate, any retries, and roughly where the input went (instructions, tool descriptions, your messages, tool output). `/context` shows the window and what has been spent so far, including helper agents. In the proxy: the Cache ","m":"single"},{"p":"See and reduce what a task costs","h":"What the numbers mean","u":"saving-tokens.html#what-the-numbers-mean","x":"Hit rate. The share of a request's input that the provider served from its cache instead of processing again. A long task with a stable start should sit high; it drops when something early in the request changes. Tokens written and read. The first time the provider sees a stretch of your conversation it stores it, whic","m":"single"},{"p":"See and reduce what a task costs","h":"Habits that keep costs down","u":"saving-tokens.html#habits-that-keep-costs-down","x":"Keep one model for a long task. Switching models mid-task starts the provider's cache over, and the whole conversation is written again. `/model auto` keeps your model when it fits the job for this reason. Start a new task in a new session. A fresh session starts with a small conversation. Carrying an old, finished tas","m":"single"},{"p":"See and reduce what a task costs","h":"What we do not do","u":"saving-tokens.html#what-we-do-not-do","x":"Maple Harness does not put a filter between your commands and the model, and does not ask the model to talk in shorthand. Very large outputs are cut at a limit, with a note saying so.","m":"single"},{"p":"See and reduce what a task costs","h":"What others have measured","u":"saving-tokens.html#what-others-have-measured","x":"These are other people's measurements, of other tools, on other models and workloads. They are here for context, not as a claim about Maple. rtk , a filter for shell command output that advertises 60 to 90% fewer tokens. In JetBrains' benchmark (Denis Shiryaev, July 2026; 86 tasks, 425 billed trials, Claude Code with `","m":"single"},{"p":"See and reduce what a task costs","h":"Related","u":"saving-tokens.html#related","x":"What /cost tells you The session's own bill, request by request, and what to do about each flag. Settings by goal The settings that serve efficiency, efficacy and effectiveness. Check it yourself What Maple aims at, and how to measure it on your own work. Measure with bench Repeated, paired runs on your own tasks, to s","m":"single"},{"p":"Scheduled work","h":"Scheduled work","u":"scheduling.html","x":"The agent can come back later. You, or the agent, can schedule a prompt to run at a time, on a cron schedule, or every so often (\"check the build every 15 minutes\", \"write the standup notes at 9:05 on weekdays\"), and the agent can set itself a wake-up to look at something that takes a while. A job runs while the sessio","m":"single"},{"p":"Scheduled work","h":"In plain words","u":"scheduling.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. A prompt that starts a turn later, once or again and again. `/loop 10m check the deploy` runs it now and every ten minutes. The model can schedule work too, with its `schedule` and `wakeup` tools, and each job it makes is shown in the conversation. Why you want it. Watching a deploy, re-running a flaky chec","m":"single"},{"p":"Scheduled work","h":"Make a job","u":"scheduling.html#make-a-job","x":"From the message box: That runs the prompt now, then every ten minutes for up to seven days. `/loop 10m --until \"gh pr checks 123\" …` ends on its own once that check passes, and `--max` and `--budget` bound it: see Loops . Without an interval, the agent paces itself: It runs now, and when it is done the agent sets its ","m":"single"},{"p":"Scheduled work","h":"When a job fires","u":"scheduling.html#when-a-job-fires","x":"Only while the session is open and idle , between turns. Its turn starts with a line such as: A held turn. The person is not asking for this work right now, so the turn is held, as a turn another agent's message starts is: in auto or yolo mode its tools still ask, and your own allow rules still apply. When nobody answe","m":"single"},{"p":"Scheduled work","h":"Limits","u":"scheduling.html#limits","x":"Limit Value Jobs per session 20, besides one wake-up Wake-ups One at a time: a new one replaces the earlier one Recurring jobs Seven days: past that, a job fires once more and is removed, and `/schedule` says \"its last\" beside it Shortest interval One minute Furthest one-time job A year ahead","m":"single"},{"p":"Scheduled work","h":"See and change the jobs","u":"scheduling.html#see-and-change-the-jobs","x":"`/schedule` lists them: id, schedule, next fire, the last result, and the prompt. `/schedule delete <id>`, `/schedule pause <id>`, `/schedule resume <id>` and `/schedule run <id>` act on one. The lineup (`/tasks`, `Alt+T`): the Scheduled group, with `x` to delete (then `y`), `p` to pause or resume and `r` to run one no","m":"single"},{"p":"Scheduled work","h":"Durable jobs","u":"scheduling.html#durable-jobs","x":"A durable job runs whether or not Maple Harness is open : on a schedule you approved, headless, in this project's folder, resuming this session's conversation. Use one for work that must happen while you are away, like a nightly check. Off by default. Turn them on with `harness.schedule.durable: on` (in `/settings`, or","m":"single"},{"p":"Scheduled work","h":"How a durable run behaves","u":"scheduling.html#how-a-durable-run-behaves","x":"Where it runs A systemd user timer on Linux, a launchd agent on macOS, under your own user. Nothing is installed system-wide, and nothing secret is written into the timer What it runs `maple-harness --session <name> --prompt <the job's prompt>`, in the job's folder Sandbox Always, strictly: a command runs contained or ","m":"single"},{"p":"Scheduled work","h":"Remove a durable job","u":"scheduling.html#remove-a-durable-job","x":"Any of these removes the job and its timer: Shell In the session that made it: `/schedule delete s7f00d1`, or `x` on its row in the lineup. On your phone: Delete on its card. `schedule list` also points out a timer left without its job (from a folder you deleted by hand), and `schedule rm <id>` removes that too. What i","m":"single"},{"p":"Scheduled work","h":"Plan mode","u":"scheduling.html#plan-mode","x":"While planning the agent can list the jobs, and nothing else: making, deleting, pausing a job and setting a wake-up wait for the plan to be approved.","m":"single"},{"p":"Scheduled work","h":"Settings","u":"scheduling.html#settings","x":"Key Default What it does `schedule.jobs` `on` Scheduled work in a session: the `schedule` and `wakeup` tools, `/loop` and `/schedule`. `off` leaves the tools out (from the next session) and fires nothing `schedule.durable` `off` `on` lets a job be installed with your system's timers, each one approved by you `schedule.","m":"single"},{"p":"Scheduled work","h":"Technical notes","u":"scheduling.html#technical-notes","x":"A session's own jobs are saved in its session file and fire only in the full-screen terminal (and a session it shares with your phone). `--prompt`, `--repl`, `--rpc`, `serve` and A2A agents do not offer the tools. A job's fire is appended at the end of the conversation, after what is there, so the provider's prompt cac","m":"single"},{"p":"Scheduled work","h":"See also","u":"scheduling.html#see-also","x":"Loops A goal it works toward until a check passes, a `/loop` that ends on its own, Stop hooks and shell loops. Approvals when nobody answers What happens to a question nobody answers, in a held turn or a run with no terminal. Background commands and monitors Commands that run while the agent works, and wake it when the","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Search by meaning","u":"search-by-meaning.html","x":"The agent looks things up with three tools: `history_search` (older parts of the conversation that were moved out of view to make room), `recall` (memory notes) and `graph_search` (the project index of files and symbols). On their own they match words, so a search for \"the login bug\" misses a message about \"authenticat","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"In plain words","u":"search-by-meaning.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. The agent can find old parts of the conversation, its memory notes and places in your code by what they mean, not only by the words: \"the login bug\" also finds \"authentication failures\". Why you want it. Fewer misses when the agent looks something up, so less re-reading and fewer wrong guesses. The clicks. ","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"The two models","u":"search-by-meaning.html#the-two-models","x":"bge-small-en-v1.5 (default) EmbeddingGemma 300M (optional) Runs in the proxy itself, on the CPU; nothing else to install the proxy itself, on the CPU, or your own embeddings server (Ollama, LM Studio and others) Download about 128 MB, once; about 33 MB is kept on disk about 1.2 GB, once; about 304 MB is kept on disk. N","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Choose the engine","u":"search-by-meaning.html#choose-the-engine","x":"The Search by meaning card has two engines: Built-in (Go): the proxy runs the model itself, on the CPU, with nothing else to install. Pick bge-small or EmbeddingGemma, download it, and press Use this model . Your own server (OpenAI-compatible): the proxy sends the text to an embeddings server you already run on this ma","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Set it up","u":"search-by-meaning.html#set-it-up","x":"From the setup page: open the proxy's setup page, go to Models & routing , find the Search by meaning card, keep Built-in (Go) selected and press Download on bge-small-en-v1.5. The card shows the download's progress (with Cancel ), this machine's memory and CPU cores, and a note if the machine is below what the model i","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Use EmbeddingGemma built in","u":"search-by-meaning.html#use-embeddinggemma-built-in","x":"EmbeddingGemma is gated on Hugging Face: Google asks everyone to accept its terms before downloading it. Sign in to Hugging Face , open the EmbeddingGemma 300M page and accept the terms there. Read the Gemma Terms of Use and the Gemma Prohibited Use Policy first. Create a token with read access at huggingface.co/settin","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Use your own server","u":"search-by-meaning.html#use-your-own-server","x":"Any server with an OpenAI-compatible `/v1/embeddings` works, for example: Server Base URL (default port) Model name, for example Ollama `http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1` `embeddinggemma` (after `ollama pull embeddinggemma`) LM Studio `http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1` the embedding model you loaded llama.cpp's `llama-server` (starte","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"When something goes wrong","u":"search-by-meaning.html#when-something-goes-wrong","x":"The card's status line, its messages and the terminal commands use the same words. Each problem says what to do: What you see What to do The network is not reachable Check the connection (and any VPN), then try again The secure connection could not be verified Check the machine's clock and any network filter that inter","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Is my machine enough?","u":"search-by-meaning.html#is-my-machine-enough","x":"The proxy reads the machine's total and free memory, its CPU cores, its processor type and operating system, and shows them on the card. Before a download it warns, and never blocks, when the machine is below these: Model Comfortable from Basis bge-small-en-v1.5 4 GB of memory and 2 CPU cores measured: about 150 MB pea","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"What changes for the agent","u":"search-by-meaning.html#what-changes-for-the-agent","x":"Tool With a model Without one `history_search` Results ranked by words and meaning combined. The filters (role, tool, path, turn range) apply first, as before Words only, unchanged `recall` Gains a `query` argument that returns the eight closest notes by words and meaning. Without `query` it lists notes as before No `q","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Measured results","u":"search-by-meaning.html#measured-results","x":"A retrieval test, measured on 28 September 2026: queries worded differently from what they look for, over a 43-message coding conversation (24 queries), 24 project memory notes (16 queries), and this project's own index of about 7,100 files and symbols (16 queries). Each query was run twice through the real tools, word","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"The proxy's cache","u":"search-by-meaning.html#the-proxys-cache","x":"The proxy keeps every vector it computes, so a text it has already seen is answered at once instead of computed again: the harness re-reading the same messages, notes or files costs almost nothing the second time. In a request with several texts, only the new ones are computed, and the answer keeps the order you sent. ","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Where things are kept, and removing them","u":"search-by-meaning.html#where-things-are-kept-and-removing-them","x":"What Where Removed by A built-in model `embeddings/bge-small-en-v1.5/` or `embeddings/embeddinggemma-300m/` in your Maple Harness config folder (folder private to you, files readable only by you) Remove on the card, or `maple-proxy embeddings remove <name>` The Hugging Face token, and your server's API key the proxy's ","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Privacy","u":"search-by-meaning.html#privacy","x":"Text goes from the harness to the proxy it already uses, and no further than a model on this machine or your own network. The built-in models run inside the proxy. With your own server, the proxy forwards only to an address it can tell is local, and refuses one that is not. When it forwards, the proxy does not follow a","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"See also","u":"search-by-meaning.html#see-also","x":"Proxy settings: Search by meaning The keys behind the card. The semantic cache Answer a question already answered, worded differently, without asking the provider. Commands and keys Everything you can type in a session, every key, and the shell commands. Give other tools this project's knowledge This project's memory a","m":"single"},{"p":"Security","h":"Security","u":"security.html","x":"An agent runs commands and writes files on your machine; that is its job. This page says what Maple Harness does to keep that safe, and what you should do. The short version","m":"both"},{"p":"Security","h":"In plain words","u":"security.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. What Maple does to keep an agent that runs commands safe, and what you should do. The short version. It asks before it writes or runs anything; your keys are in your system keyring; the setup page answers only on this machine; and you can put a password on it and contain the agent's commands. The clicks. On","m":"both"},{"p":"Security","h":"Reporting a problem","u":"security.html#reporting-a-problem","x":"Email Kinn Coelho Juliao at <kinncj@gmail.com> with what you found, how to reproduce it, and the version (`maple-harness version`). Please allow a reasonable time to respond before disclosing it publicly. Reports are handled in confidence.","m":"both"},{"p":"Security","h":"What it does for you","u":"security.html#what-it-does-for-you","x":"It asks first. Every tool call passes your rules. By default it asks before writing a file or running a command, and a `deny` rule is absolute. `--yolo` skips the asking, never the denying, and it must be given on the command line: a configuration file can never turn it on by itself. The shell can be contained. With bu","m":"both"},{"p":"Security","h":"What you should do","u":"security.html#what-you-should-do","x":"Treat a device token like an SSH key. It is a shell on your computer. Prefer pairing codes, keep tokens out of chats and screenshots, and revoke a lost phone straight away. Use Tailscale, or another private network, for remote control. Do not forward the port through your router to the internet. See Control it from you","m":"both"},{"p":"The semantic cache","h":"The semantic cache","u":"semantic-cache.html","x":"The semantic cache lets the proxy answer a question it has already answered, worded differently, without asking the provider again. \"How do I reset my password?\" and \"how can I reset my password\" get the same answer, and the second one costs nothing at the provider. Off unless you turn it on With no `semantic_cache:` b","m":"both"},{"p":"The semantic cache","h":"In plain words","u":"semantic-cache.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. When a new question means nearly the same as one the proxy answered a moment ago, for the same person, the same model and the same conversation, the proxy returns that earlier answer. Why you want it. Help desks, documentation bots and scripts often ask the same thing in many ways. Each repeat answered by t","m":"both"},{"p":"The semantic cache","h":"What it answers, and what it never answers","u":"semantic-cache.html#what-it-answers-and-what-it-never-answers","x":"A request may be answered from the semantic cache only when all of these hold: the same caller asked before: the same access token, or the same kind of tool on this machine (with `share: team`, anyone of the same team); for a request a chained proxy forwarded, the same person at the same proxy (a forwarded request that","m":"both"},{"p":"The semantic cache","h":"The settings","u":"semantic-cache.html#the-settings","x":"All under `semantic_cache:` in `config.yaml`, or on the setup page: Models & routing , card Semantic cache . The form needs a page password (the cache can keep questions in a Qdrant server you name); the Cache tab shows what it answered. YAML Key Default What it does `enabled` `off` `on` turns it on for every model and","m":"both"},{"p":"The semantic cache","h":"Where answers are kept","u":"semantic-cache.html#where-answers-are-kept","x":"One proxy (disk mode, the default). In the proxy's memory, and in one file in the config folder, `semantic-cache.bin`, so a restart keeps them. The file is encrypted with a key only this install has, readable by you alone, and a file that was changed is not used. Several replicas (storage mode central). In the shared R","m":"both"},{"p":"The semantic cache","h":"Forget saved answers","u":"semantic-cache.html#forget-saved-answers","x":"On the setup page: the Cache tab, Saved answers , choose a cache and press Clear saved answers . In a terminal: `maple-proxy cache clear` forgets the answers of both caches; `--semantic` or `--exact` only one; `--team <id>` only the answers a team shares, `--caller <token id>` (or `local:<tool>`) only one caller's. Wit","m":"both"},{"p":"The semantic cache","h":"What a client sees","u":"semantic-cache.html#what-a-client-sees","x":"Every request the semantic cache is on for says what it did, in the response header `X-Maple-Semantic-Cache`: `hit; similarity=0.973`: an earlier answer, to a latest message that close in meaning (0 to 1). The answer reports no tokens and no cost, since nothing was sent to the provider, and `X-Maple-Cache-Response` say","m":"both"},{"p":"The semantic cache","h":"What it saves, honestly","u":"semantic-cache.html#what-it-saves-honestly","x":"An answer from the semantic cache costs no AI units at the provider: the usage log records it with zero tokens and zero cost ( measured : nothing was sent). What it saved is valued at what the original request cost when it was first answered, and shown on the Savings card's response cache line as an estimate . The new ","m":"both"},{"p":"The semantic cache","h":"On the setup page","u":"semantic-cache.html#on-the-setup-page","x":"The Cache tab counts the requests answered from Maple's caches today, with those answered by meaning apart, and, while the semantic cache is on, its own figures since the proxy started: answered, asked of the provider, not eligible, and how many answers it keeps. `maple-proxy cache` prints the same count. While no mode","m":"both"},{"p":"The semantic cache","h":"When something goes wrong","u":"semantic-cache.html#when-something-goes-wrong","x":"Every request says `bypass`. No embedding model is installed, or the request is not one it answers (see the list above). Install the model on the Search by meaning card. No hits, only `miss`. The questions differ in more than wording (another system prompt, an earlier message, another setting, other numbers), or they a","m":"both"},{"p":"The semantic cache","h":"Not yet verified","u":"semantic-cache.html#not-yet-verified","x":"The cache's behaviour is tested with a stand-in embedding model, a stand-in Qdrant and an in-process Redis. Not yet run: real questions through the built-in model (how often near questions match at a given threshold), a real Qdrant server, and a real Redis. Choose the threshold on your own traffic.","m":"both"},{"p":"The semantic cache","h":"See also","u":"semantic-cache.html#see-also","x":"Proxy settings: Caching The exact-match response cache, which is checked first. Search by meaning The built-in model the cache uses to compare questions. See what you saved How savings are estimated and shown, and what they never claim. Run the proxy on Kubernetes Several replicas sharing one cache through Redis.","m":"both"},{"p":"Sign in","h":"Sign in","u":"sign-in.html","x":"You can sign in from the terminal or from the proxy's setup page at `http://127.0.0.1:11539`. Either way, the proxy holds the credential and the agent only asks it for models. Signing in again refreshes the same account — the models it can use, its plan, its allowance — rather than adding another. Shell This page is ab","m":"both"},{"p":"Sign in","h":"In plain words","u":"sign-in.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Signing in tells the proxy which accounts it may use for you: a GitHub Copilot seat, an Anthropic API key, or another provider. The proxy keeps the credential; the agent never sees it. Why you want it. Without an account (or a model server of your own) there is no model to talk to. With two, the proxy can m","m":"both"},{"p":"Sign in","h":"GitHub Copilot","u":"sign-in.html#github-copilot","x":"Shell The command prints a code and a link. Open the link, enter the code, and approve. The agent then asks Copilot which models your plan allows and configures exactly those. Free plan. It works, including Copilot's auto mode. The setup page shows how many chats you have left and when they reset, and the status bar in","m":"both"},{"p":"Sign in","h":"Claude (Anthropic API key)","u":"sign-in.html#claude-anthropic-api-key","x":"Shell It asks for an API key (typed hidden), checks it with Anthropic, and configures the models that key can call. You can also paste it into the Anthropic (Claude) card on the setup page. Create a key in the Claude Console . An account with no credit will sign in but its requests will fail with a \"credit balance too ","m":"both"},{"p":"Sign in","h":"OpenRouter","u":"sign-in.html#openrouter","x":"OpenRouter can make a key for you when you sign in with your OpenRouter account. Nothing about OpenRouter appears until you choose it. On the setup page, click Add provider , choose OpenRouter as the type, and click Sign in with OpenRouter . Approve in the OpenRouter tab that opens. Back on the setup page, the provider","m":"both"},{"p":"Sign in","h":"Grok (xAI API key)","u":"sign-in.html#grok-xai-api-key","x":"Shell It asks for an API key (typed hidden), checks it with xAI, and configures the chat models that key can call, with their context windows. Create a key in the xAI Console . Nothing about Grok appears on the setup page until you choose it: to add it there, open Providers , click + Add provider , choose Grok (xAI) as","m":"both"},{"p":"Sign in","h":"Gemini API (Google AI Studio key)","u":"sign-in.html#gemini-api-google-ai-studio-key","x":"Read this first. On Google's free (unpaid) tier, Google uses what you send and the responses to improve its products and machine learning, and human reviewers may read, annotate and process them. Do not send sensitive, confidential or personal information there, which for a coding agent includes private code and secret","m":"both"},{"p":"Sign in","h":"Azure and Google Cloud","u":"sign-in.html#azure-and-google-cloud","x":"Azure OpenAI, Microsoft Foundry and Google's Vertex AI sign in with your cloud identity (`az login`, `gcloud`, or a pod's workload identity) or an Azure API key: see Azure and Google Cloud .","m":"both"},{"p":"Sign in","h":"Amazon Bedrock","u":"sign-in.html#amazon-bedrock","x":"Bedrock signs in with your AWS credentials (a profile, `aws sso login`, or an IAM role on EC2, ECS or EKS); Maple stores no AWS key: see Amazon Bedrock .","m":"both"},{"p":"Sign in","h":"More than one account","u":"sign-in.html#more-than-one-account","x":"Add a second (or third) account of the same provider — another GitHub Copilot seat, another Anthropic API key — and give it a name, so you can tell it apart from the others. A name can be used by one account only, across all providers, and cannot be a provider's id: Shell On the setup page, each provider's card shows \"","m":"both"},{"p":"Sign in","h":"Where the credentials are kept","u":"sign-in.html#where-the-credentials-are-kept","x":"Keys and sign-ins go in your operating system's keyring (Keychain on macOS, the Secret Service on Linux). The configuration file holds only a reference such as `${secret:anthropic-key}`, never the key, so it is safe to paste into an issue. Without a keyring they fall back to a private file, and the proxy tells you. `ma","m":"both"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"Single sign-on for the setup page","u":"single-sign-on.html","x":"Team admins can sign in to a shared proxy's setup page with their company account (Google, Microsoft Entra ID, Okta, any OpenID Connect provider, or GitHub) instead of sharing one password, and developers can sign in to get their own access tokens. Off unless you turn it on. A proxy for one person on one machine does n","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"In plain words","u":"single-sign-on.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Your company's sign-in page (the one you use for email) guards the proxy's setup page. Admins open an address like `https://maple-admin.example.com`, sign in with their work account, and see the dashboards and manage people and budgets. Nobody shares a password. Developers too, if you want. With developer s","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"Set it up with the wizard","u":"single-sign-on.html#set-it-up-with-the-wizard","x":"The Single sign-on tab on the setup page on the proxy's own machine. The wizard is not available through single sign-on itself: who may sign in is only ever changed on the proxy's machine (or in `config.yaml`). Identity provider. Google, Microsoft Entra ID (your tenant id), Okta (your org or authorization server), GitH","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"How it works","u":"single-sign-on.html#how-it-works","x":"The setup page on the proxy's machine does not change. It still listens on this machine only, and its password (if you set one) still works there. Maple's own commands keep using it. Single sign-on serves the page on a second address you choose (`setup_page.sso.listen`), which people reach through your load balancer or","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"Developer sign-in","u":"single-sign-on.html#developer-sign-in","x":"Off by default (`developers.onboarding: off`). Turn it on in the wizard's step 6, or in `config.yaml`: Ask an admin (`approve`): a developer's first sign-in files a request; an admin accepts it on People & teams (as a new person, or as someone already there) or refuses it. Automatic (`auto`): someone whose verified ema","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"Recent sign-ins","u":"single-sign-on.html#recent-sign-ins","x":"The Single sign-on tab lists the sign-ins of the last seven days from the proxy's audit log: who signed in (and as an admin or a developer), who was added, and who was refused and why: not an admin or a developer here, no verified email address, a Google account outside your Workspace domain, not in an approved GitHub ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"The settings","u":"single-sign-on.html#the-settings","x":"All under `setup_page.sso` in `config.yaml`. The wizard writes them for you. Read at start, except `admins`, `groups_claim`, `password`, `session_lifetime`, `self_view`, `people` and `developers`, which apply on the next request after the file is saved. Key Default What it does `enabled` `false` Turns single sign-on on","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"Google","u":"single-sign-on.html#google","x":"In the Google Cloud console, APIs & Services > Credentials > Create credentials > OAuth client ID , type Web application . Add your redirect address under Authorized redirect URIs . In the wizard choose Google (`issuer: https://accounts.google.com`), and the client id and secret it shows. Admins: `emails` or `domains`.","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"Microsoft Entra ID","u":"single-sign-on.html#microsoft-entra-id","x":"App registrations > New registration : supported account types this organizational directory only , redirect URI of type Web = your redirect address. Then Certificates & secrets > New client secret . In the wizard choose Microsoft Entra ID and type your directory (tenant) id: the issuer is `https://login.microsoftonlin","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"Okta","u":"single-sign-on.html#okta","x":"Applications > Create App Integration > OIDC - OpenID Connect > Web Application . Sign-in redirect URI = your redirect address. In the wizard choose Okta : the issuer is `https://<your-org>.okta.com` (the org authorization server) or `https://<your-org>.okta.com/oauth2/default` (a custom one). Admins by group: on the o","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"GitHub","u":"single-sign-on.html#github","x":"GitHub has no OpenID Connect sign-in for people, so Maple uses a GitHub OAuth app with PKCE and then asks GitHub for the account's verified email addresses and, when groups are used, its organisations and teams. Settings > Developer settings > OAuth Apps > New OAuth App ; Authorization callback URL = your redirect addr","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"Other OpenID Connect providers","u":"single-sign-on.html#other-openid-connect-providers","x":"Keycloak, Auth0, Authentik, Dex and others work the same way: register a confidential web client, type the issuer whose `/.well-known/openid-configuration` names that same issuer, and use emails, domains, groups or subjects. The provider must support the authorization code flow with PKCE (S256). Only an `email_verified","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"On Kubernetes","u":"single-sign-on.html#on-kubernetes","x":"When `config.yaml` comes from a ConfigMap (`storage.settings: file`) or a settings repository, the wizard cannot save: use Show the YAML and put the block there, and the client secret in your Secret under `sso.client_secret`. For example: YAML Expose port 11547 with a Service, and an Ingress (or a Gateway route) for `m","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"People see their own usage","u":"single-sign-on.html#people-see-their-own-usage","x":"With `self_view: on`, someone who is not an admin but whose verified email is one of your people's sees that person's own usage (the last 30 days, by day and by model) at `/sso/me`, and nothing else. Map emails to person ids under `people` (`ana@example.com: ana`), or give a person their email as their id. Off by defau","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"What is not verified","u":"single-sign-on.html#what-is-not-verified","x":"Tested against a stand-in OpenID Connect provider and a stand-in GitHub, and in Chromium through a TLS front like a load balancer. Not yet run against real Google, Microsoft Entra ID, Okta or GitHub accounts, real ingress controllers, or Firefox and Safari.","m":"teams"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"Subagents","u":"subagents.html","x":"A subagent is a second session that the main one hands a small, self-contained job to: \"find where the retry logic lives\", \"review this diff\", \"plan the change\". It has its own instructions, its own list of tools and optionally its own model. It does not see your conversation, and only its final report comes back, so a","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"In plain words","u":"subagents.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. A helper the agent hands a small job to (\"find where retries happen\", \"review this diff\"). It works on its own and only its report comes back. Why you want it. A long search stays out of your conversation, so the main session stays short and cheap. What to do. Nothing: the agent uses them when a job fits. T","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"The ones you already have","u":"subagents.html#the-ones-you-already-have","x":"Name What it does `general-purpose` Takes any self-contained task. Has the same tools as the main session `explore` Reads and searches the project and answers questions about it. Cannot change anything `plan` Studies the code and returns a step-by-step plan. Cannot change anything The model uses them by itself when a j","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"Watching them work","u":"subagents.html#watching-them-work","x":"In the conversation, the line under the call that started a subagent says what it is doing while it runs: While a subagent runs, a small list also appears just above the input box (which does not move): `◯` is running, `!` is waiting for you, `✓` finished, `✗` failed or stopped. Press the down arrow from an empty input","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"Make your own","u":"subagents.html#make-your-own","x":"Type `/agents`. You see every agent with where it lives, and can view, create, edit or delete them. Creating one walks you through it: Where it lives: this project, or all your projects. A name and a description. The description is what tells the model when to use it, so say when, not only what (\"Reviews a diff for bug","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"More fields","u":"subagents.html#more-fields","x":"An agent file can say more than that. Claude Code reads the first five of these too; it ignores the Maple ones, so one file works in both: Markdown Field Meaning `disallowedTools` Tools taken out of whatever `tools` allows, by the harness's names or Claude Code's (`Bash`, `Read`, `Write`, `Edit`, `Grep`, …). Taking `ba","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"Notes of its own","u":"subagents.html#notes-of-its-own","x":"In plain words: an agent with `memory:` remembers what it learned last time (where the tests are, which one is flaky, what fixed it) and starts its next run knowing it. When it starts, the first part of its `MEMORY.md` (200 lines or 25 KB at most) is put in its prompt, marked as its own earlier writing, not your instru","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"Agents in the background","u":"subagents.html#agents-in-the-background","x":"In plain words: What it is. The agent can start a subagent and keep working while it runs, instead of waiting for it. When the subagent finishes, its report arrives as a message and the agent carries on with it. Why you want it. Independent pieces of a large task (search one part, change another) go on at the same time","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"The lineup: the task list you can watch","u":"subagents.html#the-lineup-the-task-list-you-can-watch","x":"In plain words: What it is. The shared task list, on screen. `/tasks` (or `Alt+T`, or the palette) opens The lineup : every task, who is on it (the main agent or a subagent), what it waits for, and what is done. The sidebar lists the tasks too, the line above the message box names the one in progress, and the conversat","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"How many at once, and on which model","u":"subagents.html#how-many-at-once-and-on-which-model","x":"`harness.subagents.mode` sets how subagents run: Mode What it does `auto` (default) Several at once, up to `max_parallel`, but never more on one model than its backend serves at once when you have said so in `capacity`. A background agent on your main model, when that model serves one request at a time, runs in the for","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"Settings","u":"subagents.html#settings","x":"Setting Default Meaning `harness.subagents.max_parallel` `3` (in `auto`: what the backends serve, when known) How many run at the same time when the model hands out several jobs at once. Set, it wins in every mode `harness.subagents.max_turns` `50` The most turns any one subagent may take `harness.subagents.mode` `auto","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"Delegate to your local Claude","u":"subagents.html#delegate-to-your-local-claude","x":"If you have Claude Code installed and signed in on your machine, the harness can hand it a whole task: \"ask my local Claude to review the error handling in this package\". The harness runs your own `claude` program in this project folder (`claude -p`, its non-interactive mode), shows its progress in the agents list, and","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"Turn it on","u":"subagents.html#turn-it-on","x":"It is off until you turn it on in your own `config.yaml` (a project's `.mapleharness/config.yaml` cannot): YAML The first time it runs in a project, and again whenever that project's Claude settings files change, the harness shows you what it is about to run and asks. The question lists what the project's `.claude/sett","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"Choose which `claude` runs","u":"subagents.html#choose-which-claude-runs","x":"By default the harness runs the `claude` it finds on your `PATH`. To run a particular one, name it in your own `config.yaml`: YAML The path must be absolute (`~/` for your home folder is fine). Before it runs, the harness checks that it exists, is a regular executable file and is not inside the project, and that nobody","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"Extra options for Claude Code","u":"subagents.html#extra-options-for-claude-code","x":"`claude_code_options` adds options to the `claude -p` command, one per entry, written as `--flag value` (or `--flag=value`): YAML Only these are allowed, each with a check on its value: Option What it does Accepted values `--model` The model it uses An alias such as `sonnet` or `opus`, or a full model name (letters, di","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"Checks before it runs","u":"subagents.html#checks-before-it-runs","x":"Before its first run in a session, and again when the program or the options change, the harness checks that the program answers `claude --version` within a few seconds (asked in an empty folder of its own, or inside the sandbox when the sandbox applies), that the options pass the checks above, and that the sandbox wou","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"How it is kept safe","u":"subagents.html#how-it-is-kept-safe","x":"Only in a project the harness trusts. If the project has files the harness itself would run and you have not trusted them (`maple-harness trust approve`), it refuses. It also refuses in your home folder itself, and refuses a `claude` inside the project, whether found on `PATH` or named in `claude_code_path`. The projec","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"See also","u":"subagents.html#see-also","x":"Background agents Agents that keep working after the turn, and how to talk to them. Commands and keys The agents tray, its keys and the palette. Questions the agent asks you How a subagent asks you for a decision. See and reduce what a task costs Why a subagent keeps your main conversation small.","m":"single"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","u":"teams-and-hubs.html","x":"This guide is for running Maple for more than one person: a small team sharing one proxy, a company with a hub per team, or one person whose laptop should fall back to a shared hub when their own account runs out. It walks through each setup with the configuration to type, what you see afterwards, and where to look whe","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"In plain words","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#in-plain-words","x":"One proxy, several people: open the proxy to your network, give each person a token, and see who used what. The setup page's People & teams tab walks you through it. Your laptop and a team hub: your own proxy joins the hub, by signing in with your company account or with a token from the hub's admin. Maple Harness keep","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"Who this is for, and who can skip it","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#who-this-is-for-and-who-can-skip-it","x":"If you use Maple on your own machine, you need none of this. Install it, sign in and work, as before. Nothing on this page is on until you add it to `config.yaml`: a proxy with no `people:`, no `teams:` and no other Maple proxy as a provider behaves exactly as it always has, its setup page shows no People or Chained pr","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"The building blocks","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#the-building-blocks","x":"Word What it is Proxy `maple-proxy`: the program that holds sign-ins and keys and talks to the models. Every Maple install has one, usually listening only on `127.0.0.1` Hub A proxy that other machines or other proxies use. Nothing extra is installed: a hub is a proxy that listens on a network address and asks every ca","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"A. One shared proxy for a small team","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#a-one-shared-proxy-for-a-small-team","x":"One machine runs the proxy with the team's accounts; everyone else connects to it with their own token. One shared proxy: a token per person, the accounts on one machine. The hub can run as a program on a machine, as below, in a container ( Docker ), or on a cluster, with several replicas when a restart must not interr","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"B. Who uses which account","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#b-who-uses-which-account","x":"A hub can hold several accounts for the same models, and give them out: one for everyone, one per team, or one for a single person. YAML The same with the commands: Shell The order the accounts are tried in. An account is only used for models of its own kind. An account that does not serve the model, or that the person","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"C. Chained hubs","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#c-chained-hubs","x":"When there is more than one proxy, chain them: each proxy uses the one above it as a provider of `type: maple`. A laptop goes to a team hub, and the team hub goes to the top hub that holds the company's accounts. Requests go up; people and teams come down. On the setup page (the quickest way): on the hub, Hubs & chaini","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"People defined once, at the top","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#people-defined-once-at-the-top","x":"Define the people and teams on the top hub. Every proxy below fetches them at start and again as soon as they change (it waits on the hub for news), with their budgets, model lists and accounts, and a check value for each of their tokens, never the tokens. So a person's token made at the top also works at the team hub ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"Usage rolls up","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#usage-rolls-up","x":"Each proxy sends its usage records (counts and labels only, never prompts, answers or files) up to its parent, with the records its own children sent it, so the top hub can report on the whole chain. Each request is counted once: every hop shares one request id. If the parent is down, the child keeps its records and se","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"D. Fallback across the chain","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#d-fallback-across-the-chain","x":"The pattern most people want from a chain is a fallback that crosses machines, either way round. Both are ordinary fallbacks on the proxy where you set them, so they work on any proxy in the chain. Your account first, or the hub first.","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"Your own account first, the team hub when you run out","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#your-own-account-first-the-team-hub-when-you-run-out","x":"On your laptop: your own Copilot account serves your requests, and when it is out of credits or rate limited they go to the team hub, for the same model. YAML While your account is marked as out of quota, later requests go straight to the hub (the route reason is `exhausted-order`), and back to your account once its wa","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"The hub first, your local model when the hub says no","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#the-hub-first-your-local-model-when-the-hub-says-no","x":"Everything goes to the hub, and a model on your own machine answers when the hub refuses (a budget there is spent, its accounts are out of quota) or cannot be reached: YAML With the team hub stopped, the answer came from the local model: and with the top hub's account out of quota, two hubs up, the same: Both can be se","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"E. See who used what","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#e-see-who-used-what","x":"Every proxy in the chain records, on each request, who asked (`person`, `team`), which proxy they asked (`origin_proxy`), the path (`via`, `hops`), the account that served it and why (`provider`, `account_source`, `route_reason`). Filter by any of them on the setup page, with `maple-proxy usage query`, or in DuckDB. On","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"F. Day to day: revoking, outages and security","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#f-day-to-day-revoking-outages-and-security","x":"Revoking someone. Revoke their token on the proxy that made it (the setup page's token list). It stops working there at once, and on every proxy below within seconds while the hubs answer, one hop after another (a proxy below that cannot reach its hub keeps the last copy for up to `chain.directory_grace`, 15 minutes by","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"What is not covered, and what is not verified","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#what-is-not-covered-and-what-is-not-verified","x":"Downgrading. A proxy older than 2.0 ignores `people:`, `teams:` and `default_accounts:` and keeps working, but no budget, model list or account rule applies and requests are no longer attributed (see Going back to an older version ). A chain was not tested with an older proxy in it. Older usage. Requests from before 2.","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"Related","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#related","x":"Teams People, teams, access tokens, budgets and rate limits on one proxy. Access control Organizations, roles, rules per access token, end users and lifetime budgets. Chain proxies into hubs One proxy using another: a laptop behind a team hub, a team hub behind a company one. The proxy and its setup page The proxy's po","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Teams","u":"teams.html","x":"Several people can share one proxy, each with their own access token. The proxy then knows who sent every request, can give each person or team their own provider accounts, model list and budget, and lets each person see their own usage. If you use Maple on your own machine, you need none of this. Nothing on this page ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"In plain words","u":"teams.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Several people share one proxy, each with a token of their own, so the proxy knows who sent each request. Why you want it. Each person or team can get their own accounts, models and budget, and you see who spent what. The clicks. On the setup page, Set up more , Share this proxy with a team : the People & t","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"How each person signs in","u":"teams.html#how-each-person-signs-in","x":"A person has no password of their own on the proxy. What identifies a person is an access token tied to them. Every request that carries one of their tokens is theirs: their budget, their model list, their accounts, their line on the People card. A person with no token cannot connect from another machine at all. (With ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Make a token and tie it to the person","u":"teams.html#make-a-token-and-tie-it-to-the-person","x":"On the proxy's machine: Open the setup page (`http://127.0.0.1:11539`), then People & teams , and find the person on the People card. Open Access and tokens under their name. Give the token a name you will recognise later, such as `carol-laptop`, and choose when it expires. Select Make a token . It has the proxy scope ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Hand the token over safely","u":"teams.html#hand-the-token-over-safely","x":"Send it over a private channel: in person, a password manager's shared item, or a direct message that is not kept in a shared channel. Never in a group chat, an issue, an email list or a commit. The person keeps it in their secret store or an environment variable, never in a file that goes into a repository. If it may ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"How each tool uses the token","u":"teams.html#how-each-tool-uses-the-token","x":"Each person's tools send the token as their API key. How they connect , under the person's Access and tokens , shows these lines with this proxy's addresses. Replace `proxy.example` with the address of the proxy's machine, and use the ports it listens on (`11535` for OpenAI-compatible tools and `11537` for Anthropic-co","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"`local` and `unattributed`","u":"teams.html#local-and-unattributed","x":"`local` is a request from the proxy's own machine that carries no token. It needs no token, so the People card shows no note for it. Add a person with the id `local` to give it a team, a budget or rules. `unattributed` is a request no person was recorded for: one made with a token tied to nobody, or sent before people ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Several tokens per person","u":"teams.html#several-tokens-per-person","x":"A person can have as many tokens as they like, one per device (`carol-laptop`, `carol-desktop`). Tie each one to them; all their requests count as theirs, and revoking one leaves the others working. The People card says what each person signs in with (\"signs in with: carol-laptop, carol-desktop\"), and `maple-proxy peop","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Rotate or revoke a token","u":"teams.html#rotate-or-revoke-a-token","x":"Revoke: under the person's Access and tokens , select Revoke next to the token (or Revoke all ), or run `maple-proxy token revoke <token-id>`. It stops working at once and is untied from the person. Rotate: on Security & access , select Rotate next to the token (or run `maple-proxy token rotate <token-id> --grace 24h`)","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"When a request is refused","u":"teams.html#when-a-request-is-refused","x":"Answer What it means What to do `401`, \"a valid access token with the proxy scope is required\" No token, a mistyped, revoked or expired one, or one without the proxy scope Check the key the tool sends; make a new token with the proxy scope if needed `403` from the proxy's address The address the tool uses is not in `se","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Set up a team on the People & teams tab","u":"teams.html#set-up-a-team-on-the-people--teams-tab","x":"The setup page's People & teams tab does all of this step by step, on the proxy's own machine. On a proxy used by one person it is not in the menu: open Set up more and press Set up on Share this proxy with a team (the tab then stays in the menu on that browser). Used alone, it says there is nothing to do, with one but","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Set up a team in four steps","u":"teams.html#set-up-a-team-in-four-steps","x":"Make a token for each person. On the proxy's setup page, open Security & access → Remote access . It asks what the token is for: choose A person's tools to send model requests , tick proxy (the only scope it offers), choose Token , and give each person their token. (The other choice, A phone or browser to follow and dr","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Who a request belongs to","u":"teams.html#who-a-request-belongs-to","x":"A request with a token belongs to the person that token is tied to. A proxy token tied to nobody still works; its requests are counted as unattributed and no person's rules apply to them. `maple-proxy people list` lists such tokens, so you can tie or revoke them. On a proxy that listens only on this machine, a token is","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Budgets","u":"teams.html#budgets","x":"A budget is in AI units, as the providers reported them: the same units as the provider budgets (`budgets:`). Set a daily limit, a monthly limit or both, per person and per team. Days and months are counted in UTC, so a daily budget resets at 00:00 UTC and a monthly one on the 1st. AI units are what GitHub Copilot repo","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Rate limits","u":"teams.html#rate-limits","x":"A person, and a team as a whole, can be held to a pace: requests a minute, input tokens a minute, and requests at once. Set them on the People & teams tab (a person's or a team's Edit , Rate limits ), or in the file: YAML A person's Edit : the rate limits beside the budget. A request over a limit is answered `429` with","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Models","u":"teams.html#models","x":"`models.allow` lists the models a person or a team may use: names as asked for (an alias such as `coder-default`, or `auto`), model ids, or `account/model`, with `*` as a wildcard (`copilot/*`; a name ending in `/*` also covers everything a hub passes on under it, such as `hub/copilot-work/gpt-5`). An empty list allows","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Accounts","u":"teams.html#accounts","x":"Each account (a provider entry, such as one GitHub Copilot sign-in) has an id and may have a nickname; either works in these lists. `accounts` on a person, then on their team, then `default_accounts` for everyone, are tried first, in that order, for a model of their kind. A person on two accounts falls back from the fi","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"The self view","u":"teams.html#the-self-view","x":"With `people_self_view: \"on\"` (off by default; the switch is on the People card, or `maple-proxy people self-view on`), each person can ask the proxy for their own figures with their own token: Shell It answers with the person, the label of the token that asked, their own budget used and left, and their requests, token","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Share one proxy with your team","u":"teams.html#share-one-proxy-with-your-team","x":"","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"On the proxy's machine","u":"teams.html#on-the-proxys-machine","x":"Set up the people as above. Let the proxy listen where your teammates can reach it, for example `server.openai_compat: 0.0.0.0:11535` and `server.anthropic_compat: 0.0.0.0:11537`. Off this machine every request needs a token with the proxy scope; the proxy will not start that way until one exists. The setup page stays ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"On each teammate's machine","u":"teams.html#on-each-teammates-machine","x":"One token per person Each person uses their own token, with the `proxy` scope. Do not share tokens: every request made with one is counted as its person's. Maple Harness. With Maple installed on your machine, join your own proxy to this one and keep using Maple Harness as before: paste the token on your proxy's setup p","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Teams across chained hubs","u":"teams.html#teams-across-chained-hubs","x":"When a proxy uses another Maple proxy as its parent, it inherits the parent's people, teams, their accounts, budgets and model rules, and the parent's tokens (as hashes, never the tokens), and passes them on to its own children. What the proxy uses is the inherited people and teams plus its own. Inherited entries are r","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Limits","u":"teams.html#limits","x":"Budgets and spend are in AI units as the providers reported them; `cost_estimated` is an estimate from list prices. Neither is a bill. Only requests with a token can be told apart. On a proxy that listens only on this machine, anyone on the machine can leave the token out and be counted as `local`; listen off this mach","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Going back to an older version","u":"teams.html#going-back-to-an-older-version","x":"A proxy older than 2.0 ignores `people:`, `teams:`, `default_accounts:` and `people_self_view:` and keeps working: every token still works, but no person's budget, model list or accounts apply, and requests are no longer attributed. The keys stay in `config.yaml` and apply again after an upgrade. The usage log stays re","m":"teams"},{"p":"Settings by goal","h":"Settings by goal","u":"three-es.html","x":"Maple Harness aims at three things at once: to be efficient (fewer tokens for the same work), efficacious (the work stays as good, and defaults are safe) and effective (whole tasks succeed and waste stops early). This page maps a goal to the settings and commands that serve it. It makes no promise about your bill: resu","m":"single"},{"p":"Settings by goal","h":"In plain words","u":"three-es.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. A map from what you want (spend less, keep quality, stop runaways, get reliable results) to the setting that serves it. What to do. Find your goal below, change the setting where it says (most are on the setup page's Models & routing tab, or on Advanced , which Find a setting reaches by name), and measure t","m":"single"},{"p":"Settings by goal","h":"I want to spend less on long runs","u":"three-es.html#i-want-to-spend-less-on-long-runs","x":"Do this Where Trade-off Keep one model for a long task instead of switching `/model <name>`, or `/model auto` (it keeps your model when it fits) A fixed model may be more than a small job needs See where the money goes `/cost`, the setup page's Cache card, `maple-proxy cache` None Keep the provider's cache working `pro","m":"single"},{"p":"Settings by goal","h":"I want the work to stay as good","u":"three-es.html#i-want-the-work-to-stay-as-good","x":"Do this Where Trade-off Let the compiler check the model's edits `lsp` (on by default), `/lsp` to see what is running The first edit starts the server in the background Keep the safe defaults `permission.mode: ask`, `sandbox.mode`, `file_policy.*` More questions, fewer surprises Prefer the tools that show a diff `bash_","m":"single"},{"p":"Settings by goal","h":"I want to stop runaways","u":"three-es.html#i-want-to-stop-runaways","x":"Do this Where Trade-off End a turn that keeps failing the same way, with a summary `loop_guard.mode: stop`, `loop_guard.stop_after` See above Cut a reply that repeats itself `degenerate_guard` (the repeat guard) on the proxy's Guards card A repetitive answer could be cut short Refuse or trim a request bigger than the m","m":"single"},{"p":"Settings by goal","h":"I want results I can rely on","u":"three-es.html#i-want-results-i-can-rely-on","x":"Do this Where Trade-off Get told when a session needs you `notifications.*`, `maple-harness notify test` None Resume where you left off `--continue`, `--resume`, `/sessions` Old sessions take disk; `cleanup` removes them Keep a checklist for a big request `ledger: auto`, `/ledger` One extra model call Make the model ca","m":"single"},{"p":"Settings by goal","h":"See also","u":"three-es.html#see-also","x":"Proxy settings Every card on the setup page and every key in `config.yaml`. Commands and keys Everything you can type in a session, every key, and the shell commands. Harness settings Every `harness.*` setting, with its default and its trade-off. Measure with bench Repeated, paired runs on your own tasks, to see what a","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with tmux","h":"Use it with tmux","u":"tmux.html","x":"Maple Harness can open panes and windows beside itself in tmux, the way it does in herdr : to run a server or a long build next to the conversation, to start another agent, or to open a file, a diff or a worktree. WezTerm and kitty work the same way. The short version","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with tmux","h":"In plain words","u":"tmux.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. When you run Maple inside tmux, the agent can split the window or open a new one, run a command there and read what it printed, and start another agent (a second Maple session, Claude Code, Codex) in a window of its own. You can do the same yourself with `/tab`, `/pane`, `/panes` and `/worktree`. What it as","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with tmux","h":"What you can do","u":"tmux.html#what-you-can-do","x":"Your commands. Typed by you, they run without asking. Windows are tmux's tabs. Command What it does `/tab new [name]` a new window beside this session's, and moves you to it `/pane split [right|down] [command]` splits this session's pane, with the command running in the new one `/pane run <command>` the command in the ","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with tmux","h":"Worktrees","u":"tmux.html#worktrees","x":"`/worktree feat/login` (or the agent's `worktree_open`, which asks every time) runs `git worktree add` with the repository's hooks turned off, from the main checkout only (where `.git` is a folder of its own). The worktree goes beside the repository, as `<repository>-<branch>` (`../app-feat-login` for `app` on `feat/lo","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with tmux","h":"Choosing the workspace","u":"tmux.html#choosing-the-workspace","x":"`harness.workspace` decides where panes open: Value What it does `auto` (default) herdr when the session runs in one of its panes, else tmux, WezTerm or kitty, whichever it runs in, else a herdr running on this machine (new tabs only) `herdr`, `tmux`, `wezterm`, `kitty` only that one `off` no panes: the tools and comma","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with tmux","h":"Technical notes","u":"tmux.html#technical-notes","x":"tmux. The harness talks to the tmux server it runs in, named from `TMUX`, and to the pane in `TMUX_PANE`; it never reaches another tmux server. A command is typed as literal text and then Enter, so nothing in it is read as a key name. Folder and window names with `#` are passed as plain text. WezTerm. Through `wezterm ","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with tmux","h":"Tested versions","u":"tmux.html#tested-versions","x":"tmux 3.x (3.8, 2026-11-19: splits, windows, typing a command and reading its output, listing and closing panes, and names with `#`, on a private tmux server). WezTerm and kitty are written to their documented command lines and checked against recorded command lines only: they have not been tried in a running WezTerm or","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with tmux","h":"If it does not work","u":"tmux.html#if-it-does-not-work","x":"Check that the harness runs inside tmux: `echo $TMUX` in the same shell prints the server's socket. For kitty, check that `KITTY_LISTEN_ON` is set (remote control on); for WezTerm, that `WEZTERM_PANE` is. `harness.workspace: off` turns it all off; `harness.workspace: tmux` uses tmux even inside herdr.","m":"single"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html","x":"Start with the checker. It looks at your configuration, the proxy, sign-ins, ports, remote control and the sandbox, and says what to do about each problem it finds: Shell Two commands inside a session answer most of the rest: `maple-proxy serve --logs=true` shows every request and the reason behind every failed one. Th","m":"both"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"In plain words","u":"troubleshooting.html#in-plain-words","x":"Start with the checker. `maple-harness doctor` looks at everything and says what to fix, in plain words. Then look here. Find the symptom below; each has the fix. On the setup page, What's configured on the Overview marks anything that needs attention, with a link to where it is set.","m":"both"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"The proxy","u":"troubleshooting.html#the-proxy","x":"The agent says the proxy is unreachable. Nothing answers at the configured address. `/proxy` shows where the agent is looking, and `/proxy start` starts one. This usually means `server.openai_compat` in `config.yaml` differs from where a proxy was started. The setup page at `127.0.0.1:11539` does not load. Check the pr","m":"both"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Signing in","u":"troubleshooting.html#signing-in","x":"Copilot sign-in never finishes. Open the link it printed and enter the code within ten minutes, then run `maple-harness auth login github-copilot` again. A `403` means the account has no Copilot seat, or the grant was revoked; signing in again is the first thing to try. \"This account has no chat models enabled for Copi","m":"both"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Models","u":"troubleshooting.html#models","x":"The context window shows 0, or history is trimmed too early. A window of 0 means unknown. Leave `context_window` out of `config.yaml` so the proxy asks the server, and check `/context`. If the server reports nothing, set it by hand. A local model repeats itself or stops without finishing. Small models do this. Use them","m":"both"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Cost","u":"troubleshooting.html#cost","x":"","m":"both"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"A run cost more than I expected","u":"troubleshooting.html#a-run-cost-more-than-i-expected","x":"Type `/cost`. It shows what the session has spent, the last few requests with how much of each was served from the provider's cache, and where the input goes. A request marked \"low cache hit\" or \"model changed\" is where the cache was lost: a new model starts with a cold cache. To keep it, use `/model fixed` (or pick a ","m":"both"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"The agent keeps retrying the same failing command","u":"troubleshooting.html#the-agent-keeps-retrying-the-same-failing-command","x":"When the same problem fails again and again, Maple Harness steps in. After three similar failures it adds a note to the result telling the model to stop trying variations, say what it is assuming, check that with a cheap read-only command, and choose a different approach. After six, it ends the turn with a summary of w","m":"both"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Installing and updating","u":"troubleshooting.html#installing-and-updating","x":"`checksum mismatch`. The installer stops and leaves the existing files untouched. A truncated download is the usual cause; run it again. `permission denied` writing to `/usr/local/bin`. Use `--install-location ~/.local/bin`, or run the installer with `sudo`. macOS says a downloaded binary cannot be opened. Binaries dow","m":"both"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Remote control","u":"troubleshooting.html#remote-control","x":"The phone cannot reach the address. Check both devices are on the same Tailscale network (or the same local network for a LAN address) and that a firewall is not blocking port 11541. Pairing shows a link that does not load. The gateway may have stopped. `/remote pair` starts it again; or run `maple-harness gateway star","m":"both"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"The shell sandbox","u":"troubleshooting.html#the-shell-sandbox","x":"`/sandbox` says bubblewrap is missing, or installed but not working. It is optional; the install command for your system is in Install . \"Installed but does not work\" usually means unprivileged user namespaces are blocked, for example in a container. Until it works, commands run uncontained. A command fails inside a sh","m":"both"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Disk space","u":"troubleshooting.html#disk-space","x":"The configuration directory (`~/.config/maple-harness`) holds saved sessions, memory notes and logs. To see how much it uses and clear old sessions, run `maple-harness cleanup --help`.","m":"both"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"More help on other pages","u":"troubleshooting.html#more-help-on-other-pages","x":"Some pages have their own troubleshooting section for the feature they cover: Language servers: Troubleshooting : a server is not found, or its errors do not show up. Search by meaning: When something goes wrong . The shell sandbox: When a command is blocked . Control it from your phone: If it does not work . Keep it r","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 2.0","h":"Upgrading to 2.0","u":"upgrading-to-2.0.html","x":"If you use Maple Harness on your own machine, upgrading to 2.0 asks nothing of you: update, restart the proxy, and carry on. The team, hub and container features are new, but they stay out of the way until you set them up. This page says what keeps working, what is new and on, what is new and off, what changed, that go","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 2.0","h":"In plain words","u":"upgrading-to-2.0.html#in-plain-words","x":"On one computer, nothing to do. Update, restart the proxy, carry on. Your settings, sign-ins, tokens, sessions and usage history keep working. New things stay out of the way. Teams, hubs and containers only appear once you set them up. The setup page looks different. Its tabs now sit in two groups, Watch and Configure ","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 2.0","h":"Nothing to do","u":"upgrading-to-2.0.html#nothing-to-do","x":"Everything a 1.x release left on your disk keeps working on 2.0: your `config.yaml` and settings, sign-ins and provider keys; access tokens, and the phones and computers you paired; saved sessions, which resume where you left them; the usage log, the events log, your exports, and the DuckDB queries you already wrote. W","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 2.0","h":"New, and on by default","u":"upgrading-to-2.0.html#new-and-on-by-default","x":"These need no setup and change nothing about how your requests are routed. The setup page in tabs. Overview, Usage, Costs & alerts, Cache, Health, Auto, Data and Settings in 2.0 (2.2 groups them into Watch and Configure, and Settings is split up: see The setup page ), with charts of your usage and a live view of reques","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 2.0","h":"New, and off until you turn it on","u":"upgrading-to-2.0.html#new-and-off-until-you-turn-it-on","x":"None of these appear in your setup, ask you anything, or show up on the setup page until you configure them. People and teams on one proxy, each with their own budgets, models and accounts. See Teams . The self view , where a person's token can read their own budget and spend (`people_self_view`). See The self view . H","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 2.0","h":"What changed","u":"upgrading-to-2.0.html#what-changed","x":"The setup page's sections moved into tabs. Nothing was removed; see the table in The setup page for where each card went. `maple-proxy usage status` explains how long the usage log is kept. It used to say \"everything (usage_retention_days: 0)\" when the setting was not set. It now shows the retention the daily cleanup r","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 2.0","h":"Upgrade","u":"upgrading-to-2.0.html#upgrade","x":"On your own machine: Shell Or run the installer one-liner again. Then restart the proxy so the new version runs (`/proxy restart` in the harness does it); if it runs as a background service, see Update . Check the versions: Shell In a container: no image is published, so build a new one from the 2.0 release with the sa","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 2.0","h":"Going back to 1.x","u":"upgrading-to-2.0.html#going-back-to-1x","x":"Releases before 2.0.0 are no longer published, so the installers cannot install 1.18, 1.17 or any other 1.x release. To go back to an earlier version, install an earlier 2.x release, for example `MAPLEHARNESS_VERSION=v2.6.2` (the install page shows how).","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 2.0","h":"Known limitations in 2.0","u":"upgrading-to-2.0.html#known-limitations-in-20","x":"The setup page's live view is limited to eight open views per proxy; past that, a tab refreshes every few seconds instead. With several replicas, \"in flight\" counts the replica serving the page, and the others' requests reach the live view within about ten seconds. The setup page's charts read the usage log through the","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 2.0","h":"Related","u":"upgrading-to-2.0.html#related","x":"Upgrading to 3.0 What keeps working, what behaves differently, and how to go back. Install One line on macOS, Linux or Windows, and how to update. Keep it running in the background The proxy and the gateway as services that start with your computer. Share Maple with a team Hubs, people and fallbacks, from one shared pr","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 3.0","h":"Upgrading to 3.0","u":"upgrading-to-3.0.html","x":"If you use Maple Harness on your own machine, upgrading to 3.0 asks nothing of you: update, restart the proxy, and carry on. 3.0 is a large release, so its number changes, but what worked on 2.x keeps working. The new features stay out of the way until you set them up. This page says what keeps working, what is new, wh","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 3.0","h":"In plain words","u":"upgrading-to-3.0.html#in-plain-words","x":"On one computer, nothing to do. Update, restart the proxy, carry on. Your settings, sign-ins, tokens, sessions and usage history keep working. New things stay out of the way. Model groups, rate limits, request tags and the LiteLLM provider only do something once you add them to your settings. Approvals nobody answers a","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 3.0","h":"Nothing to do","u":"upgrading-to-3.0.html#nothing-to-do","x":"Everything a 2.x release left on your disk keeps working on 3.0: your `config.yaml` and settings, sign-ins and provider keys; access tokens, people and teams, and the phones and computers you paired; saved sessions, which resume where you left them; the usage log, the events log, your exports, and the DuckDB queries yo","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 3.0","h":"How to upgrade","u":"upgrading-to-3.0.html#how-to-upgrade","x":"Installed with the one-line installer: run `maple-harness update` (or the installer again), then restart the proxy. See Install . The proxy as a background service: update, then restart the service. See Update . A distribution package (deb, rpm, apk): install the 3.0 package, then restart the proxy. In a container or o","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 3.0","h":"New, and off until you set it up","u":"upgrading-to-3.0.html#new-and-off-until-you-set-it-up","x":"None of these changes how your requests are routed until you add it to your settings. The proxy: routing and limits Model groups. One model name served by several accounts, regions or servers, spread between them, with retries, and a conversation kept on the deployment that answered it. See Model groups . Rate limits f","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 3.0","h":"New, and on by default","u":"upgrading-to-3.0.html#new-and-on-by-default","x":"These need no setup and change nothing for the tools you already use. The proxy The OpenAI Responses API on the proxy's OpenAI-compatible address, so tools that speak it, such as Codex CLI, work through the proxy. See Codex CLI . More of the OpenAI API: rerank, moderations, images, audio, files and batches, for provide","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 3.0","h":"What behaves differently","u":"upgrading-to-3.0.html#what-behaves-differently","x":"Nothing here needs a change from you, but you may notice it. Read your `allow` rules for `write_file` and `edit_file` once Those rules now match the file's path. An `allow` rule such as `write_file(src/*)` that used to ask now allows those writes (details in the first item below). Permission rules match what they name.","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 3.0","h":"The question that may come back","u":"upgrading-to-3.0.html#the-question-that-may-come-back","x":"Maple Harness asks before it runs anything a project brings with it (hooks, MCP servers, project settings), and asks again when those files change. In 3.0 a project's Claude Code settings file counts as one of them when it has hooks, so a project you trusted under 2.x that has such a file is asked about once more. In t","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 3.0","h":"Going back to 2.x","u":"upgrading-to-3.0.html#going-back-to-2x","x":"Install a 2.x release by name (see Install ) and restart the proxy. Before you start a 2.x proxy Take out every provider of type `litellm` from `config.yaml`. A 2.x proxy does not know that type and refuses to start, naming the provider. Everything else 3.0 wrote stays readable by 2.6.3: settings, tokens, people and te","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 3.0","h":"Technical details","u":"upgrading-to-3.0.html#technical-details","x":"Versioning. 3.0 is a major release by size. Nothing that worked on 2.x is removed or changes meaning: the config keys, commands, ports, the proxy's HTTP API and the stored data of 2.x keep working. New settings are optional keys. What was checked. An upgrade check starts the 3.0 programs on files left by each release f","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 3.0","h":"See also","u":"upgrading-to-3.0.html#see-also","x":"What's new in 3.0 The 3.0 tour, area by area. Install One line on macOS, Linux or Windows, and how to update. Keep it running in the background The proxy and the gateway as services that start with your computer. Run the proxy on Kubernetes Kustomize samples and a Helm chart, from one pod to several replicas.","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Your usage data","u":"usage-export.html","x":"Every request through the proxy leaves one line in a usage log on your own disk: which model answered, the tokens, the AI units, how long it took and how it ended. Counts and labels only, never a prompt, an answer or a key, and in plain files you own rather than locked inside Maple. This page is the reference for that ","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"In plain words","u":"usage-export.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. A receipt drawer for your model requests: one line per request, one file per day, in your config folder. What was asked, who answered, how big it was, how long it took, how it ended. Nothing you typed. What you can do with it. Ask it questions (what did last week cost, which tool used the most, how often di","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Where the files are","u":"usage-export.html#where-the-files-are","x":"By default the log is in the `usage` folder of your Maple Harness config folder (`~/.config/maple-harness/usage/` on Linux and macOS), one file per UTC day with a name nothing else uses: `maple_harness_proxy_usage_ledger-2026-09-28.jsonl`, `maple_harness_proxy_usage_ledger-2026-09-29.jsonl`, and so on. The folder is cr","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"How long it is kept","u":"usage-export.html#how-long-it-is-kept","x":"Old days are deleted `server.usage_retention_days` ( Keep on the Usage data card) is how many days of daily files to keep. Older daily files are deleted when the proxy starts and then once a day, the proxy logs one line saying what it removed, and each cleanup that deletes files is recorded in the audit log (how many, ","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Turn it off","u":"usage-export.html#turn-it-off","x":"Press Turn off on the Usage data card (or on the one-time notice the setup page shows after upgrading), or set `usage_ledger: off` in `config.yaml`. For one run of the proxy, `CCH_USAGE_LEDGER=off` in its environment does the same (and `CCH_USAGE_LEDGER=/some/path` sends that run's log elsewhere). Nothing already writt","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"From the setup page","u":"usage-export.html#from-the-setup-page","x":"The proxy's setup page has, on its Data & storage tab, a Usage data card with everything below without a terminal: whether the log is on and where; how much disk the daily files take, how many there are and the days they cover, with an estimate at the retention limit once there is a whole day of data; Location (Move, U","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Query it directly with DuckDB","u":"usage-export.html#query-it-directly-with-duckdb","x":"This is the fastest way to ask a real question of your own data — no export step, no code. DuckDB reads JSON Lines natively. Start by creating a typed view named `usage`: Shell `usage schema --format sql` prints a `CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW usage AS ...` statement over every daily file (it finds them the same way `usage e","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Named views","u":"usage-export.html#named-views","x":"For the questions most people ask, `--views` adds ready-made views to the `usage` view: Shell The output starts with exactly what `usage schema --format sql` prints without `--views`, then makes a schema named `maple` (`CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS maple`) and the views inside it. Nothing outside that schema is created ","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Lots of history? Use the DuckDB file or monthly Parquet","u":"usage-export.html#lots-of-history-use-the-duckdb-file-or-monthly-parquet","x":"A view over the daily files reads one file for every day. That is quick for weeks of data; over months or years, and on a hub that also keeps what the proxies below it send up, it gets slower. Two ways to read far fewer files: Monthly Parquet files. `maple-proxy usage compact` packs each month's closed UTC days into on","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Several pods? Query Postgres","u":"usage-export.html#several-pods-query-postgres","x":"In central mode ( Kubernetes ) every replica writes to one PostgreSQL, so there are no daily files on any one machine. `--source postgres` prints the same named views over the shared tables, through DuckDB's `postgres` extension: Shell The connection string is read from the variable each time a view is queried; it is n","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Cost and savings","u":"usage-export.html#cost-and-savings","x":"Each line carries what the request cost and, as estimates, what the proxy's techniques saved and cost on it: the same figures the setup page's Savings card and `maple-proxy savings` show, per request. The card adds them up; the log keeps them one request at a time, so you can add them up your own way. A sum over a day ","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"The usage record schema","u":"usage-export.html#the-usage-record-schema","x":"Every line of the log, and every row of an export, is one usage record. The schema is versioned; this release writes version 9 , with 99 columns. The promise: a later version may add columns at the end, and never renames, removes, reorders or retypes one. Lines written by earlier releases stay readable and have none of","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"The events log","u":"usage-export.html#the-events-log","x":"Next to the daily usage files the proxy keeps an events log: things that happen that are not requests, one JSON object per line in `events-YYYY-MM-DD.jsonl` (one file per UTC day, in the same folder). It is separate so that counting the usage files' lines still counts requests, and so every existing query and export is","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Export to CSV or Parquet","u":"usage-export.html#export-to-csv-or-parquet","x":"For handing the data to a tool that cannot read JSON Lines directly: Shell `--since`/`--until` are UTC-day boundaries (`YYYY-MM-DD`); `--until` is exclusive. Without `--path`, it finds the log the same way the running proxy does (`usage_ledger` from your `config.yaml`, the default place when that is unset, and `CCH_USA","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Filter your data","u":"usage-export.html#filter-your-data","x":"`maple-proxy usage query` answers questions about the usage log without DuckDB or an export: totals, or a table grouped by what you choose, for any mix of filters. Shell The filters: Filter Flags Matches Time `--since`, `--until` A UTC day (`YYYY-MM-DD`) or a time (RFC 3339); `--until` is exclusive. Recent `--last` Who","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Requests with no person","u":"usage-export.html#requests-with-no-person","x":"Two names stand for a request without a person of its own: `unattributed` (stored `person`: empty, NULL): no person was recorded. The request came before people were set up (or before schema version 4), or with an access token tied to no person. `local` (stored `person`: `local`): a request from the proxy's own machine","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Keep it tidy: prune","u":"usage-export.html#keep-it-tidy-prune","x":"The proxy already deletes daily files older than `server.usage_retention_days` (see How long it is kept ). To remove more now, by hand: Shell Only rotated, dated files are ever candidates for deletion. A plain, undated file left over from before rotation existed is never touched by `prune` — if you want that gone, remo","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Wire it into Grafana","u":"usage-export.html#wire-it-into-grafana","x":"Point an existing Grafana plugin at your files rather than a bespoke Maple Harness dashboard, so it stays current with however you actually run Grafana: Infinity (maintained by Grafana Labs, in the official plugin catalog) reads CSV and JSON directly, by path or URL, and is the simpler route: Shell Add it as a data sou","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Every usage command","u":"usage-export.html#every-usage-command","x":"All of them read files only, so the proxy does not need to be running, and each takes `--path` for a log somewhere other than where `config.yaml` says. `maple-proxy usage <command> --help` lists every flag. Command What it does More `usage status` Where the log is, whether it is on, how long it is kept, and the disk th","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Troubleshooting","u":"usage-export.html#troubleshooting","x":"What you see Why What to do `Referenced column \"ai_units\" not found` (or another column) in DuckDB `read_json_auto` guesses columns from the data, and a field the log leaves out when empty is not a column on a day that never had it Use the typed view: `maple-proxy usage schema --format sql` ( why ) `Too many open files","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"See also","u":"usage-export.html#see-also","x":"See and filter your usage The guide, with examples of every question people ask first. Query your usage Tested recipes for `usage query`, DuckDB, the lake and PostgreSQL. Your data The three places usage can live, and which one to use. DuckLake Years of history, time travel and a shared lake for a team. Chain proxies i","m":"both"},{"p":"Web pages, downloads and finding files","h":"Web pages, downloads and finding files","u":"web-and-files.html","x":"Four tools that save the agent a lot of guessing, eh. `web_fetch` reads a page on the web, `download` saves a file from one, `glob` finds files by name, and `read_file` now shows the agent a picture or a PDF instead of a screenful of bytes. The two that touch the network ask you first, site by site, unless you have sai","m":"single"},{"p":"Web pages, downloads and finding files","h":"In plain words","u":"web-and-files.html#in-plain-words","x":"Reading a page. Ask for \"the changelog of that library\" or \"what the docs say about `asyncio.timeout`\" and the agent can read the page itself with `web_fetch`. A web page comes back as plain text with its headings, lists, links, code and tables, in pages of a few hundred lines, so a long page is read a bit at a time. Y","m":"single"},{"p":"Web pages, downloads and finding files","h":"Reading a page: web_fetch","u":"web-and-files.html#reading-a-page-web_fetch","x":"Argument What it does `url` The page, `http` or `https` only. A URL with a user name or password in it is refused `prompt` What the agent is looking for; shown with the result, to keep it on track `offset`, `limit` Which lines of the page's text: from line `offset`, at most `limit` lines (400 by default) What comes bac","m":"single"},{"p":"Web pages, downloads and finding files","h":"Saving a file: download","u":"web-and-files.html#saving-a-file-download","x":"Argument What it does `url` The file, `http` or `https`, with the same network rules as `web_fetch` `path` Where to save it. Missing folders are made It writes only where `write_file` may write: your project and the temporary folder by default, never your SSH keys, cloud credentials, Maple's own configuration or git's ","m":"single"},{"p":"Web pages, downloads and finding files","h":"Finding files: glob","u":"web-and-files.html#finding-files-glob","x":"Argument What it does `pattern` `*` any characters but `/`, `**` any folders (none too), `?` one character, `[abc]` a class, `{a,b}` either. A pattern without a `/` matches file names in every folder: `*.go` finds Go files anywhere `path` The folder to search (the current one by default) Newest first: the files someone","m":"single"},{"p":"Web pages, downloads and finding files","h":"Pictures and PDFs: read_file","u":"web-and-files.html#pictures-and-pdfs-read_file","x":"`read_file` on a picture (`.png`, `.jpg`, `.jpeg`, `.gif`, `.webp`) or a PDF shows the agent the file itself instead of its bytes. The result says what it is (`PNG image, 1200×800, 245 KB`), and the file follows the tool results in the conversation, for the agent to look at. Limits. A picture up to 3 MB (the same limit","m":"single"},{"p":"Web pages, downloads and finding files","h":"Rules by site","u":"web-and-files.html#rules-by-site","x":"`web_fetch` and `download` rules name a site, not a URL: YAML A rule names the site alone, without `https://`, a port or a path: a rule with one of those is refused when the settings load (it could never match). The site in a rule and in the request are read the same way (case, a trailing dot, another script: `bücher.e","m":"single"},{"p":"Web pages, downloads and finding files","h":"What is never reached","u":"web-and-files.html#what-is-never-reached","x":"Whatever a rule says, whatever the agent was told by a page, and after every redirect, the network tools refuse: Refused Examples This machine `localhost`, `127.0.0.1`, `::1`, `0.0.0.0` Private networks `10.x`, `172.16–31.x`, `192.168.x`, `fc00::/7` Shared and link-local addresses, where cloud metadata services answer ","m":"single"},{"p":"Web pages, downloads and finding files","h":"Settings","u":"web-and-files.html#settings","x":"Key Default What it does `web.allow_domains` none Sites `web_fetch` reads without asking `web.fetch_max_mb` `5` The most `web_fetch` reads of one answer, in MB (1 to 50) `web.download_max_mb` `100` The largest file `download` saves, in MB (1 to 4096) All three are in `/settings`, under Tools , and in `config.yaml` unde","m":"single"},{"p":"Web pages, downloads and finding files","h":"On a phone and in the transcript","u":"web-and-files.html#on-a-phone-and-in-the-transcript","x":"The calls read in everyday words: Fetch with the URL, Download with the URL and where it goes, Find files with the pattern. A run of looking calls folds to one line (\"Read 2 files, fetched 1 page\"). Tested on Linux, with local test servers and a fake name resolver; no real site was fetched for these checks. macOS and W","m":"single"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"What's inside Maple","u":"whats-inside.html","x":"Maple Harness is more than a chat window on a model. This page walks through what it does for you behind the scenes: what each part does, why it helps, and where you can see it or change it. Each one is described in plain words first, then with the settings that control it. How they work inside is not described here. N","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"In plain words","u":"whats-inside.html#in-plain-words","x":"The agent remembers your project. It keeps notes, builds a map of your code, and can look things up by what they mean. It doesn't have to rediscover your project every session. Long sessions stay affordable. Requests are shaped so your provider can reuse what it has already read, old bulky tool output can be shortened,","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"The agent","u":"whats-inside.html#the-agent","x":"","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Project memory and the code map","u":"whats-inside.html#project-memory-and-the-code-map","x":"In plain words. The agent keeps short notes about your project and about you (how you like things done, what was decided), and a map of your project's files and the names defined in them. When you mention a function, it can find it in the map instead of searching the whole folder again. A new session starts already kno","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Search by meaning","u":"whats-inside.html#search-by-meaning","x":"In plain words. Looking something up by its words misses what was said differently: \"the login bug\" is not \"authentication failures after the upgrade\". With search by meaning, the agent's look-ups in the conversation's history, its notes and the code map also find things by what they mean. Words still count: an exact m","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Long conversations","u":"whats-inside.html#long-conversations","x":"In plain words. A long task outgrows what a model can see at once. What leaves the model's view is kept and searchable: Maple keeps every message, puts a record of what happened in its place (requests, files changed, commands and whether they passed, decisions), and the agent can search the old part whenever it needs i","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Stopping when it is stuck","u":"whats-inside.html#stopping-when-it-is-stuck","x":"In plain words. Sometimes an agent keeps trying different fixes for the same failing problem. Maple notices, tells the model to step back, and if it still fails, ends the turn with a summary for you instead of spending more. The technical view. `loop_guard.mode` is `stop` by default (`warn` only notes it; `off` turns i","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Language servers","u":"whats-inside.html#language-servers","x":"In plain words. After each edit, the errors your editor would underline come straight back to the agent, so it fixes its own mistake on the next step instead of finding out when the build fails. The technical view. The agent uses the language servers already on your `PATH` for your project's languages (`harness.lsp: au","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"The sandbox and file rules","u":"whats-inside.html#the-sandbox-and-file-rules","x":"In plain words. The agent asks before it writes a file or runs a command, and `deny` rules always win. Its file tools refuse your keys and shell history. With the sandbox on, the commands it runs can change your project but not the rest of your computer, and cannot see where your logins live. The technical view. `/sand","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Remote control from your phone","u":"whats-inside.html#remote-control-from-your-phone","x":"In plain words. Share a session with your phone. You see what the agent is doing, read the exact change before you approve it, answer its questions, and start new sessions in folders you allowed. Notifications say when it needs you, without commands, paths or error text in them. The technical view. `/remote` in a sessi","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"The proxy","u":"whats-inside.html#the-proxy","x":"","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Auto routing","u":"whats-inside.html#auto-routing","x":"In plain words. Ask for the model `auto` and the proxy picks one for each request: a stronger model for planning and deciding, an everyday one for work already under way, a light one for short side jobs. You choose which model each of those is, or keep the defaults from your provider's own ranking. The technical view. ","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Requests shaped for your provider's cache","u":"whats-inside.html#requests-shaped-for-your-providers-cache","x":"In plain words. A long conversation sends the same beginning on every turn. Providers that cache charge less for the part they have already seen, but only if that beginning stays exactly the same. Maple keeps it the same from turn to turn, and shows you how often the cache was used and why it missed. The technical view","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"The response cache","u":"whats-inside.html#the-response-cache","x":"In plain words. Some tools send exactly the same request again and want the same answer. With the response cache on, the proxy answers such a repeat itself, without asking the provider again. Off by default: a reused answer is the same text as before, not a fresh one. The technical view. `response_cache`, `response_cac","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"The semantic cache","u":"whats-inside.html#the-semantic-cache","x":"In plain words. A question asked again in other words, by the same person, on the same model and in the same conversation, can be answered with the earlier answer, without asking the provider. Off by default: the answer is the earlier one, not a fresh one, and two questions close in wording can need different answers. ","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Fallbacks","u":"whats-inside.html#fallbacks","x":"In plain words. When an account is out of quota, rate limited or down, the request goes to the next account or model you chose, mid-task, with nothing for you to do. Nothing falls back unless you add a rule. The technical view. `model_fallbacks` and `provider_fallbacks`, on Models & routing , Proxy settings , Fallbacks","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Provider health","u":"whats-inside.html#provider-health","x":"In plain words. The proxy watches how each provider and model answers your real requests, and marks one that is failing far more than usual. It sends no requests of its own to check. If you choose, a struggling model is tried after your other fallbacks. The technical view. The Health tab and `maple-proxy health`; `heal","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Budgets and alerts","u":"whats-inside.html#budgets-and-alerts","x":"In plain words. Cap what an account may use a day (requests or AI units), or give an Anthropic account a monthly allowance in dollars. You are warned at 80% and 100%; at the limit, requests move to a fallback or stop, as you choose. Every dollar figure Maple counts itself is labelled estimated. The technical view. `bud","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Teams and hubs","u":"whats-inside.html#teams-and-hubs","x":"In plain words. One proxy can serve several people, each with a token of their own, their own accounts, models and budget. Proxies can be chained: a laptop's proxy uses a team hub, which uses a company hub; people are set up once at the top and everyone's usage arrives there. The technical view. People & teams and Hubs","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Seeing everything","u":"whats-inside.html#seeing-everything","x":"In plain words. Every request leaves one line of counts and labels in a log on your own disk: never a prompt or an answer. The setup page draws it; you can ask it questions in a terminal or in DuckDB, and feed Prometheus, OpenTelemetry or a security tool. The technical view. Usage , Costs & budgets , Cache and Health o","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"One page to set it all up","u":"whats-inside.html#one-page-to-set-it-all-up","x":"In plain words. The proxy's setup page is the control panel for all of the above. What's configured on its first tab says what is on, what is off and what needs a look; Find a setting takes you to any setting by a word; Set up more starts the team features only when you want them. The technical view. See The setup page","m":"both"},{"p":"What's new in 3.0","h":"What's new in 3.0","u":"whats-new-3.0.html","x":"Maple Harness 3.0 is a big one, eh: agents that keep working while you talk, an agent that asks before it guesses, a terminal with a fresh coat of paint, and a proxy that can run a whole organization's model traffic, LiteLLM included. On one computer it asks nothing of you: update, restart the proxy and carry on. Every","m":"both"},{"p":"What's new in 3.0","h":"In plain words","u":"whats-new-3.0.html#in-plain-words","x":"Nothing to do on one computer. Your settings, sign-ins, sessions and usage history keep working. See Upgrading to 3.0 . The agent does more on its own. Helpers keep working after the turn ends and report back by themselves; long test runs tell the agent when they finish; and when a decision is yours, the agent asks you","m":"both"},{"p":"What's new in 3.0","h":"The agent","u":"whats-new-3.0.html#the-agent","x":"Helpers that stay out on the ice while you talk, and a lineup you can watch. When a limit runs out Out of a provider's limit with no fallback left? The session and its agents pause until the reset the proxy says, and carry on, sooner if you add a fallback or switch model. Background agents Agents that keep working afte","m":"both"},{"p":"What's new in 3.0","h":"Steering and the terminal","u":"whats-new-3.0.html#steering-and-the-terminal","x":"Steer a turn while it works A correction typed mid-turn reaches the model at its next step; `Ctrl+G`, `/steer` and `/queue` decide it yourself. How the interface looks Framed dialogs, tinted diffs, a mode chip, `/theme` to try every theme live, three themes from home (`birch`, `northern-lights`, `toque`) and screen rea","m":"both"},{"p":"What's new in 3.0","h":"Agents that call agents","u":"whats-new-3.0.html#agents-that-call-agents","x":"Maple Harness as an A2A agent `maple-harness a2a serve` takes tasks from other agents over A2A 1.0 and 0.3; tool calls still wait for approval. The proxy as an A2A gateway Several agents at one address, with access per person and team, each agent's own credentials, loop limits and a record of every call. Find an agent ","m":"both"},{"p":"What's new in 3.0","h":"The proxy: routing and capacity","u":"whats-new-3.0.html#the-proxy-routing-and-capacity","x":"Model groups One model name served by several accounts, regions or servers, with retries and a conversation kept on the deployment that answered it. Capacity, in three layers The proxy asks your model server how many requests it takes at once, learns it from busy answers otherwise, and always obeys what you set. Intera","m":"both"},{"p":"What's new in 3.0","h":"The proxy: control and insight","u":"whats-new-3.0.html#the-proxy-control-and-insight","x":"Guardrails Block, mask or log secrets, personal data, banned terms and your own patterns, before a request is sent, on the answer and around MCP tool calls. Investigations Scheduled checks over the usage log for recurring errors, loops, cost spikes, cache regressions and providers that fail or slow. Content export and ","m":"both"},{"p":"What's new in 3.0","h":"One proxy for an organization","u":"whats-new-3.0.html#one-proxy-for-an-organization","x":"Everything here is opt-in: a single-user setup is unchanged. Organizations and roles One budget and model list over several teams, and roles for the people who manage the proxy. Rules per access token A budget, a model list and the addresses a token may be used from. Budgets per end user A spending limit for each custo","m":"both"},{"p":"What's new in 3.0","h":"LiteLLM, both ways","u":"whats-new-3.0.html#litellm-both-ways","x":"Maple in front of LiteLLM A `litellm` provider type; Detect models lists what the key may use. LiteLLM in front of Maple Your LiteLLM serves some models from a Maple proxy, a GitHub Copilot seat for example. LiteLLM's management API Scripts written for `/key`, `/user`, `/team` and spend keep working against Maple. Move","m":"both"},{"p":"What's new in 3.0","h":"The MCP hub, ready for teams","u":"whats-new-3.0.html#the-mcp-hub-ready-for-teams","x":"No longer experimental. A 2.x `mcp_hub` section works unchanged; everything new is opt-in. Servers that ask you to sign in One sign-in for everyone, each person their own, or the hub as an app. Acting for each person without a browser Token exchange: each person's calls reach the server as them, and nobody opens a brow","m":"both"},{"p":"What's new in 3.0","h":"The setup page","u":"whats-new-3.0.html#the-setup-page","x":"Every setting on the page Access control, routing rules, concurrency, the semantic cache, cloud accounts, pass-through routes, callbacks, the MCP hub and the A2A gateway have forms; a secret typed there is write-only. The setup page, card by card What each card shows and does, Findings and Guardrails included.","m":"both"},{"p":"What's new in 3.0","h":"Deployment","u":"whats-new-3.0.html#deployment","x":"The published image A container image for every release, for Intel/AMD and ARM, with Compose quick starts. With Helm A Helm chart, beside the Kustomize samples, from one pod to several replicas. Codex CLI A tested Codex CLI setup through the proxy's Responses API. Install With GitHub's `gh` installed, the installers an","m":"both"},{"p":"What's new in 3.0","h":"Not verified yet","u":"whats-new-3.0.html#not-verified-yet","x":"Note Some of 3.0 was checked against stand-ins rather than the real thing: the provider guardrail services, the semantic cache with real traffic, token exchange with Keycloak, Okta or Entra, and the container image and Helm chart in a real cluster. macOS and Windows were not run for most of the agent features, and the ","m":"both"},{"p":"What's new in 3.0","h":"Upgrading","u":"whats-new-3.0.html#upgrading","x":"Upgrading to 3.0 What keeps working, what behaves differently, the question that may come back, and how to go back to 2.x. Install `maple-harness update`, or the installer again, then restart the proxy.","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"Which setup do I need?","u":"which-setup.html","x":"Maple runs the same way on one laptop and in a company: a proxy holds the accounts and talks to the models, and your tools talk to a proxy. The setups below differ only in how many proxies there are and how they are joined. Start with the first one that fits, and move to another later: nothing you set up is lost (see H","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"In plain words","u":"which-setup.html#in-plain-words","x":"Answer three questions: Is it just you? Then use one laptop . Install it, sign in, work. Nothing on this page is needed, and nothing here shows up until you set it up. Does your company or team already run a Maple hub? Then join your laptop's proxy to it : sign in with your company account (about a minute), or paste a ","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"At a glance","u":"which-setup.html#at-a-glance","x":"Setup Pick this if What you run Step by step 1. One laptop You use Maple on your own `maple-harness` (it starts `maple-proxy` for you) Your first session 2. Laptop joined to a hub Your team runs a hub and you want its models, budget and accounts Your own proxy, signed in to the hub Join your laptop to a team hub 3. A t","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"Words used on these pages","u":"which-setup.html#words-used-on-these-pages","x":"Word What it means Proxy `maple-proxy`, the program that holds sign-ins and keys and talks to the models. Every Maple install has one Hub A proxy that other machines or other proxies use. Nothing extra is installed: a hub is a proxy that listens on the network and asks every caller for a token Personal token An access ","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"1. One laptop","u":"which-setup.html#1-one-laptop","x":"One laptop: your tools, your proxy, your accounts. Pick this if you use Maple on your own. This is the setup everyone starts with, and most people stay with. What you run. `maple-harness`. It starts `maple-proxy` on the same machine when nothing is running, and you sign in to your accounts on the proxy's setup page (`h","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"2. Your laptop's proxy joined to a team hub","u":"which-setup.html#2-your-laptops-proxy-joined-to-a-team-hub","x":"Your tools keep using your own proxy; your proxy uses the hub, as you. Pick this if your team or company runs a Maple hub and you want its models, under your own name, budget and model list. What you run. Nothing new. Your own proxy joins the hub, and Maple Harness keeps talking to your own proxy, as on one laptop. Eve","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"3. A team hub for many people","u":"which-setup.html#3-a-team-hub-for-many-people","x":"One hub: a token per person, the accounts on one machine. Pick this if several people should share accounts, and you want to see who used what, give each person or team their own accounts, or cap what each can spend. What you run. One `maple-proxy` on a machine your team can reach (a server, a container , or one replic","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"4. Hubs chained to a company hub","u":"which-setup.html#4-hubs-chained-to-a-company-hub","x":"A company hub, a team hub joined with a chain token, and a laptop signed in to the team hub. Pick this if there is more than one hub: a hub per team or per office, with people defined once and every hub's usage in one place. What you run. A proxy per team, each joined to the hub above it with a chain token made by that","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"5. Several replicas behind a load balancer","u":"which-setup.html#5-several-replicas-behind-a-load-balancer","x":"Several replicas: PostgreSQL for what lasts, Redis for what is shared and short-lived. Pick this if a hub (setup 3 or 4) must keep answering through a restart, an upgrade or a lost machine. One replica is enough for most teams: the proxy is light and restarts in seconds. What you run. Copies of one proxy behind a load ","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"6. CI jobs and containers","u":"which-setup.html#6-ci-jobs-and-containers","x":"Pick this if Maple Harness runs in a CI job, a script or a container, where nobody is at a terminal to answer. In plain words. A run that nobody can answer (`--prompt`, `--rpc`, `serve`, or no terminal) uses the proxy on the same machine only when it can check that the proxy is yours: started as the same user, with the","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"GitHub Actions: start the proxy in the job","u":"which-setup.html#github-actions-start-the-proxy-in-the-job","x":"The harness starts the proxy from the same settings folder, so it is verified. The provider's key comes from the job's secrets through an environment variable; nothing secret is written in the file. YAML The same in any other CI, or a plain shell script: Shell To see the proxy's log, start it yourself first, as the sam","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"GitHub Actions: a proxy in a service container","u":"which-setup.html#github-actions-a-proxy-in-a-service-container","x":"A proxy in a container listens beyond its own loopback, so it asks every caller for an access token (see Run the proxy in Docker ); no image is published, so use your own build of the Docker sample. Keep the token's value in the job's secrets. The container is given the value, and the harness sends it with `mh_` in fro","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"When a run does not start","u":"which-setup.html#when-a-run-does-not-start","x":"A run that nobody can answer, on a proxy the harness cannot verify, ends at once with exit status 1 and these lines on standard error (nothing on standard output, and no `--rpc` events): Exit status 1 is also what other errors use; to tell this one apart in a script, look for the line `This session cannot ask you, so i","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"How to move between setups","u":"which-setup.html#how-to-move-between-setups","x":"From To What to do What is kept One laptop Joined to a hub Sign in (setup 2) Everything: your accounts, settings and usage stay on your proxy Joined to a hub One laptop Disconnect on Hubs & chaining , or `maple-proxy chain disconnect` (the token is revoked at the hub too); for a pasted token, delete the hub's provider ","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"What is not verified","u":"which-setup.html#what-is-not-verified","x":"The setups on this page were run on one Linux machine: several proxies on loopback with stand-in model services, a stand-in identity provider through a TLS front, and the replicas with a local PostgreSQL and a stand-in Redis. Not yet run: real Google, Microsoft Entra ID, Okta or GitHub accounts, chains over HTTPS betwe","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"Related","u":"which-setup.html#related","x":"Share Maple with a team Hubs, people and fallbacks, from one shared proxy to a hub of hubs. Teams People, teams, access tokens, budgets and rate limits on one proxy. Chain proxies into hubs One proxy using another: a laptop behind a team hub, a team hub behind a company one. Single sign-on Team admins sign in to a shar","m":"both"},{"p":"Worktrees","h":"Worktrees","u":"worktrees.html","x":"A git worktree is a second (or third) checkout of the same repository, on another branch, in a folder of its own. You can work on a fix while a long branch stays as it is, or let an agent work on its own branch beside you. Maple Harness lists your worktrees, makes new ones, opens a session in one, and removes them, fro","m":"single"},{"p":"Worktrees","h":"In plain words","u":"worktrees.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. `/worktrees` shows every worktree of the repository: its name, its branch, whether it has uncommitted changes, how far it is ahead of or behind its branch's upstream, who works in it, and when it was last used. From there you open a session in one, copy its folder, remove it, or make a new one. Where they g","m":"single"},{"p":"Worktrees","h":"Choose where worktrees go","u":"worktrees.html#choose-where-worktrees-go","x":"`worktrees.location` A worktree named `fix` of `~/code/shop` goes in Good for `beside` (the default) `~/code/shop-fix` What you had: each worktree a folder beside the repository `inside` `~/code/shop/.worktrees/fix` Everything about the project in one folder. Git is told to leave `.worktrees` out (an entry in `.git/inf","m":"single"},{"p":"Worktrees","h":"A path of your own","u":"worktrees.html#a-path-of-your-own","x":"With `location: custom`, `worktrees.path` is a template: In the template Becomes `{repo}` The repository's folder name (`shop`) `{repo_parent}` The folder that holds the repository (`~/code`) `{name}` The worktree's name (`fix`) `{branch}` Its branch, with a slash made a dash (`feature/login` becomes `feature-login`) `","m":"single"},{"p":"Worktrees","h":"Only you choose","u":"worktrees.html#only-you-choose","x":"`worktrees.location` and `worktrees.path` are read from your own `config.yaml`. A project's own settings file (`.mapleharness/config.yaml`) may suggest `location: inside`, and that applies only when you have not chosen a location; a project can never set a path, or send worktrees anywhere outside the repository.","m":"single"},{"p":"Worktrees","h":"Make a worktree","u":"worktrees.html#make-a-worktree","x":"From the message box: The branch works as in GitHub Desktop: left out, a new branch is named after the worktree; an existing branch is checked out; a branch that exists only on `origin` gets a local branch that tracks it. A branch already checked out in another worktree is refused, with where it is. The repository's gi","m":"single"},{"p":"Worktrees","h":"The worktrees dialog","u":"worktrees.html#the-worktrees-dialog","x":"`/worktrees` opens the list: `/worktrees` in a repository with two worktrees, one with uncommitted changes. A demo setup with a scripted model. Enter on one offers what fits it: Action What happens Open a session there A new Maple session in that worktree, in a new tab (herdr, tmux, WezTerm or kitty). This session stay","m":"single"},{"p":"Worktrees","h":"What is never removed","u":"worktrees.html#what-is-never-removed","x":"The main checkout. A worktree that is not where your setting puts worktrees (made by hand, by another tool, or before you changed the setting): it is listed as not Maple's , and `git worktree remove` is yours to run. A worktree git has locked (`git worktree lock`). A worktree whose links were changed (its `.git` file, ","m":"single"},{"p":"Worktrees","h":"From the shell","u":"worktrees.html#from-the-shell","x":"Shell `maple-harness worktree`: the same list from the shell.","m":"single"},{"p":"Worktrees","h":"From your phone","u":"worktrees.html#from-your-phone","x":"The session sheet (the gear) lists the repository's worktrees: branch, uncommitted changes, who uses each one, and where new ones go. A device that drives sessions has New worktree (a name, and a branch if you want one), Open session here (the new-session sheet, with that folder filled in) and Remove , which takes a se","m":"single"},{"p":"Worktrees","h":"The agent and worktrees","u":"worktrees.html#the-agent-and-worktrees","x":"The agent has a `worktree` tool: it lists the worktrees, makes one, or removes one. Listing never asks. Making or removing one asks you , in every mode, yolo included, unless your permission rules decide: `worktree(create *)` allows it to make worktrees, `worktree(remove *)` to remove them, and a deny rule refuses. Whe","m":"single"},{"p":"Worktrees","h":"Agents in their own copy","u":"worktrees.html#agents-in-their-own-copy","x":"A background agent that changes files works in its own copy of the project, a worktree too ( Agents in the background ). With the default location its copy stays in the project's `.mapleharness/worktrees` folder, as before. With any other location it goes there, named `agent-<id>-…`, and the sandbox and the file tools ","m":"single"},{"p":"Worktrees","h":"Settings","u":"worktrees.html#settings","x":"Key Default What it does `worktrees.location` `beside` Where the worktrees Maple makes go: `beside`, `inside`, `sibling` or `custom`. With any but `beside`, a background agent's copy goes there too `worktrees.path` For `custom` only: the template, such as `{repo_parent}/{repo}-worktrees/{name}` or `~/worktrees/{repo}/{","m":"single"},{"p":"Worktrees","h":"Technical details","u":"worktrees.html#technical-details","x":"Worktrees are listed, made and removed from the repository's main checkout. A session started inside a linked worktree says so and points you there: what git would read in a worktree is the worktree's own to change, and git runs outside the sandbox. Worktrees are made with `git worktree add` from the main checkout, wit","m":"single"},{"p":"Worktrees","h":"Not yet checked","u":"worktrees.html#not-yet-checked","x":"Tested on Linux with real git (2.56) in temporary repositories, the interface in tmux and the phone page in Chromium. macOS, Windows, herdr's own worktree helper and a real phone were not run.","m":"single"},{"p":"Worktrees","h":"See also","u":"worktrees.html#see-also","x":"Agents in the background Panes, tabs and agents and Use it with tmux Control it from your phone Approvals when nobody answers","m":"single"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","u":"your-data.html","x":"Every request that goes through the proxy leaves one usage record: counts and labels, never content. This page is the map: what is recorded, the three places that data can live, which one to use, how it moves between them, and what that costs to run. The details are on their own pages: Query your usage is the cookbook,","m":"both"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"In plain words","u":"your-data.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Where your usage history lives: daily files on this machine (the default), PostgreSQL for a team with several replicas, and an optional lake for years of history. What to do alone on one machine. Nothing: the files are already there. The setup page's Data & storage tab says where, how much and for how long.","m":"both"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"What is recorded, and what never is","u":"your-data.html#what-is-recorded-and-what-never-is","x":"One record per request, with: what was asked for and what answered: the model the client asked for, the provider and model that answered, and whether a fallback stepped in; how big it was: input, output and cached tokens, and the AI units the provider reported; how long it took, and how it ended: fine, failed (with a f","m":"both"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"The three places your data can live","u":"your-data.html#the-three-places-your-data-can-live","x":"","m":"both"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"The daily files: the default","u":"your-data.html#the-daily-files-the-default","x":"The proxy writes one file per UTC day into the `usage` folder of its config folder (`~/.config/maple-harness/usage/` on Linux and macOS), and keeps 90 days unless you change `server.usage_retention_days`. This is how a single user, or a single container, keeps its data, and it is all most people need. `maple-proxy usag","m":"both"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"PostgreSQL: teams and several pods","u":"your-data.html#postgresql-teams-and-several-pods","x":"When several copies of one proxy run behind a load balancer ( central mode ), each copy writes its usage into a shared PostgreSQL instead, so every copy, the setup page and `usage query` see the whole team's traffic. PostgreSQL is the live database: it holds the recent days (`storage.retention_days`, by default the sam","m":"both"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"DuckLake: history and analysis, for both","u":"your-data.html#ducklake-history-and-analysis-for-both","x":"A DuckLake keeps your usage as Parquet data files plus a small catalog that lists them, and it only ever adds: rows the daily files or PostgreSQL delete later stay in the lake. It is where history longer than your retention lives, and the fastest thing to query over months or years. On one machine its catalog is a file","m":"both"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"On the setup page","u":"your-data.html#on-the-setup-page","x":"The Data & storage tab says where this proxy keeps its data, how much there is and how long it is kept, with queries to copy into DuckDB, and holds the Usage data card: the location, Keep , Analytics files , Export and Remove old days . Its charts and the other tabs read the daily files (in central mode, PostgreSQL); t","m":"both"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"Which one should I use?","u":"your-data.html#which-one-should-i-use","x":"You are Live data History beyond retention Query it with One person, one machine The daily files (nothing to do) Raise `usage_retention_days`, or add a local lake `usage query`, the setup page, DuckDB over the files One person who keeps a lot of history The daily files A local lake (`usage_analytics: lake`), or the Duc","m":"both"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"How data moves","u":"your-data.html#how-data-moves","x":"Usage goes up a chain of hubs, and from the live store into the lake. In batches, not per request. A request is written to the daily file or to PostgreSQL as it ends. The lake is filled by a job: hourly for a team (the Kubernetes sample's schedule), and once a day on one machine when Analytics files is `lake` (or whene","m":"both"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"What it costs to run","u":"your-data.html#what-it-costs-to-run","x":"There are no new services to pay for on one machine: the daily files, the monthly Parquet files, the DuckDB file and a local lake are files on your disk, made by the proxy or by your own `duckdb` program. What they take: Disk. The daily files take about 0.25 to 0.65 KB a request (an estimate from sample lines; `usage s","m":"both"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"Your proxy's identity key","u":"your-data.html#your-proxys-identity-key","x":"Since 2.2 your proxy also keeps `local-identity.key` in your settings folder (`~/.config/maple-harness/` on Linux and macOS, `%APPDATA%\\maple-harness\\` on Windows). It is a random key, made by the proxy on its first start, readable and writable only by you (mode `0600`); the proxy replaces it if anyone else could read ","m":"both"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"Where to go next","u":"your-data.html#where-to-go-next","x":"Query your usage : tested recipes for `usage query`, DuckDB over the files, the DuckDB file, the lake and PostgreSQL, and exports to spreadsheets. The Maple data lake : set up a lake on one machine or for a team, maintenance, time travel, backups. See and filter your usage : the filters, the ready-made views, the event","m":"both"}];
